window.LW_SEARCH_INDEX={"lessons/acids-bases.html":"acids and bases — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate acids and bases understand the logarithmic ph scale, compare solutions, and try a simple colour-indicator experiment. acids and bases in everyday life lemon juice, vinegar and some cleaning solutions are associated with acidity or basicity. a useful way of describing an aqueous solution is its ph . at about 25°c, neutral pure water has ph 7. a lower ph means a higher hydrogen-ion activity; a higher ph indicates a lower hydrogen-ion activity. ph is logarithmic : a difference of one unit corresponds to a factor of ten in hydrogen-ion activity. for dilute ideal solutions: ph ≈ −log₁₀[h⁺] the concentration [h⁺] is expressed in mol/l relative to the standard state. this approximation is useful for introductory problems, though precise ph is defined using activity. an acid–base reaction often involves transfer of a proton (h⁺) between chemical species. everyday examples include vinegar, lemon juice and baking soda solutions, but taste is not a safe method of identifying chemicals. acidity and basicity describe chemical behaviour rather than simply whether a liquid feels strong or weak. water can act as an acid or a base depending on its reaction partner. an aqueous solution contains a balance of hydronium and hydroxide ions; changing that balance alters ph. an indicator changes colour because its molecular forms respond differently to acidity—not because it mechanically “measures” the amount of acid present. what a colour indicator tells us some plant pigments change colour depending on solution acidity. red-cabbage indicator is a familiar example. an indicator provides a rough classification ; exact ph requires suitable calibration or a ph meter. for dilute aqueous solutions, ph=−log₁₀(a_h⁺), commonly approximated as −log₁₀[h⁺] when activity effects are negligible. a tenfold change in hydrogen-ion activity changes ph by one unit. a strong acid dissociates extensively in water, whereas a weak acid only partially dissociates; “strong” and “concentrated” are distinct ideas. neutralisation transfers protons and often yields water and a salt, but the ph at the equivalence point is not always 7, especially for weak acid–strong base or weak base–strong acid combinations. for home experiments, indicator solution made from red cabbage can show qualitative changes using dilute kitchen materials. avoid mixing household cleaners or attempting to identify unknown chemicals by smell. explore the ph scale move the indicator on a ph scale selected ph 7 0 7 14 acidic neutral* basic approximate colour illustration (not a test result) approximate spectrum to illustrate the scale; actual indicators have different colour ranges. at ph 7, the solution is approximately neutral at 25°c. ph describes aqueous solutions; different solvents and temperatures require additional care. worked examples problem: solution a has ph 3 and solution b has ph 5. in the dilute approximation, how do their hydrogen-ion concentrations compare? difference in ph = 5 − 3 = 2 . each ph step is a factor of 10 in hydrogen-ion concentration. 10² = 100 . solution a has approximately 100 times the hydrogen-ion concentration of solution b. this compares hydrogen-ion concentration, not directly the overall corrosiveness or safety of either solution. worked example 2 question. a dilute strong-acid solution has [h⁺]=1×10⁻³ mol/l. estimate ph at standard dilute-solution approximation. solution. ph=−log₁₀[h⁺]=−log₁₀(10⁻³)=3. this is approximate because activity effects are neglected. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: a dilute strong-acid solution has [h⁺] = 1 × 10⁻⁴ mol/l. estimate its ph. show worked answer ph = −log₁₀(10⁻⁴) = 4 . problem 2 of 5 estimate ph when [h⁺]=10⁻⁵ mol/l in dilute solution. show answer and method 5. problem 3 of 5 how many times more concentrated is [h⁺] at ph 2 than ph 4? show answer and method 100 times. problem 4 of 5 is ph 3 typically acidic or basic in water? show answer and method acidic. problem 5 of 5 what colour change might red-cabbage indicator show between acidic and basic solutions? show answer and method typically reddish/pink in acid and greenish/yellow toward strongly basic, depending on concentration. try a safe kitchen indicator with supervision where appropriate, soak chopped red cabbage in warm water, let it cool, and strain. place small samples in separate cups and add a little lemon juice to one and baking-soda solution to another. observe how their colours differ. safety: wear eye protection, do not taste test solutions, and never mix cleaning products or use bleach in this experiment. dispose of samples after the activity. references mit ocw 5.111sc — principles of chemical science (acid–base equilibria) original explanations, illustrations, examples, and interactive demonstrations. the mit ocw course is credited as a subject reference; no mit text or figures are reproduced. check the mit ocw terms before reusing materials directly. ← back to courses free, independent learning materials. not affiliated with mit.","lessons/atomic-structure.html":"atomic structure — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate atomic structure meet protons, neutrons and electrons; discover what makes each element—and isotope—different. matter is built from atoms imagine cutting a sample into smaller and smaller pieces. an atom is the smallest unit that retains the chemical identity of an element. its tiny nucleus contains positively charged protons and (usually) neutrons; negatively charged electrons occupy regions around it. the number of protons—the atomic number z —determines the element. changing the neutron count makes a different isotope ; changing the electron count gives an ion . mass number a = protons + neutrons for a neutral atom, electron count equals proton count. the mass number is an integer count of nucleons, not the average atomic mass shown on a periodic table. matter is made of atoms, but an atom is not a miniature solar system with electrons following fixed little planetary paths. it consists of a small dense nucleus containing protons and usually neutrons, surrounded by electrons whose behaviour is described by quantum mechanics. the positive nuclear charge attracts the negatively charged electrons. the number of protons identifies the element: six protons makes carbon, regardless of its neutron count. neutrons can vary within one element, giving isotopes; electrons can be gained or lost to form ions. these distinctions explain why atomic identity, atomic mass and electrical charge are different properties. why the usual atom picture is only a model a common illustration shows electrons as dots on concentric circles. that is useful for learning shell occupancy, but real electrons do not orbit like tiny planets; quantum mechanics describes probability distributions called orbitals. we'll use a simplified shell model here for the first ten elements. it is a teaching picture, not a true scale drawing of electron clouds or the nucleus. an atom's atomic number z counts protons, while the mass number a counts protons plus neutrons. a neutral atom has z electrons; an ion may have more or fewer electrons. carbon-12 contains six protons and six neutrons, while carbon-14 contains six protons and eight neutrons. they have very similar chemical identity but different nuclear properties. the electrons occupy allowed energy states rather than arbitrary energies. when an atom absorbs or emits a photon with the right energy, it can move between states. this helps explain characteristic spectra. diagrams showing circular electron orbits can introduce the nucleus and shells, but should be treated as simplified teaching models, not literal pictures of electron trajectories. explore an atom choose a neutral atom element: hydrogen-1 helium-4 carbon-12 oxygen-16 neon-20 nucleus schematic; not to scale electron • the circles indicate simplified electron shells; the dots show electron count. electron positions are illustrative. carbon-12: 6 protons, 6 neutrons, 6 electrons. worked examples problem: a carbon-12 atom has atomic number 6 and mass number 12. how many protons, neutrons and electrons does it have? atomic number = protons = 6 . neutrons = mass number − protons = 12 − 6 = 6 . a neutral atom has the same number of electrons as protons: 6 electrons . the 12 identifies a particular isotope. carbon-13 would have 7 neutrons. worked example 2 question. an aluminium-27 atom has atomic number 13. count protons, neutrons, electrons in a neutral atom. solution. protons = atomic number = 13. neutrons = mass number − protons = 27−13=14. neutral electrons = 13. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: a sodium-23 ion has charge +1 and atomic number 11. find its protons, neutrons and electrons. show worked answer protons = 11; neutrons = 23 − 11 = 12. a +1 ion has lost one electron, so electrons = 10 . problem 2 of 5 carbon-14 has z=6: neutrons? show answer and method 14−6=8. problem 3 of 5 a neutral atom contains 8 protons. electrons? show answer and method 8. problem 4 of 5 an ion has 12 protons and 10 electrons. charge? show answer and method 2+. problem 5 of 5 do isotopes of one element have different proton counts? show answer and method no; they differ in neutron number. try a periodic-table investigation find hydrogen, carbon and oxygen on a periodic table. compare atomic numbers and ask: what exactly changes when you move from one element to the next? extension: sketch carbon-12 and carbon-13. what stays the same—and why do both remain carbon? references mit ocw 5.111sc — principles of chemical science mit ocw — chemistry lecture notes (atomic structure, lecture 2) original explanations, illustrations, examples, and interactive demonstrations. the mit ocw course is credited as a subject reference; no mit text or figures are reproduced. check the mit ocw terms before reusing materials directly. ← back to courses free, independent learning materials. not affiliated with mit.","lessons/attitude-control.html":"attitude control and reaction wheels — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate attitude control and reaction wheels why can a spinning wheel help turn a spacecraft without pushing against air? keeping a spacecraft pointed attitude is spacecraft orientation. reaction wheels store angular momentum; speeding up a wheel causes the spacecraft body to rotate in the opposite sense, preserving total momentum when external torque is negligible. sensors estimate orientation and controllers issue commands. orbit describes where a satellite travels; attitude describes which direction it points. a telescope can follow the right orbit yet miss its target if its pointing is wrong. sensors estimate orientation, actuators create torques, and control algorithms decide how much correction is needed. a reaction wheel is a rotating mass that stores angular momentum. accelerating the wheel applies a reaction torque to the satellite in the opposite direction, allowing fine pointing without continuously expelling propellant. over time, environmental torques can cause wheels to accumulate momentum and require unloading. torque, momentum and control in a simplified one-axis system, angular momentum is iω. if a reaction wheel changes momentum by δh, the spacecraft body takes an approximately equal and opposite change, unless external torques intervene. δh_body ≈ −δh_wheel for a simplified single-axis system, angular momentum conservation means a change in wheel angular momentum produces an opposing change in spacecraft angular momentum, neglecting external torques. the spacecraft rotational response depends on its moment of inertia. magnetic torquers can interact with earth's magnetic field to unload momentum in some orbits. pointing precision is limited by sensor noise, actuator capacity, disturbances and structural flexing. fast wheel commands can excite flexible appendages. the control system must balance responsiveness with stability, while attitude knowledge and attitude control accuracy remain distinct performance quantities. worked examples question. a reaction wheel changes its angular momentum by +0.12 n·m·s. approximately how much does spacecraft body momentum change? solution, step by step. conservation gives δh_body ≈ −0.12 n·m·s: equal magnitude, opposite sign, neglecting external torque. the final angular speed also depends on body inertia. worked example 2 question. a reaction wheel changes its angular momentum by 0.08 n·m·s in 4 s. find average torque magnitude exchanged with the satellite. solution. average torque magnitude =|δh|/δt=0.08/4=0.02 n·m; spacecraft torque is opposite wheel change. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. if the spacecraft has inertia 6 kg·m², what angular-speed change corresponds to body momentum −0.12 n·m·s? show worked answer δω = δh/i = −0.12/6 = −0.020 rad/s. problem 2 of 5 what is a reaction wheel used for? show answer and method changing spacecraft attitude by exchanging angular momentum. problem 3 of 5 a constant 0.01 n·m torque for 5 s changes angular momentum by how much? show answer and method 0.05 n·m·s. problem 4 of 5 can a reaction wheel continuously remove total system angular momentum without external torque? show answer and method no; desaturation generally requires external torques, e.g. thrusters or magnetorquers. problem 5 of 5 what sensor can measure angular rate? show answer and method gyroscope. try it yourself sit on a swivel chair holding a small spinning wheel under safe supervision, or use a simulation to study opposite rotations. where this model stops reaction wheels can saturate; real spacecraft often need external torque sources to unload stored momentum. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← anatomy of a satellite satellite communications and antennas → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/bernoulli.html":"bernoulli’s principle — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate bernoulli’s principle how are fluid pressure and speed connected along a flow? pressure and speed in a moving fluid a flowing fluid carries mechanical energy in pressure, movement and elevation. under ideal conditions, increasing velocity along a streamline can be accompanied by a decrease in static pressure. the energy balance is more useful than a slogan that fast flow always has low pressure. when fluid accelerates through a narrowing passage, its pressure and kinetic energy can change together. bernoulli’s equation helps track these changes along a streamline when the flow assumptions permit it. the familiar claim that faster flow “always means lower pressure” is too broad: pumps, friction, height changes and different streamline conditions can alter the relationship. an instructive question is where the energy comes from. if a fluid travels at the same height and gains speed in an ideal unpowered passage, pressure energy can be converted into kinetic energy. in a real pipe, some mechanical energy also becomes internal energy through viscous losses. following energy along a streamline for steady, incompressible, inviscid flow along a streamline with no added or extracted shaft work, bernoulli’s equation relates pressure p, density ρ, speed v and elevation z. real flows need loss terms. p + ½ρv² + ρgz = constant for steady, incompressible, inviscid flow along a streamline without shaft work, p+½ρv²+ρgz is constant. each term has units of pressure: static pressure, dynamic pressure and elevation-related pressure. in a horizontal ideal nozzle, the elevation term does not change, so an increase in speed corresponds to a decrease in static pressure. for a real system, introduce a pressure-loss or head-loss term rather than forcing the ideal equation to fit measured data. a pitot tube uses a pressure difference to estimate speed under appropriate assumptions, while a pump adds mechanical energy to the fluid. pressure differences also drive acceleration; interpreting cause and effect requires momentum as well as energy conservation. worked examples question. water flows horizontally from 2 m/s to 4 m/s without losses. what is p₂ − p₁ if ρ = 1000 kg/m³? solution, step by step. elevation cancels. p₂ − p₁ = ½ρ(v₁² − v₂²) = 500(4 − 16) = −6000 pa. pressure drops by 6 kpa in this ideal model. worked example 2 question. water flows horizontally: speed rises from 2 to 6 m/s. neglect losses; ρ=1000 kg/m³. find pressure change. solution. bernoulli: p₂−p₁=½ρ(v₁²−v₂²)=500(4−36)=−16,000 pa. pressure falls by 16 kpa. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. at equal height, water accelerates from 1 to 3 m/s. estimate pressure change, neglecting losses. show worked answer δp = ½ × 1000 × (1² − 3²) = −4000 pa. problem 2 of 5 for ideal level flow, speed increases. static pressure tends to…? show answer and method decrease. problem 3 of 5 write bernoulli in head form between two points. show answer and method p/(ρg) + v²/(2g) + z = constant along a streamline under its assumptions. problem 4 of 5 does bernoulli in its simplest form include pipe friction? show answer and method no; loss terms must be added. problem 5 of 5 can bernoulli be used without checking flow conditions? show answer and method no; assumptions such as steady, negligible viscous loss, incompressible along a streamline need checking. try it yourself draw pressure and velocity labels at two pipe sections. explain which quantities must be measured to check the ideal prediction. where this model stops do not apply bernoulli without checking its assumptions or ignoring viscous losses, pumps and turbulence. references fluid dynamics (2.06) — mit opencourseware, spring 2013. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← conservation of mass and fluid flow flow through pipes and pressure losses → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/buoyancy.html":"buoyancy and floating objects — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate buoyancy and floating objects how can an enormous steel ship float when a small steel nail sinks? why some objects float a small steel nail sinks, yet a large steel ship can float. the difference is not that water “supports heavy things.” it is how much water the whole object can displace before it becomes fully submerged. think of lowering a block into water. water pressure pushes on every wetted surface. because pressure increases with depth, the upward force on the bottom is generally greater than the downward force on the top. the overall vertical difference is the buoyant force . a floating object settles at a depth where this upward buoyant force exactly balances its weight. that might be a shallow immersion for cork or a much deeper immersion for a densely loaded boat. an object can be made of metal and still have a low average density if it encloses enough air. density is useful—but volume matters too for a freely floating object in a liquid, the density of the complete object, including hollow spaces, must be lower than the liquid's density. if it is the same, the object can be neutrally buoyant; if higher, it normally sinks unless another force supports it. changing the shape of a fixed mass does not change its material density, but a hollow shape changes the volume and average density of the entire structure. archimedes’ principle imagine temporarily replacing a submerged object's shape with the exact volume of water occupying that space. before the object arrived, the surrounding water supported this volume of water in equilibrium. the net upward force from the surrounding pressure must therefore equal the weight of that displaced water. this leads to archimedes' principle: f b = ρ fluid g v displaced here ρ is the surrounding fluid density, g is gravitational acceleration and v is the submerged volume displacing fluid —not always the total volume of the object. for a free object floating at rest, f b equals its weight mg. hence ρ fluid gv submerged = mg. for a uniform object floating in a uniform liquid, that rearranges to: submerged fraction = ρ object, average / ρ fluid for instance, if an object's average density is 600 kg/m³ and the water density is 1,000 kg/m³, roughly 60% of its total volume must sit below the surface at equilibrium. if the ratio exceeds one, no partially submerged equilibrium is possible. why depth alone does not increase buoyancy pressure grows with depth, but the pressure on the top and bottom of a rigid fully submerged object both grow as the object moves deeper. in an incompressible fluid of constant density, the net difference—and therefore buoyant force—stays essentially unchanged when its submerged volume stays constant. a compressible object, or one in a fluid whose density varies, can behave differently. will it float? adjust the object's average density and the fluid's density. watch how much of the object must be submerged. surface fluid 60% submerged object average density 600 kg/m³ fluid density 1000 kg/m³ outcome floats submerged volume fraction 60% uniform rectangular block, fluid at rest. if the object is denser than the liquid, it sinks; the equilibrium fraction formula does not apply then. worked examples example 1 — displacement. a floating object weighs 19.62 n. how much freshwater does it displace? use ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 9.81 m/s². step 1. floating equilibrium means f b = w = 19.62 n. step 2. rearrange f b = ρgv to v = w/(ρg). step 3. v = 19.62/(1000×9.81) = 0.002 m³ = 2 litres . notice that the required displacement depends on weight , not directly on whether the object is made of wood, plastic or steel. example 2 — how far underwater? a uniform rectangular block has density 750 kg/m³ and floats in freshwater of density 1,000 kg/m³. what fraction of its height is submerged? step 1. at rest, ρ water gv submerged = ρ block gv total . step 2. divide both sides by ρ water gv total : fraction = 750/1000 = 0.75 . result: about 75% of its height is below the surface, provided its cross-section is uniform. example 3 — weight versus buoyancy. a completely submerged sealed object displaces 0.004 m³ of freshwater and weighs 50 n. does it rise or sink at first, neglecting drag? step 1. buoyancy is 1000 × 9.81 × 0.004 = 39.24 n upward. step 2. net force is 39.24 − 50 = −10.76 n (taking upward positive). conclusion: it initially accelerates downward. fluid drag will influence its subsequent motion. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. what buoyant force acts on an object displacing 0.001 m³ of freshwater? show worked answer f_b = 1000 × 9.81 × 0.001 = 9.81 n. problem 2 of 5 displace 1 l water; g=10 m/s². buoyant force? show answer and method 1000×10×0.001=10 n. problem 3 of 5 for floating equilibrium, what equals the object weight? show answer and method buoyant force. problem 4 of 5 does an object sink when its average density exceeds that of the fluid? show answer and method normally yes, if no other supporting forces. problem 5 of 5 does buoyancy require an object to be fully submerged? show answer and method no; partially submerged floating objects also experience buoyancy. try it yourself use a bowl of water and a small piece of modelling clay. first roll it into a solid ball; then reshape the same clay into an open boat with a wide base. can the boat displace enough water to support its weight while the ball cannot? for a second experiment, float an empty plastic container and add coins one at a time. observe the waterline. each extra coin increases weight, so the container has to displace more water to restore equilibrium. stop before it overflows, and keep the experiment away from electronic devices. when the simple picture changes archimedes' principle still applies to submerged objects, but the density-ratio rule describes a simple floating equilibrium, not every situation. surface tension matters at very small scales (for example, some insects), while tethering, trapped gas, changing fluid density, and compressible materials can modify what happens. for a moving object, drag and acceleration must also be considered. references fluid dynamics (2.06) — mit opencourseware, spring 2013. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← properties of fluids conservation of mass and fluid flow → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/chemical-bonding.html":"chemical bonds: sharing or transferring electrons — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate chemical bonds: sharing or transferring electrons why do atoms join together? compare ionic and covalent bonding through familiar examples and simple electron diagrams. why atoms form bonds atoms can reach lower-energy, more stable arrangements when they interact with other atoms. the valence electrons —electrons in the outermost occupied region—are especially important in predicting how atoms bond. two useful beginner models are ionic bonding , in which oppositely charged ions attract, and covalent bonding , in which atoms share electron density. real chemical bonding involves electrons described by quantum mechanics; these sketches are simplified learning models. atoms bond because certain electron arrangements can lower the total energy of the combined system. the common classroom categories—ionic, covalent and metallic—are useful models, but many real bonds have characteristics that lie between ideal categories. the chemical properties of a substance depend strongly on how its atoms and electrons are organised. table salt is built from a lattice of oppositely charged ions; water contains polar covalent bonds within molecules; copper has mobile electrons that help it conduct electricity. these structural differences explain why materials with similar-looking atoms can have radically different melting temperatures, hardness and electrical behaviour. ionic and covalent bonds sodium chloride: a sodium atom can lose an electron while a chlorine atom gains one. their ions, na⁺ and cl⁻, attract one another. solid table salt forms an extended ionic lattice , not separate nacl molecules. hydrogen gas: two hydrogen atoms each contribute an electron to a shared pair, forming h₂. in hydrogen chloride (hcl) , electrons are shared unequally: chlorine attracts them more strongly, creating a polar covalent bond. ionic bonding involves electrostatic attraction between ions in an extended structure. covalent bonding involves electron density shared between nuclei; it may be distributed unequally if the atoms have different electronegativities. in metals, bonding is often described through delocalised electrons associated with the metal lattice. a bond has an energy cost to break, but breaking bonds alone does not release energy. a chemical reaction's net energy change depends on energy required to break old bonds versus energy released when new bonds form. also distinguish bonds within molecules from intermolecular forces between molecules; water's boiling behaviour depends strongly on intermolecular hydrogen bonding, not on breaking the o–h covalent bonds in each molecule. compare three examples switch between examples. look for which electrons are shared and which are better represented as transferred. electron-picture model example sodium chloride (nacl) hydrogen molecule (h₂) hydrogen chloride (hcl) conceptual electron diagrams only; sizes, distances and electron distributions are not to scale. worked examples problem: sodium has one valence electron; chlorine has seven. describe the simplest ionic picture of sodium chloride. sodium loses one electron and becomes na⁺ . chlorine gains one electron and becomes cl⁻ . opposite charges attract, and many ions arrange into an ionic crystal lattice . electron transfer helps us understand the ion charges. an actual crystal's stability comes from the combined electrostatic interactions throughout the lattice. worked example 2 question. explain how a covalent bond forms between two hydrogen atoms. solution. each h supplies one valence electron. they share a pair, allowing each to attain a two-electron duet in h₂. the bonding involves electron density between nuclei. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: each hydrogen atom has one electron. how many electrons are in the shared pair of h₂, and what type of bond is it? show worked answer there are two electrons in one shared pair. h₂ has a nonpolar covalent bond because the two identical atoms share equally. problem 2 of 5 what bonding dominates crystalline sodium chloride? show answer and method ionic electrostatic attraction. problem 3 of 5 how many electron pairs form a single covalent bond? show answer and method one shared pair. problem 4 of 5 is a metal usually held together by isolated electron-pair bonds between fixed atom pairs? show answer and method no; metallic bonding involves delocalised electrons. problem 5 of 5 why can graphite conduct electricity while diamond generally cannot? show answer and method graphite has mobile delocalised electrons within layers; diamond bonds localise valence electrons. try it yourself draw dot diagrams for h₂, hcl and h₂o. in water, oxygen makes two o–h bonds and has two lone pairs in a basic lewis structure. compare your dot diagrams to the electron illustrations. next question: why does a molecule's shape matter for its overall polarity? references mit ocw 5.111sc: lecture 9, ionic and covalent bonds mit ocw 5.111sc: chemical bonding & structure, unit ii explanations, diagrams, examples and interactive demonstrations are original. mit opencourseware is credited as a topic reference; no mit figures or passages have been reproduced. mit ocw licensing and terms . ← back to courses free, independent learning material. not affiliated with mit.","lessons/circular-motion.html":"circular motion and gravitation — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate circular motion and gravitation why is a changing direction itself a kind of acceleration? why curved motion needs a force moving around a circle at constant speed still changes the velocity vector. the acceleration points inward, toward the centre. gravity can provide this inward acceleration for planets and satellites; a string can provide it for a whirling mass. when a stone moves around on a string, its speed may remain constant while its direction changes continuously. that change is acceleration, directed inward toward the circle's centre. the string supplies the necessary inward force; without it, the stone would move approximately along a straight tangent rather than flying outward along the original radius. gravity plays an analogous role in satellite motion. the satellite is continually falling toward earth, yet has enough sideways velocity to keep missing the surface. circular motion is therefore a useful bridge between everyday mechanics and orbital motion. centripetal acceleration for uniform circular motion, centripetal acceleration is v²/r. multiply by mass to find required inward net force. for orbiting objects, gravity supplies this force. a_c = v²/r; f_c = mv²/r for uniform circular motion of radius r at speed v, inward acceleration has magnitude a_c=v²/r, so the net inward force needed is mv²/r. it is not an extra “centripetal force” added to a free-body diagram: tension, gravity, friction or another real interaction supplies the inward resultant. for a car taking a flat bend of radius 50 m at 10 m/s, the required lateral acceleration is 2 m/s². tyre–road friction supplies the sideways force. if road conditions deteriorate, the maximum available friction can become insufficient. angular speed ω=v/r, and for steady circular motion the period t=2π/ω. these relationships assume approximately circular travel, not arbitrary curving paths. worked examples question. a 0.5 kg object moves at 4 m/s in a horizontal circle of radius 2 m. find inward force. solution, step by step. a_c = 4²/2 = 8 m/s². inward force = ma_c = 0.5 × 8 = 4 n. worked example 2 question. a 2 kg mass goes at 6 m/s around radius 3 m. find centripetal force. solution. f=mv²/r=2×36/3=24 n, directed toward the circle centre. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a car follows a curve of radius 50 m at 10 m/s. find required centripetal acceleration. show worked answer a_c = v²/r = 100/50 = 2 m/s² inward. problem 2 of 5 find centripetal acceleration for v=10 m/s, r=5 m. show answer and method a=v²/r=20 m/s². problem 3 of 5 if circular speed doubles at fixed radius, required centripetal acceleration changes by what factor? show answer and method 4. problem 4 of 5 does centripetal force point tangentially? show answer and method no; radially inward. problem 5 of 5 what provides inward acceleration for a satellite in orbit? show answer and method gravity. try it yourself draw velocity arrows tangent to a circular path at several positions. compare the change between neighbouring arrows. where this model stops centripetal force is the name for the inward net force, not a separate force to add to gravity or tension. references classical mechanics (8.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2016. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← momentum and collisions torque and rotational motion → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/coordinate-geometry.html":"coordinate geometry in 2d and 3d — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate coordinate geometry in 2d and 3d how can we describe shapes and distances precisely in two or three dimensions? describing position with coordinates coordinates locate points relative to chosen axes. once a point has numbers, you can calculate distances, directions and intersections without depending on a scale drawing. three-dimensional coordinates extend the same idea with a z-axis. coordinates let us replace a sketch with precise statements about position. a point such as (3,4) means something only after we specify an origin, directions and units. that reference choice matters: a robot's coordinates relative to its own body differ from its coordinates relative to a room. the same geometric object can be described in several systems. cartesian coordinates are convenient for rectangular layouts, while polar coordinates are useful when distance and direction from a central point are more natural. switching representations does not move the object; it changes how we describe it. distance, slope and direction in 2d the distance between points follows from pythagoras. in 3d, add the squared difference in the third direction. midpoints use the average coordinate along each axis. d = √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)² + (z₂−z₁)²) in a plane, the distance between (x₁,y₁) and (x₂,y₂) follows from a right triangle: d=sqrt((x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²). the slope of a nonvertical line is (y₂−y₁)/(x₂−x₁); a vertical line needs a different description because its horizontal difference is zero. for navigation, a point can be represented by radius r and angle θ, with x=r cosθ and y=r sinθ. in three dimensions, add z and use the same distance principle. avoid treating coordinate numbers as physical truth without considering measurement uncertainty, axis conventions and whether your reference frame is rotating or translating. worked examples question. find the distance between a(1,2,3) and b(4,6,3). solution, step by step. coordinate differences are (3,4,0). distance = √(3² + 4² + 0²) = 5 length units. the z-values match, so the separation lies in a plane. worked example 2 question. find the straight-line distance between a(1,2,3) and b(4,6,3). solution. difference = (3,4,0); distance = √(3²+4²+0²)=5. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. find the midpoint between (−2,4) and (6,8). show worked answer average x and y separately: ((−2+6)/2,(4+8)/2) = (2,6). problem 2 of 5 find midpoint of (2,4) and (6,0). show answer and method (4,2). problem 3 of 5 what is the gradient between (0,1) and (2,5)? show answer and method (5−1)/(2−0)=2. problem 4 of 5 find the distance from origin to (5,12). show answer and method 13. problem 5 of 5 write the equation of a line through (0,3) with slope −2. show answer and method y=−2x+3. try it yourself mark points for holes on a cardboard plate using two edges as reference axes. use coordinates to plan a rectangular hole pattern. where this model stops coordinate differences must use consistent units and the same reference frame. references multivariable calculus (18.02sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2010. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← matrices and linear equations probability → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/deployable-origami.html":"deployable mechanisms and origami in space — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate deployable mechanisms and origami in space why are folding patterns useful for antennas and solar arrays? why fold structures for space? spacecraft must fit inside a launch envelope but may need large collecting areas in orbit. folding and deployment mechanisms solve this packaging problem. origami-inspired geometry can help plan compact storage, repeatability and controlled motion. some spacecraft components must fit inside a launch-vehicle fairing and later become much larger. solar arrays, antennas and sunshields can use folded panels, hinges, tapes, springs or inflatable structures to meet that packaging challenge. origami offers geometric ways to fold a surface compactly while controlling how it unfolds. a folded sheet may appear easy to deploy by hand, but a flight mechanism must deploy predictably in microgravity after vibration, thermal cycling and prolonged storage. a single stuck hinge can compromise an entire structure, so deployment sequences and verification are essential parts of the design. geometry of a deployable mechanism deployment design involves hinge paths, clearances, stiffness and stored or motor-driven energy. a simple engineering check is deployment ratio: deployed span divided by stowed span, though usable area and shape accuracy also matter. deployment ratio = deployed span / stowed span a mechanism's deployment geometry relates joint rotations to final panel positions and can be studied using linkages and kinematics. stowed volume, deployed stiffness, latch reliability and stored strain energy are important design parameters. some origami patterns can fold with coordinated motions, but real panels have thickness, hinge offsets and material limits absent from ideal zero-thickness paper models. a classroom prototype might begin with cardstock and recorded fold lines, then progress to cad models that include hinge clearance and panel thickness. for a powered mechanism, consider torque required throughout travel, possible singular positions, hard stops and how the deployed structure becomes stable. never assume that successful manual deployment demonstrates launch readiness. worked examples question. a folding panel fits within a 0.50 m envelope and unfolds to a 2.0 m span. find deployment ratio. solution, step by step. ratio = 2.0/0.50 = 4. the span expands by a factor of four; this says nothing by itself about stiffness or precision. worked example 2 question. a hinged boom rotates 90° in 3 s at constant angular speed. find average angular speed in rad/s. solution. 90°=π/2 rad; ω=(π/2)/3=π/6≈0.524 rad/s. this ignores acceleration phases. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a structure packs into 0.4 m and deploys to 1.6 m. what span ratio does it achieve? show worked answer 1.6/0.4 = 4. problem 2 of 5 convert 180° to radians. show answer and method π rad. problem 3 of 5 a 2 m boom tip rotates through 0.5 rad: approximate arc distance? show answer and method s=rθ=1 m. problem 4 of 5 what makes a hinge a one-degree-of-freedom joint? show answer and method it permits one independent rotational coordinate in an ideal rigid-link mechanism. problem 5 of 5 why can folding be useful for a satellite antenna? show answer and method it packs a large deployed structure into a smaller launch envelope. try it yourself fold a strip of paper using alternating mountain and valley creases. measure its stowed and deployed spans; notice where panels collide. where this model stops a paper fold is a geometry demonstrator, not a verified spacecraft mechanism. repeated cycling, latches and thermal effects require testing. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← thermal management in space the space environment → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/derivatives.html":"derivatives: understanding change — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · lesson 01 · high school → undergraduate derivatives: understanding change from a car’s speed to a cooling object: learn to measure how fast something changes at a single instant. what does a derivative mean? a car's trip meter might tell you how far it has travelled, but it doesn't directly tell you how fast it is moving right now . a derivative answers questions about the instantaneous rate of change . on a distance–time graph, the slope between two points describes average speed over an interval. the slope at a single point describes instantaneous speed. it helps to distinguish a measurement from how that measurement changes. a thermometer tells you the temperature at one instant; repeated readings tell you whether the room is warming quickly or slowly. the derivative describes the local rate of change, even when that rate varies from moment to moment. on a graph it is the slope of the tangent line, but in an experiment it might represent speed, heating rate or flow sensitivity. a positive derivative means a quantity is increasing as the input increases, and a negative derivative means it is decreasing. zero slope does not necessarily mean nothing interesting is happening: an object thrown upward has zero vertical velocity at the very top, although gravity is still accelerating it downward. to interpret any derivative, always name the quantity being differentiated and its units. from an average to an instant consider the simple function s(t) = t² . in an imaginary motion model, s is distance in metres and t is time in seconds. average speed = [s(t + h) − s(t)] / h for t = 2 and h = 1, the average speed is (9 − 4) / 1 = 5 m/s . but if we make the time interval very small, the average speed approaches 4 m/s . derivative = lim (h → 0) [s(t + h) − s(t)] / h for s(t) = t², expanding (t + h)² gives [2th + h²] / h = 2t + h. as h approaches zero, the derivative becomes 2t . the formal idea begins with an average rate over an interval: change in output divided by change in input. shrink that interval while watching whether the average approaches one stable value. if it does, that limit is the derivative at the point. the process is not merely an algebraic trick; it explains why the instantaneous rate is meaningful. for s(t)=t², the average rate from t to t+h is ((t+h)²-t²)/h = 2t+h when h≠0. as h approaches zero, the rate approaches 2t. if s is in metres and t is in seconds, this derivative has units m/s. if the graph has a sharp corner, the slope from the left and the slope from the right may disagree, so a derivative need not exist there. later, derivatives will let us optimise designs and model acceleration. explore the slope move the time and interval sliders. the secant joins two points; as the interval shrinks, its slope approaches the tangent 's slope at the first point. the curve s(t) = t² starting time, t 1.00 time interval, h 1.00 t s t² solid ochre = secant; dashed brown = tangent; dark curve = s(t) = t². average slope = 3 m/s; instantaneous slope = 2 m/s. worked examples problem: a toy car's position follows s(t) = 2t² + 3t metres. what is its instantaneous velocity at t = 4 seconds? differentiate: ds/dt = 4t + 3 . put t = 4: velocity = 4(4) + 3 = 19 m/s . interpretation: at that instant, its position is increasing at 19 metres per second in the chosen positive direction. this model is a mathematical example, not a typical real toy-car speed. worked example 2 question. the position of a particle is x(t) = t³ − 3t metres. find its velocity at t = 2 s. solution. differentiate: v(t) = 3t² − 3. substitute t = 2: v(2) = 12 − 3 = 9 m/s. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: the temperature of an object is modelled by t(t) = 20 + 6t − t² °c. at t = 2 minutes, is it heating or cooling, and how quickly? show worked answer dt/dt = 6 − 2t. at t = 2, dt/dt = +2 °c/min , so it is heating at that instant. problem 2 of 5 differentiate f(x) = 7x² − 4x + 2. show answer and method f′(x) = 14x − 4. problem 3 of 5 if s(t) = 5t² metres, what is the velocity at 3 s? show answer and method v = 10t, so 30 m/s. problem 4 of 5 where is f(x) = x² − 6x stationary? show answer and method f′ = 2x−6 = 0 at x = 3. problem 5 of 5 what does a negative derivative tell you about a graph? show answer and method the function is decreasing at that point, provided the derivative exists. try it yourself walk at a steady pace while someone records your position every second. plot distance against time. a nearly straight line has an almost constant slope. try speeding up: what happens to the slope? next idea: when the derivative of position is velocity, what does the derivative of velocity represent? acceleration. references mit opencourseware: 18.01sc, introduction to derivatives . mit opencourseware: 18.01sc, derivative as rate of change . original explanations, examples and simulations prepared for this website. mit ocw is credited as a topic reference; no mit diagrams or text reproduced. mit ocw licence information . ← back to courses independent, free learning material. no affiliation with mit.","lessons/differential-equations.html":"introduction to differential equations — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate introduction to differential equations what happens when a system’s rate of change depends on its current state? an equation for changing quantities a differential equation describes a rule for change rather than giving a direct formula for the state. such equations model cooling, population growth, spring motion and electric circuits. an initial condition selects one particular solution. some scientific laws describe not the quantity itself but how quickly it changes. newton’s second law relates acceleration to force; a cooling law may relate temperature-change rate to the temperature difference from the surroundings. a differential equation captures that relationship and asks what function of time satisfies it. this is why differential equations connect disparate subjects. the same mathematical pattern can describe water draining from a tank, radioactive decay, and the voltage across a discharging capacitor, though the physical meanings and parameters differ. the equation tells us how a system evolves; its initial condition tells us which particular evolution we are observing. relating a function to its rate of change in a simple growth model, rate is proportional to amount: dy/dt = ky. the solution has the form y(t) = y₀e^(kt), where k controls growth or decay. a positive k produces growth, a negative k decay. dy/dt = ky; y(t) = y₀e^(kt) consider dy/dt=−ky with constant k>0. it says that the rate of decrease is proportional to the amount remaining. a solution is y(t)=ce^(−kt), because differentiating it gives −k times itself. if y(0)=y₀, then c=y₀. the time constant 1/k gives a useful sense of how quickly the process changes. not every differential equation has a simple closed-form solution, and a mathematically valid equation may be a poor physical model outside its assumptions. real cooling coefficients can depend on airflow and temperature; real drag may not be proportional to speed. later, numerical stepping methods help compute approximate trajectories when analytical solutions are difficult. worked examples question. a quantity starts at 100 units and obeys dy/dt = 0.1y per hour. estimate its amount after 3 hours. solution, step by step. with y₀ = 100 and k = 0.1 h⁻¹, y(3) = 100e^0.3 ≈ 134.99 units. its rate increases as the amount increases. worked example 2 question. a hot object cools with dt/dt = −0.2(t−20), with t(0)=80°c. find t(5). solution. separate variables: t−20 = ce^(−0.2t). initially c=60. thus t(5)=20+60e^(−1)≈42.1°c. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. if y₀ = 80 and k = −0.2 h⁻¹, approximately how much remains after 5 hours? show worked answer y(5) = 80e⁻¹ ≈ 29.43 units. problem 2 of 5 does y=e²ˣ solve dy/dx=2y? show answer and method yes: y′=2e²ˣ=2y. problem 3 of 5 solve dy/dx=3 with y(0)=2. show answer and method y=3x+2. problem 4 of 5 what extra information selects one solution from y′=2x? show answer and method an initial or boundary condition; general solution y=x²+c. problem 5 of 5 if y′=−ky with k>0 and y>0, is y rising or falling? show answer and method falling: its derivative is negative. try it yourself start with 100 and repeatedly add 10% of the current value in a spreadsheet. compare discrete updates with the continuous exponential model. where this model stops the exponential rule assumes k stays constant and no capacity limit intervenes; real systems often violate these assumptions. references differential equations (18.03sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2011. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← applications of integration matrices and linear equations → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/dot-cross-products.html":"dot products and cross products — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate dot products and cross products what makes one kind of vector multiplication describe work and another turning? two ways to combine vectors the dot product measures how aligned two vectors are; the cross product measures their perpendicular interaction and has a direction. both use the same pair of vectors yet answer different physical questions. two vector products answer different geometric questions. the dot product asks how much one vector points along another. the cross product asks how strongly they point across one another and gives a direction perpendicular to both. that difference becomes crucial when calculating work and torque. pushing a crate horizontally performs mechanical work through the horizontal part of your force. if you also pull upward, that vertical component makes no contribution to the work done during a purely horizontal displacement. by contrast, when tightening a bolt, the turning effect depends on how nearly perpendicular your push is to the wrench. projection, torque and direction a force acting along a displacement does work according to its dot product. a force at a lever arm produces torque according to a cross product; only the perpendicular part contributes. in 2d, the torque direction is perpendicular to the plane. a · b = |a||b| cos θ; |a × b| = |a||b| sin θ the dot product a·b=|a||b|cosθ is a scalar. it is zero for perpendicular vectors and positive or negative according to their relative directions. the cross product magnitude |a×b|=|a||b|sinθ is greatest for perpendicular vectors and zero for parallel ones; its direction follows the right-hand rule. for example, a 20 n force applied perpendicular to a 0.3 m lever arm generates a torque magnitude of 6 n·m. the same force applied along the lever arm produces zero torque about the pivot. in cartesian coordinates, the dot product is axbx+ayby+azbz; the cross product uses component combinations and produces another vector. neither operation can generally be substituted for the other. worked examples question. a 20 n force acts 60° from a 3 m displacement. what work is done? solution, step by step. work = fd cos θ = 20 × 3 × cos 60° = 30 j. only the component of force parallel to movement transfers energy in this model. worked example 2 question. calculate a·b for a=(2,−1,3) and b=(4,0,2). solution. multiply matching components and sum: 2×4 + (−1)×0 + 3×2 = 14. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a 5 n force is perpendicular to a 0.4 m lever. find the torque magnitude. show worked answer τ = rf sin 90° = 0.4 × 5 = 2 n·m. problem 2 of 5 find (3,4)·(0,2). show answer and method 3×0 + 4×2 = 8. problem 3 of 5 what is the dot product of perpendicular nonzero vectors? show answer and method zero. problem 4 of 5 for a=(1,0,0), b=(0,1,0), find a×b. show answer and method (0,0,1), by the right-hand rule. problem 5 of 5 if two vectors point in the same direction, what is the sign of their dot product? show answer and method positive, provided neither vector is zero. try it yourself hold a ruler by one end and press it at different angles. which push turns it most effectively? relate your observation to the sine factor. where this model stops the dot product is a scalar; the cross product is a vector. in 3d its direction follows the right-hand rule. references multivariable calculus (18.02sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2010. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← vector components and resultants trigonometry and its applications → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/electricity-magnetism.html":"electricity and magnetism — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate electricity and magnetism how does a battery create current, and why can a magnet move a motor? charges, currents and fields electric potential difference is energy per unit charge. current is the rate of moving charge, and resistance tells us how strongly a component opposes current. changing magnetic fields and electric currents are linked, enabling motors and generators. electricity begins with charge and its interactions. an electric field describes the force that a small test charge would experience at a point; a voltage difference describes energy transferred per unit charge between points. these are related ideas, not interchangeable terms. moving charge makes electric current, and currents generate magnetic fields. magnetism also influences moving charges, allowing motors to produce torque and generators to produce electrical energy. many devices combine both effects: fans, sensors, wireless communication and power supplies all rely on electromagnetic principles. how electrical and magnetic forces relate ohm’s law describes an ideal resistor: voltage equals current times resistance. electrical power delivered to that resistor is voltage times current. magnetic force on a current-carrying conductor depends on current, field and orientation. v = ir; p = vi ohm’s law v=ir describes the voltage–current relation of an ideal resistor at fixed conditions. for a 6 ω resistor connected across 12 v, current is 2 a and dissipated power is vi=24 w. real components can be nonlinear or temperature dependent, and a circuit's current depends on the full network. a changing magnetic flux through a circuit induces an electromotive force, as described by faraday’s law. that principle underlies transformers and generators. the direction of the induced effect follows lenz’s law: it opposes the change in flux that produced it. electrical safety and appropriate current limits are essential in any practical experiment, even when the theory seems simple. worked examples question. a 12 v source is connected to a 6 ω ideal resistor. find current and power. solution, step by step. i = v/r = 12/6 = 2 a. power = vi = 12 × 2 = 24 w. the resistor converts electrical energy to heat. worked example 2 question. a 12 v battery is across a 4 ω resistor. find current and power. solution. ohm’s law: i=v/r=12/4=3 a. electrical power p=vi=12×3=36 w. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a 9 v source drives 0.3 a through a resistor. find resistance. show worked answer r = v/i = 9/0.3 = 30 ω. problem 2 of 5 6 v applied to 2 ω resistor: current? show answer and method 3 a. problem 3 of 5 power at 5 v and 2 a? show answer and method 10 w. problem 4 of 5 two 3 ω resistors in series have total resistance? show answer and method 6 ω. problem 5 of 5 how does magnetic force act on a moving charged particle relative to its velocity? show answer and method perpendicular to velocity for the magnetic part of the lorentz force. try it yourself build a low-voltage circuit with a battery pack, suitable resistor and led following component ratings. compare series and parallel connections on paper. where this model stops do not connect a battery directly across a wire without current limiting; mains electricity is not appropriate for home experiments. references electricity and magnetism (8.02) — mit opencourseware, spring 2019. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← waves, sound and light ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/energy.html":"work and conservation of energy — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate work and conservation of energy follow energy as an object descends: gravitational potential energy becomes kinetic energy in an ideal, frictionless model. energy changes form hold a ball above the floor. it has gravitational potential energy because of its position relative to the floor. release it, and its speed increases: potential energy becomes kinetic energy. the total mechanical energy remains constant if we neglect air resistance and other losses. this is often an easier way to solve motion problems than calculating acceleration at every moment. real systems can transfer mechanical energy into heat, sound and deformation; total energy is still conserved. energy is a way of accounting for a system's ability to produce changes. a lifted book, a moving bicycle and a compressed spring store energy in different forms. work transfers energy through a force acting over displacement, while heat transfers energy because of temperature difference. the value of an energy approach is that we do not need to follow every instant of motion to compare initial and final states. a skateboard rolling down a hill trades gravitational potential energy for kinetic energy, but some mechanical energy also becomes internal energy through friction. “energy is conserved” does not mean useful mechanical energy stays constant. two useful equations gravitational potential energy u = mgh kinetic energy k = ½mv² here m is mass (kg), h is height above the chosen reference (m), g ≈ 9.81 m/s² near earth's surface, and v is speed (m/s). an object released from rest at height h has initial mechanical energy mgh. at a lower height h, mgh + ½mv² = mgh . rearranging gives v = √[2g(h − h)] . the mass cancels: the speed in this ideal model does not depend on mass. for a constant force acting along a displacement, work w=fd; more generally w is the integral of the force component along the path. kinetic energy is k=½mv², and the net work on a particle equals its change in kinetic energy. near earth's surface, gravitational potential energy changes by mgδh. if a 2 kg object falls through 3 m from rest without drag, the potential-energy loss is 2×9.81×3≈58.9 j. that becomes kinetic energy, giving v=sqrt(2gh)≈7.67 m/s. if drag is significant, the final speed is lower while total energy remains conserved across a larger system including the surrounding air and thermal effects. set system boundaries clearly before writing conservation equations. move the object downward choose a release height and how far the object has descended. the bars show how the same initial total energy is shared between potential and kinetic energy. ideal drop (no air drag) release height h (m) 5 distance fallen (% of h) 40% mass = 1 kg floor: h = 0 energy is measured relative to the floor; the illustration is schematic. worked examples problem: a 2 kg cart starts from rest 5 m above a reference level and rolls along an ideal frictionless track. what is its speed at height 1.8 m? height lost: δh = 5 − 1.8 = 3.2 m . use ½mv² = mgδh, cancelling m: v = √(2gδh). v = √(2 × 9.81 × 3.2) ≈ 7.92 m/s . on a real track, rolling rotation and friction can change the result. our ideal model treats the cart as a particle with translational kinetic energy only. worked example 2 question. a 1.5 kg cart moves at 4 m/s. find its kinetic energy. solution. eₖ=(1/2)mv²=0.5×1.5×16=12 j. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: an object is released from rest 8 m above ground. neglect air resistance. how fast is it moving just before it reaches ground level? show worked answer v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.81 × 8) ≈ 12.53 m/s . problem 2 of 5 lift a 2 kg book by 3 m; take g=10 m/s². gpe gained? show answer and method mgh=60 j. problem 3 of 5 a 10 n force acts along 5 m of displacement. work? show answer and method w=fs=50 j. problem 4 of 5 what happens to potential energy when an ideal pendulum descends? show answer and method it converts to kinetic energy. problem 5 of 5 if a moving object doubles its speed, how does kinetic energy change? show answer and method fourfold, as eₖ ∝ v². try it yourself roll a small ball down a low, stable cardboard ramp and compare how far it travels when released from two different heights. keep the setup near the ground and away from edges. note that the ball also has rotational kinetic energy, so the point-mass speed formula above is only an approximation. think further: where does mechanical energy go when the ball stops? references mit ocw 8.01sc: kinetic energy and work, week 7 mit ocw 8.01sc: classical mechanics (energy conservation, week 8) explanations, diagrams, examples and interactive demonstrations are original. mit opencourseware is credited as a topic reference; no mit figures or passages have been reproduced. mit ocw licensing and terms . ← back to courses free, independent learning material. not affiliated with mit.","lessons/equilibrium.html":"chemical equilibrium — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate chemical equilibrium how can a reaction keep going while its amounts stop changing? why reactions do not always go to completion at dynamic chemical equilibrium, forward and reverse reactions continue at equal rates. concentrations remain stable, but molecules are still reacting. changing concentrations, pressure or temperature can shift the equilibrium position. many chemical reactions proceed in both directions. at equilibrium, forward and reverse reaction rates are equal, so macroscopic composition stops changing even though molecules continue reacting. equilibrium is dynamic, not a sign that all chemical activity has ceased. a closed bottle of carbonated drink offers an everyday connection. dissolved carbon dioxide and gas in the space above the liquid can reach an equilibrium. opening the bottle changes the pressure and conditions, and bubbles form as the system moves toward a new state. the balance between forward and reverse reactions for a simple reversible reaction a ⇌ b, the equilibrium constant kc describes the ratio of equilibrium concentrations according to the balanced reaction. a large k favours products, but does not indicate how quickly equilibrium is reached. for a ⇌ b: kc = [b]/[a] for a reaction aa+bb⇌cc+dd, an equilibrium constant is formed from activities of products and reactants raised to their stoichiometric coefficients. under suitable approximations, concentrations may be used in a familiar kc expression. the numerical value depends on temperature and indicates the relative equilibrium composition. changing concentration or pressure can shift the equilibrium composition, but a catalyst changes forward and reverse reaction rates without changing k or the equilibrium state at fixed temperature. it helps reach equilibrium faster. le châtelier’s principle is a useful qualitative guide, though quantitative predictions require the actual equilibrium relationships and constraints. worked examples question. at equilibrium for a ⇌ b, [a] = 0.20 mol/l and [b] = 0.60 mol/l. find kc. solution, step by step. kc = 0.60/0.20 = 3, dimensionless for this simplified reaction. the equilibrium mixture contains more b than a. worked example 2 question. for n₂o₄(g) ⇌ 2no₂(g), predict effect of decreasing volume at fixed temperature. solution. pressure rises; the equilibrium tends to shift toward fewer gas moles: n₂o₄ side. this is a qualitative le châtelier prediction. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. if [a] = 0.50 mol/l and [b] = 0.25 mol/l for a ⇌ b, calculate kc. show worked answer kc = 0.25/0.50 = 0.5. problem 2 of 5 at equilibrium, must reactant and product concentrations be equal? show answer and method no: forward and reverse rates are equal. problem 3 of 5 adding a reactant often shifts equilibrium which way? show answer and method toward products, if other conditions remain suitable. problem 4 of 5 does a catalyst change equilibrium composition? show answer and method no; it speeds approach to equilibrium. problem 5 of 5 for an exothermic forward reaction, increasing temperature favours which direction? show answer and method the reverse (endothermic) direction. try it yourself model a and b as beads in two cups. move beads both directions each round while adjusting rates until net counts stabilise. where this model stops equilibrium constants depend on temperature; adding a catalyst changes how quickly equilibrium is reached, not k at fixed temperature. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← energy in chemical reactions ph, indicators and buffers → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/escape-velocity.html":"gravity and escape velocity — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate gravity and escape velocity why can a spacecraft leave earth without continuing to fire its engine forever? can an object escape gravity? gravity weakens with distance, and gaining altitude increases gravitational potential energy. escape speed is the minimum starting speed needed for an unpowered object to reach arbitrarily far away with zero leftover speed in a simplified two-body model. throw a ball upward and earth's gravity slows it until it reaches a highest point. give it more initial speed and it rises higher. escape speed is the ideal threshold at which an object can move indefinitely far from a gravitating body without needing more propulsion, ending with zero speed at infinity in the simplified model. it is not a rule that rockets must immediately exceed escape speed at ground level. a rocket can climb and accelerate under sustained thrust, and mission planning may use parking orbits and later manoeuvres. escape energy is a statement about the total mechanical energy of a trajectory, not a universal launch instruction. energy and escape speed set initial kinetic energy equal to the magnitude of gravitational binding energy. the object’s own mass cancels, so escape speed depends on the attracting body’s mass and launch radius, not on spacecraft mass. real launches also contend with atmosphere and earth rotation. v_escape = √(2gm/r) for a spherical central mass m, the specific mechanical energy of an object is ε=v²/2−gm/r. setting ε=0 gives the local escape speed v_esc=sqrt(2gm/r). the expression assumes no atmospheric drag, no other gravitational bodies and no additional propulsion after the initial condition. because orbital speed for a circular orbit at the same radius is sqrt(gm/r), local escape speed is sqrt(2) times circular speed. the radius is measured from the centre of the gravitating body. in real missions, atmospheric losses and gravity losses, finite burns, other-body effects and planetary rotation influence required launch performance. worked examples question. earth’s gravitational parameter is μ = gm ≈ 3.986 × 10¹⁴ m³/s²; earth radius r ≈ 6.371 × 10⁶ m. estimate escape speed at the surface. solution, step by step. v = √(2 × 3.986×10¹⁴ / 6.371×10⁶) ≈ 11,186 m/s ≈ 11.2 km/s. this is a vacuum idealisation, not a launch vehicle’s required single burn. worked example 2 question. calculate escape speed at a planet surface with μ=400,000 km³/s² and radius 6400 km, ignoring atmosphere/rotation. solution. vₑ=√(2μ/r)=√(800000/6400)=√125≈11.18 km/s. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. if a planet had the same radius as earth but four times its mass, how would escape speed change? show worked answer v_escape scales as √m at fixed radius; quadrupling mass doubles escape speed. problem 2 of 5 escape speed formula in terms of μ and radius? show answer and method √(2μ/r). problem 3 of 5 if planet mass doubles at same radius, escape speed changes by factor? show answer and method √2. problem 4 of 5 is reaching escape velocity the only way a powered craft can escape? show answer and method no; continuous propulsion can escape without an initial instantaneous speed equal to the ballistic escape speed. problem 5 of 5 does ideal ballistic escape speed depend on spacecraft mass? show answer and method no. try it yourself compare the formula with circular-orbit speed v_c = √(gm/r). why is escape speed √2 times greater at the same location? where this model stops escape speed is not the same as the rocket’s required total δv: losses, trajectories and staging matter. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← powering a satellite rocket stages and the rocket equation → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/fluid-continuity.html":"conservation of mass and fluid flow — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate conservation of mass and fluid flow why does water speed up when a pipe becomes narrower? where does the moving fluid go? mass cannot disappear between two sections of a closed pipe. in steady flow with no leaks, mass entering each second equals mass leaving. if fluid density stays approximately constant, a smaller flow area requires higher mean velocity. imagine squeezing the nozzle on a garden hose. water often leaves the smaller opening faster, even though the hose is supplied at roughly the same overall flow rate. water is not disappearing inside the nozzle; the same amount of mass must cross successive sections each second under steady conditions without leaks. the distinction between volume flow and mass flow is essential. for an incompressible liquid, conserving mass usually also conserves volume flow. for a compressible gas, density can change substantially, so equal mass flow can correspond to different volume flow rates along a duct. conservation of mass mass flow rate is density × cross-sectional area × mean speed. for incompressible steady flow through two sections, a₁v₁ = a₂v₂. this statement is about average section velocities, not necessarily each point in the flow. ṁ = ρav; a₁v₁ = a₂v₂ mass flow rate is ṁ=ρav for uniform average velocity normal to a cross-section. for steady flow in a single passage with no additions or removals, ρ₁a₁v₁=ρ₂a₂v₂. if density is effectively constant, the factors ρ cancel, giving a₁v₁=a₂v₂. suppose a water pipe narrows from 40 mm to 20 mm diameter. its cross-sectional area becomes one quarter as large, so average speed must become four times greater if flow is steady and incompressible. this does not mean pressure automatically rises or falls by a particular amount; momentum and energy equations and frictional losses are needed for those questions. always use actual inner diameters for pipe calculations. see continuity in action move the area slider to narrow the outlet. for steady incompressible flow, volume flow is unchanged. inlet outlet v₁ = 2 m/s v₂ = 4 m/s outlet area / inlet area 50% inlet speed 2.0 m/s outlet speed 4.0 m/s inlet / outlet volume flow 2.0 / 2.0 (relative) schematic geometry, not drawn to scale. steady incompressible flow; velocities are cross-sectional averages. the inlet area is set to 1 arbitrary area unit. worked examples question. water flows at 2 m/s through a pipe area of 0.004 m², then enters a narrower region with area 0.001 m². find speed. solution, step by step. volume flow = 0.004 × 2 = 0.008 m³/s. new speed = 0.008/0.001 = 8 m/s. worked example 2 question. in steady incompressible flow, area changes from 0.04 to 0.01 m²; inlet speed is 2 m/s. find exit speed. solution. conservation of volume flow: a₁v₁=a₂v₂. v₂=0.04×2/0.01=8 m/s. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a pipe narrows from area 6 cm² to 3 cm². if speed before narrowing is 1 m/s, find speed after. show worked answer a₁v₁ = a₂v₂ gives v₂ = (6/3) × 1 = 2 m/s. problem 2 of 5 if a=0.02 m² and v=3 m/s, find volume flow rate. show answer and method q=av=0.06 m³/s. problem 3 of 5 why does fluid speed rise through a narrowing pipe in steady incompressible flow? show answer and method the same volume must cross every section per unit time. problem 4 of 5 for a compressible gas, which flow rate is conserved in steady flow? show answer and method mass flow rate, ρav. problem 5 of 5 convert 0.001 m³/s to l/s. show answer and method 1 l/s. try it yourself compare water leaving a tap when the opening is partly narrowed. sketch a control volume spanning two cross-sections. where this model stops compressible gas flow requires accounting for density changes; the simple av = constant rule may not apply. references fluid dynamics (2.06) — mit opencourseware, spring 2013. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← buoyancy and floating objects bernoulli’s principle → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/fluid-properties.html":"properties of fluids — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate properties of fluids why does honey flow more slowly than water, and why do droplets bead up? what makes one fluid different from another? fluids can deform continuously under shear. density describes mass per volume, dynamic viscosity describes resistance to shearing flow, and surface tension describes energy associated with creating liquid surface. they influence different phenomena. water, honey and air are all fluids: they continue to deform when subjected to shear stress rather than retaining one permanent shape like an elastic solid. yet they behave very differently because of density, viscosity, compressibility and surface tension. each property answers a different physical question. density relates mass to volume and helps determine hydrostatic pressure and buoyancy. viscosity describes resistance to shear and strongly affects flow losses. surface tension originates from molecular interactions at a liquid interface and becomes particularly important for droplets, capillary effects and small-scale flows. density, viscosity and surface tension density ρ = m/v. for a newtonian liquid in simple shear, shear stress is proportional to the velocity gradient with dynamic viscosity μ as the constant. surface tension σ acts along the liquid interface, influencing droplets and capillary rise. ρ = m/v; τ = μ (du/dy) dynamic viscosity μ connects shear stress to velocity gradient in a newtonian fluid: τ=μ(du/dy). water and air are approximately newtonian over many common conditions, while some paints, slurries and food products are not. kinematic viscosity ν=μ/ρ combines viscosity with density and has units m²/s. for flow around an object or through a pipe, the reynolds number compares inertia with viscosity, typically re=ρvl/μ. it is dimensionless; its interpretation depends on geometry and the flow problem. viscosity is also temperature dependent: liquid viscosity generally decreases as temperature rises, while gas viscosity often increases. never assume that one room-temperature property value is adequate for a wide operating range. worked examples question. a sample of liquid has mass 1.2 kg and volume 0.0015 m³. find its density. solution, step by step. ρ = 1.2/0.0015 = 800 kg/m³. it is less dense than water at ordinary room conditions. worked example 2 question. a liquid has mass 3 kg and volume 0.002 m³. find density. solution. ρ=m/v=3/0.002=1500 kg/m³. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a liquid has mass 450 g and volume 500 ml. calculate density in g/ml. show worked answer ρ = 450/500 = 0.90 g/ml. problem 2 of 5 mass of 2 l of water at 1000 kg/m³? show answer and method 2 kg, since 2 l = 0.002 m³. problem 3 of 5 which flows more easily under equal geometry and driving force: higher- or lower-viscosity liquid? show answer and method generally lower viscosity. problem 4 of 5 give one effect caused by surface tension. show answer and method formation of small droplets or capillary effects. problem 5 of 5 why must fluid temperature be stated when specifying viscosity? show answer and method viscosity changes with temperature. try it yourself time equal volumes of water and syrup flowing down the same gentle slope. put a droplet of water on a clean and waxy surface. where this model stops flow time is not a direct viscosity measurement unless geometry and driving conditions are controlled. references fluid dynamics (2.06) — mit opencourseware, spring 2013. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← springs and oscillations buoyancy and floating objects → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/free-body.html":"forces, free-body diagrams and equilibrium — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate forces, free-body diagrams and equilibrium why must we account for every force before predicting motion? isolating a body from its surroundings a free-body diagram isolates one object and draws the external forces acting on it. it is a bookkeeping tool, not decoration. once forces are separated into directions, newton’s laws tell us whether the object is balanced or accelerating. a free-body diagram is a deliberate choice to study one object at a time. imagine a box sitting on a table. gravity pulls the box downward, and the table exerts an upward normal force. the box pushes the table down too, but that last force belongs to the table's diagram, not the box's. this separation is especially important for assemblies. a mounting screw, a bracket and a payload interact through contact forces, but each component can be isolated and analysed. drawing all action–reaction partners on one diagram often makes every force seem to cancel and hides the actual loading of a part. drawing the forces and finding equilibrium for an object at rest, the sum of forces in each direction is zero. for a moving object, net force equals mass times acceleration. the normal reaction acts perpendicular to a surface; friction, when present, acts tangentially. σfₓ = maₓ; σfᵧ = maᵧ choose axes, identify external forces, split angled forces into components and write σfx=ma_x and σfy=ma_y. static equilibrium means both translational acceleration and angular acceleration are zero, so moments also need to balance when rotation is possible. a resting box has vertical force balance, but a long shelf may require torque balance to find its support reactions. if someone pushes horizontally on a 4 kg box with 12 n while friction acts backward with 4 n, the horizontal acceleration is (12−4)/4=2 m/s². the normal force remains mg on a horizontal surface if no other vertical forces act. a normal reaction is a contact force determined by constraints; it is not always equal to weight on a slope or during vertical acceleration. worked examples question. a 6 kg box rests on a level table, with no other vertical forces. find the normal reaction. use g = 9.81 m/s². solution, step by step. its weight is mg = 6 × 9.81 = 58.86 n downward. vertical acceleration is zero, so upward normal force n = 58.86 n. worked example 2 question. a 5 kg crate rests on a level floor. find the upward normal force (g=9.81 m/s²), assuming no other vertical forces. solution. vertical equilibrium: n−mg=0. therefore n=5×9.81=49.05 n. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a 2 kg block experiences 10 n east and 4 n west on a horizontal frictionless surface. find acceleration. show worked answer net force = 10 − 4 = 6 n east. a = f/m = 6/2 = 3 m/s² east. problem 2 of 5 two people pull equally in opposite directions with 100 n each. net force? show answer and method 0 n. problem 3 of 5 a hanging 2 kg mass is stationary. cable tension, g=10? show answer and method 20 n. problem 4 of 5 a free-body diagram of a sliding box should include velocity as a force arrow? show answer and method no. velocity is not a force. problem 5 of 5 what condition defines translational equilibrium? show answer and method vector sum of external forces is zero. try it yourself choose a book on a table and draw only forces acting on the book. repeat while you gently push it sideways. where this model stops do not include forces the chosen object exerts on its surroundings in its own free-body diagram. references classical mechanics (8.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2016. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← displacement, velocity and acceleration friction and drag → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/friction-drag.html":"friction and drag — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate friction and drag why is it harder to start sliding a box than to keep it moving? why motion is resisted friction is a contact force opposing relative sliding or its tendency. static friction adjusts up to a maximum value; kinetic friction acts when surfaces slide. drag is resistance from a surrounding fluid and often varies with speed. when two solid surfaces slide against one another, microscopic contact interactions resist relative motion. static friction can adjust to match the applied tangential force up to a limiting value; kinetic friction applies during sliding and is often modelled approximately with a constant coefficient. neither coefficient tells the whole story about real materials. fluid drag is different. air and water flow around a moving body, producing pressure and viscous forces that typically oppose its motion relative to the fluid. cyclists feel drag rise with speed; engineers try to reduce it through smoother shapes, while parachutes deliberately increase it. frictional forces and fluid drag in a simple dry-friction model, maximum static friction equals μₛn and kinetic friction roughly equals μₖn. a common high-speed drag approximation uses density, speed squared, projected area and a drag coefficient. fₛ,max = μₛn; fₖ ≈ μₖn; d ≈ ½ρcᴅav² a common dry-friction model uses |f_s|≤μ_sn for static contact and f_k≈μ_kn during sliding. for a crate on a horizontal floor, n≈mg when there are no other vertical loads. the static inequality matters: friction is not automatically μ_sn before slipping begins. for many objects moving fast enough through air, drag can be approximated by f_d=½ρc_dav², where ρ is fluid density, a a reference area and c_d a shape-dependent coefficient. the quadratic law is not universal; very slow viscous flows can behave differently. if speed doubles while other factors remain the same in the quadratic regime, drag increases fourfold. aerodynamic coefficient values depend on geometry and flow conditions and often need testing. worked examples question. a 10 kg crate has μₛ = 0.40 on a horizontal floor. estimate the smallest horizontal push needed to start sliding. solution, step by step. normal force n = mg = 10 × 9.81 = 98.1 n. maximum static friction = 0.40 × 98.1 = 39.24 n. the applied push must just exceed about 39.2 n. worked example 2 question. a 10 kg box rests on horizontal floor with μk=0.3; find kinetic friction while sliding (g=10 m/s²). solution. normal force n=mg=100 n. kinetic friction =μkn=0.3×100=30 n opposite relative motion. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a 5 kg block has μₖ = 0.20 on a level floor. approximate kinetic friction. show worked answer fₖ = μₖmg = 0.20 × 5 × 9.81 ≈ 9.81 n. problem 2 of 5 if μk=0.2 and n=50 n, kinetic friction? show answer and method 10 n. problem 3 of 5 why does air drag usually increase with speed? show answer and method faster relative flow generally increases momentum transfer and resistive force. problem 4 of 5 can static friction be zero? show answer and method yes, if no force tends to cause slip. problem 5 of 5 does the basic law fk=μkn give the direction of friction by itself? show answer and method no; friction opposes relative sliding direction. try it yourself slide a small object over paper, fabric and a smooth desk. predict which surface creates more resistance before testing. where this model stops friction coefficients vary with material, loading and conditions; quadratic drag is not appropriate for every flow regime. references classical mechanics (8.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2016. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← forces, free-body diagrams and equilibrium momentum and collisions → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/functions-graphs.html":"functions and graphs — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate functions and graphs what can a graph tell you about a moving lift? a graph tells a story about a relationship a function is a rule pairing each input with one output. a graph turns that rule into a picture: the horizontal coordinate is the input, and the vertical coordinate is the result. the shape often conveys more than a table of numbers. a function is a rule that takes an allowed input and assigns exactly one output. in practical work, the input might be elapsed time and the output could be the temperature of a cooling component. a graph lets us see a whole relationship, including trends that would be hard to recognise in a table of numbers. different graphs answer different questions. a straight line indicates a constant rate of change; a curved line suggests the rate itself changes. a sudden jump might represent switching a heater on, but it might also mean the mathematical model is missing intermediate behaviour. always check what the horizontal and vertical axes actually measure before interpreting the shape. inputs, outputs and functions for a lift rising steadily, height can be modelled by h(t) = 1.5t + 2, with t in seconds and h in metres. the intercept 2 is the starting height; the slope 1.5 is metres gained per second. a curved graph instead suggests changing rate. h(t) = 1.5t + 2 the domain specifies allowed inputs and the range specifies resulting outputs. a reciprocal function such as y=1/x excludes x=0; a square-root model y=sqrt(x) requires x≥0 in the real numbers. real-world restrictions can be even tighter: a negative pipe diameter is not physically meaningful even if some formula accepts it. think about a function as a model rather than a collection of plotted points. the slope tells you a rate, the intercept gives a reference value, and the area may represent an accumulated quantity. two different formulae can fit a small experimental dataset equally well but make different predictions outside the measured range. this is why interpolation within data and extrapolation beyond it are not equally reliable. worked examples question. where is the lift after 6 seconds, and when does it reach 17 metres? solution, step by step. insert t = 6: h(6) = 1.5 × 6 + 2 = 11 m. for 17 m, solve 17 = 1.5t + 2. subtract 2 and divide by 1.5: t = 10 s. worked example 2 question. a taxi costs ₹60 plus ₹15 per kilometre. write the fare function and find the price at 8 km. solution. let d be distance. c(d) = 60 + 15d. at d = 8, c = 60 + 120 = ₹180. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. for d(t) = 4t + 1, find d(3) and the time when d = 25. show worked answer d(3) = 13; 25 = 4t + 1 gives t = 6. problem 2 of 5 for f(x) = 2x + 1, find f(−3). show answer and method −5. problem 3 of 5 what is the slope of y = −4x + 7? show answer and method −4. problem 4 of 5 find the x-intercept of y = 3x − 12. show answer and method set y = 0: x = 4. problem 5 of 5 can one input produce two outputs in a mathematical function? show answer and method no: each input in the domain has exactly one output. try it yourself make a two-column time–height table, then plot the points on squared paper. what would a horizontal line mean physically? where this model stops check the units on both axes; a graph is not meaningful without knowing what each axis represents. references single variable calculus (18.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2010. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← integrals — adding up change limits and continuity → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/gas-laws.html":"gases and gas laws — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate gases and gas laws why does a sealed balloon grow when warmed? why gases change volume and pressure gas pressure comes from particle collisions with surrounding surfaces. volume, temperature, pressure and amount of gas are connected. temperature must be measured on an absolute scale for the ideal-gas relation. a gas is compressible because its particles occupy only a small fraction of the space available to them in ordinary conditions. heating a gas increases typical molecular kinetic energies; holding the volume fixed then tends to raise pressure. allowing the gas to expand instead can change volume and temperature together. balloon demonstrations show the effects dramatically, but the physical system matters. a sealed rigid can, a flexible balloon and an open container respond differently to heating because their constraints differ. before choosing a gas law, identify what stays fixed. pressure, temperature and the gas laws for an ideal gas, pv = nrt. at constant pressure and amount, v is proportional to temperature in kelvin. at constant volume, pressure is proportional to temperature. these relations approximate many gases under ordinary conditions. pv = nrt; t(k) = t(°c) + 273.15 the ideal-gas law pv=nrt links absolute pressure, volume, amount of gas and absolute temperature. if a fixed amount of ideal gas stays at constant temperature, pv remains constant; if volume stays constant instead, pressure is proportional to kelvin temperature. celsius must not be used directly in these proportional relationships. ideal-gas behaviour is approximate because real molecules have finite size and interact. it works reasonably well for many dilute gases, but deviations grow at high pressure or near condensation. in engineering, pressure quoted as gauge must be converted to absolute pressure before entering the ideal-gas equation. worked examples question. a balloon has volume 2.0 l at 300 k. if pressure and amount stay constant, what is its volume at 330 k? solution, step by step. v₂ = v₁t₂/t₁ = 2.0 × 330/300 = 2.2 l. the increase follows the ratio of absolute temperatures. worked example 2 question. a fixed gas occupies 2 l at 100 kpa. at same temperature it is compressed to 1 l. find pressure. solution. boyle’s law p₁v₁=p₂v₂ gives p₂=100×2/1=200 kpa. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a sealed rigid container has pressure 100 kpa at 300 k. estimate pressure at 360 k. show worked answer p₂ = 100 × 360/300 = 120 kpa. problem 2 of 5 at constant temperature, pressure doubles. volume becomes…? show answer and method half, for fixed gas amount. problem 3 of 5 convert 27°c approximately to kelvin. show answer and method 300.15 k. problem 4 of 5 at constant pressure, raising absolute temperature from 300 to 600 k changes ideal-gas volume by what factor? show answer and method it doubles. problem 5 of 5 what is the ideal-gas equation? show answer and method pv=nrt. try it yourself watch a small, untied balloon change size in safely warm and cool water. avoid heating a sealed rigid vessel. where this model stops real gases depart from ideal behaviour at high pressure or near condensation; never insert celsius directly into gas-law ratios. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← moles and chemical calculations solutions and concentrations → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/heat-transfer.html":"heat transfer — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate heat transfer why does a metal spoon get hot in soup before its handle is immersed? how heat moves from one place to another heat transfer is energy moving because of a temperature difference. conduction passes energy through matter, convection transports energy between a surface and moving fluid, and radiation transfers energy by electromagnetic waves. in real situations these often occur together. heat moves because of temperature differences, but the path matters. a metal spoon in hot tea warms through conduction; a fan changes convection around a warm object; sunlight travels through space by radiation. in real systems, these mechanisms often operate together rather than one at a time. think about insulating a house or cooling electronic equipment. thick insulation reduces conduction through walls, yet ventilation can still exchange heat through moving air. a shiny surface may have different radiative behaviour from a dull one. understanding the dominant mechanism helps choose an effective design instead of guessing. conduction, convection and radiation steady conduction across a flat wall is proportional to conductivity k, area a and temperature difference, and inversely proportional to thickness l. convection is often modelled using coefficient h. q̇conduction = kaδt/l; q̇convection = haδt one-dimensional steady conduction through a plane slab can be approximated by q̇=kaδt/l, with conductivity k, area a and thickness l. convection often uses q̇=ha(t_surface−t_fluid), while thermal radiation depends on emissivity and the fourth power of absolute temperature. thermal resistances can be arranged like an electrical circuit for simple steady heat-flow paths. for series layers, the same heat rate passes through each layer while temperature drops add. this analogy is powerful but requires care: convection coefficients vary with flow conditions, temperature properties may change, and transient heating involves stored thermal energy. a heat-transfer coefficient is not a material constant. worked examples question. a wall has k = 0.8 w/(m·k), area 2 m², thickness 0.1 m and δt = 15 k. find approximate steady conduction rate. solution, step by step. q̇ = 0.8 × 2 × 15 / 0.1 = 240 w. a thicker wall of the same material would reduce the rate. worked example 2 question. a wall has k=1 w/(m·k), area 2 m², thickness 0.1 m and 10 k temperature difference. find steady conduction rate. solution. fourier one-dimensional magnitude: q̇=kaδt/l=1×2×10/0.1=200 w. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a surface transfers heat with h = 10 w/(m²·k), area 0.5 m² and δt = 20 k. estimate convective rate. show worked answer q̇ = 10 × 0.5 × 20 = 100 w. problem 2 of 5 how much heat conducts through the wall above if δt doubles? show answer and method 400 w, under constant properties and geometry. problem 3 of 5 what is the si unit of thermal conductivity? show answer and method w/(m·k). problem 4 of 5 can heat travel through a vacuum? show answer and method yes, by thermal radiation. problem 5 of 5 which heat transfer mechanism involves bulk fluid motion? show answer and method convection. try it yourself compare a metal spoon and wooden spoon in warm—not scalding—water. explain why apparent coldness or warmth at touch differs. where this model stops these are simplified steady models with prescribed properties; temperature can vary through time and space. references introduction to heat transfer (2.051) — mit opencourseware, fall 2015. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← pumps and fluid systems introduction to thermodynamics → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/integrals.html":"integrals: adding up change — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate integrals: adding up change what happens when we add up many tiny changes? explore the area under a graph and recover a total from a rate. one number from many small pieces suppose a cyclist’s velocity increases over time. the speed at any instant tells us how fast they are moving, but how far have they travelled during a few seconds? we can split the time into small slices: velocity × duration is approximately the distance in each slice. add all the slices, make them thinner and thinner, and the estimate approaches a definite integral . on a velocity–time graph, it is the signed area between the curve and the time axis. for positive velocity throughout our example, this area is also distance travelled. imagine filling a tank at a changing rate. knowing the instantaneous inflow in litres per second tells you what is happening now, not how much water has entered altogether. the integral answers the accumulation question by adding many small contributions over time. each contribution can be approximated by flow rate multiplied by a short time interval. this is closely related to finding area under a curve, but the interpretation is broader. area under a speed–time graph gives distance travelled when speed is nonnegative; signed area under a velocity–time graph gives displacement. area under a force–displacement curve gives mechanical work. the axes and their units decide what the integral means. the simplest area to accumulate consider an idealised object with velocity v(t) = 2t + 1 m/s , where t is measured in seconds. the velocity starts at 1 m/s and increases by 2 m/s every second. displacement from 0 to 3 s = ∫₀³ (2t + 1) dt = [t² + t]₀³ = 12 m this is an example of the fundamental theorem of calculus : integrating a rate recovers the accumulated change. notice how it connects to our previous lesson on derivatives: the derivative of t² + t is 2t + 1 . divide the interval into narrow pieces, approximate the function in each piece and add rectangle areas. making the pieces narrower generally improves the approximation for a well-behaved function. the definite integral is the limiting accumulated total, not a picture that must be geometrically positive: portions below the axis contribute negative signed area. an antiderivative offers a powerful shortcut. the fundamental theorem of calculus connects the accumulation from a to b with f(b)−f(a), where f is a function whose derivative is f. for example, ∫ from 0 to 3 of 2t dt equals [t²] from 0 to 3 = 9. if the integrand is velocity in m/s, the answer is displacement in metres. always distinguish an indefinite integral (a family of antiderivatives) from a definite integral (a numerical accumulation). build an integral with rectangles move the slider to increase the number of narrow rectangles. their combined area approaches the exact area under the line. the rectangles use the left end of each interval, so they underestimate this increasing function. area under v(t) = 2t + 1 rectangles 6 velocity (m/s) time (s) shaded rectangle area approximates displacement over 0–3 seconds. 6 rectangles: approximate displacement 10.50 m (exact: 12.00 m). worked examples problem: water flows into a tank at q(t) = 3 + 2t litres/minute. how much enters in the first 4 minutes? find the accumulated volume: v = ∫₀⁴ (3 + 2t) dt. integrate: v = [3t + t²]₀⁴. evaluate: v = 12 + 16 = 28 litres . the same mathematics handles distance, accumulated rainfall, electric charge and many other quantities. worked example 2 question. a tank fills at q(t) = 2t l/min. how much water enters between 1 and 4 minutes? solution. integrate: ∫₁⁴ 2t dt = [t²]₁⁴ = 16 − 1 = 15 l. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: a cart moves with velocity v(t) = 4 + t m/s from t = 0 to 5 s. find its displacement. show worked answer ∫₀⁵ (4 + t) dt = [4t + t²/2]₀⁵ = 20 + 12.5 = 32.5 m . since v is positive throughout, that is also the distance travelled. problem 2 of 5 evaluate ∫₀³ 4 dx. show answer and method 4 × 3 = 12. problem 3 of 5 find an antiderivative of 6x. show answer and method 3x² + c. problem 4 of 5 a constant flow of 5 l/min lasts 12 min. how much passes? show answer and method area under flow curve = 5 × 12 = 60 l. problem 5 of 5 can a definite integral be negative? show answer and method yes. for example ∫₀² −3 dx = −6. try it yourself sketch a velocity–time graph for a toy car that moves steadily for 2 seconds, then moves twice as fast for 2 seconds. divide the graph into rectangles. can you calculate total displacement without using a calculus formula? extension: if velocity goes below zero, positive and negative areas cancel when you calculate displacement; for total distance, add their absolute magnitudes. references mit ocw 18.01sc: definite integrals, session 43 mit ocw 18.01sc: the fundamental theorem, session 48 explanations, diagrams, examples and interactive demonstrations are original. mit opencourseware is credited as a topic reference; no mit figures or passages have been reproduced. mit ocw licensing and terms . ← back to courses free, independent learning material. not affiliated with mit.","lessons/integration-applications.html":"applications of integration — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate applications of integration why can accumulated distance be read from a velocity–time graph? what can a sum of tiny pieces tell us? an integral adds many small contributions. if speed changes, multiplying one speed by a whole time interval may be misleading. instead, divide time into narrow intervals and add speed × interval width. an integral can sum any quantity that is naturally built from small contributions. a water tank with changing cross-sectional area fills at different rates as its level rises; a nonuniform rod can have different mass along its length. rather than forcing a single average, integration handles those changes systematically. the important first step is often deciding what the small piece represents. for mass, use a small length times linear density or a small volume times volumetric density. for work, use a small displacement times the force acting during that displacement. drawing the situation and checking units frequently matters more than memorising the final integral. using integrals to measure accumulated change for velocity v(t) = 2t (m/s) from t = 0 to 4 s, displacement equals the area under the graph. integration is an exact limit of the rectangle-addition idea. the same method calculates work from a variable force and area under a curve. displacement = ∫₀⁴ 2t dt = [t²]₀⁴ suppose a force varies with position as f(x)=3x newtons, with x in metres. the work done from x=0 to x=2 m is ∫₀²3x dx = [1.5x²]₀²=6 j. using the final force 6 n over the full distance would give 12 j and overestimate the work because the force was smaller earlier. integration can also find the volume of a solid by stacking thin slices: volume equals the sum of cross-sectional area a(x) times thickness dx. in a physical problem, make sure every contribution is counted exactly once and that the integration limits correspond to the actual system. when analytical integration is inconvenient, numerical methods approximate the same accumulation. worked examples question. a cart has v(t) = 2t m/s. how far does it move from 0 to 4 seconds? solution, step by step. antiderivative of 2t is t². evaluate at the bounds: 4² − 0² = 16 m. units confirm the result: (m/s) × s = m. worked example 2 question. a spring requires f(x)=100x n to stretch x metres. find work from 0 to 0.2 m. solution. work = ∫₀^0.2 100x dx = [50x²]₀^0.2 = 2 j. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. for velocity v(t) = 3t² m/s, find displacement from t = 0 to 2 s. show worked answer ∫₀² 3t² dt = [t³]₀² = 8 m. problem 2 of 5 find area under y=3 from x=0 to x=5. show answer and method 15 square units. problem 3 of 5 find signed area ∫₀² x dx. show answer and method [x²/2]₀² = 2. problem 4 of 5 a cart moves at v=3 m/s for 4 s. distance? show answer and method ∫ v dt = 12 m. problem 5 of 5 does integrating velocity always give distance travelled? show answer and method no; integral gives signed displacement. integrate speed |v| to get distance. try it yourself approximate the triangular area below v = 2t from 0 to 4 using four wide rectangles and then eight narrower ones. compare with 16 m. where this model stops signed area gives displacement, not always total distance: negative velocity contributes negative displacement. references single variable calculus (18.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2010. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← optimisation using calculus introduction to differential equations → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/launch-loads.html":"launch loads and vibrations — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate launch loads and vibrations what happens to delicate instruments when a launch vehicle vibrates? what a spacecraft experiences at launch launch causes sustained accelerations, acoustic excitation and transient shocks. structures must carry these loads without excessive stress, deflection or harmful resonance. a basic acceleration calculation is often the first sizing check. a satellite launched inside a rocket experiences more than simple upward acceleration. during ascent it sees changing axial and lateral loads, aerodynamic forcing, acoustic energy, stage events and vibration transmitted through its mounting structure. later, deployment devices may introduce their own short shocks. this matters for delicate equipment and fastened joints. a component might survive the average acceleration but fail because a structural natural frequency lies near a strong vibration input. mechanical design must consider how loads travel through the entire assembly, not only the mass of the payload. acceleration, vibration and structural loads an instrument subject to acceleration a imposes an inertial load ma on its mounting. structural dynamics adds stiffness, damping and natural frequencies; a system excited near resonance can respond more strongly than a static model predicts. f_inertial = ma; f_n = (1/2π)√(k/m) for an ideal spring–mass model a simple inertial load estimate uses f=ma, but actual launch qualification often requires multiple static-equivalent load cases, random vibration spectra, acoustic exposure and shock environments specified by the launcher. modal analysis identifies natural frequencies and shapes; forced-response analysis estimates response to excitation. if a 10 kg payload experiences a net specified acceleration of 6g along one axis, its inertial loading magnitude is about 10×6×9.81≈589 n. that arithmetic does not locate stress hotspots or cover dynamic amplification. mounting stiffness, preload, interfaces, test fixtures and acceptance criteria all need analysis and verification. worked examples question. a 4 kg payload experiences an axial acceleration of 6g. find the corresponding inertial-force magnitude. solution, step by step. a = 6 × 9.81 = 58.86 m/s². force magnitude f = 4 × 58.86 = 235.44 n. a real design must consider load directions and margins. worked example 2 question. a 5 kg component experiences axial acceleration 8g with g=9.81 m/s². find inertial force magnitude in a static-equivalent estimate. solution. f=ma=5×8×9.81≈392.4 n. load direction and mount geometry must also be checked. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a 2 kg unit sees 5g acceleration. estimate inertial load. show worked answer f = 2 × 5 × 9.81 = 98.1 n. problem 2 of 5 a structure vibrates at 50 hz. what is the period of one oscillation? show answer and method t = 1/f = 1/50 = 0.020 s, or 20 ms. problem 3 of 5 why is resonance important for launch structures? show answer and method excitation near a natural frequency can magnify dynamic response. problem 4 of 5 what units describe vibration frequency? show answer and method hertz (hz). problem 5 of 5 what test is used to assess survival of launch vibration? show answer and method commonly sine and random-vibration testing to mission-specific levels. try it yourself set a small object on a flexible ruler and tap lightly. what changes its vibration frequency: more mass or a stiffer ruler? where this model stops do not treat a launch vibration environment as equivalent to a single static 6g load; dynamic qualification needs spectra and test data. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← rocket stability and aerodynamics materials for rockets and spacecraft → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/limits-continuity.html":"limits and continuity — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate limits and continuity how do we describe a value we are approaching but may never reach? what happens when we get arbitrarily close? a limit concerns the behaviour near a point, not necessarily the value at the point. continuity means the limiting value agrees with the actual function value, so the graph can be traced locally without a jump or hole. a limit asks what value a function approaches as its input gets close to a chosen point. it need not ask for the value exactly at that point. this distinction matters when a formula contains a division by zero at one point but behaves perfectly predictably nearby. the limit captures that nearby behaviour. picture driving toward a junction: knowing your distance from the junction as you get closer is a different question from whether you actually enter it. similarly, a graph can approach one height without having a plotted point at that height. a discontinuity can look like a hole, a sudden step, or an unbounded rise. these shapes represent different mathematical situations. limits and continuity take f(x) = (x² − 4)/(x − 2). it is undefined at x = 2, yet everywhere else it equals x + 2. values on both sides of 2 approach 4. this is a removable hole, not a mysterious infinity. lim(x→2) (x² − 4)/(x − 2) = 4 a function is continuous at x=a when three requirements hold together: f(a) exists, the limit as x approaches a exists, and the limit equals f(a). both left-hand and right-hand approaches must agree for a two-sided limit. for example, (x²−1)/(x−1) equals x+1 whenever x≠1. the original expression is undefined at x=1, but its limit there is 2. limits form the foundation of derivatives and integrals. the slope of a secant line approaches a tangent slope through a limit; increasingly fine sums approach an integral through a limit. a limit may fail to exist if the left and right behaviours disagree or if the function oscillates without approaching one number. worked examples question. find the limit as x approaches 3 of (x² − 9)/(x − 3). solution, step by step. factor the numerator: (x − 3)(x + 3). for x different from 3, cancel the common factor to get x + 3. the approaching value is 6. worked example 2 question. find lim(x→2) (x²−4)/(x−2). solution. factor numerator: (x−2)(x+2)/(x−2) = x+2 for x≠2. limit = 4. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. what is lim(x→5) (x² − 25)/(x − 5)? is the original formula defined at x = 5? show worked answer the limit is 10; the original expression is undefined at x = 5 because of division by zero. problem 2 of 5 evaluate lim(x→3) (2x+1). show answer and method 7. problem 3 of 5 what is lim(x→0) x²? show answer and method 0. problem 4 of 5 is f(x)=1/x continuous at x=0? show answer and method no; it is not defined at x=0. problem 5 of 5 can a function have a limit at a point where it is undefined? show answer and method yes: (x²−1)/(x−1) has limit 2 at x=1 despite being undefined there. try it yourself evaluate the function close to the missing point, from both sides, such as 1.9 and 2.1. compare the trend with the factored expression. where this model stops cancelling a factor identifies the nearby behaviour, but it does not make the original function defined at its hole. references single variable calculus (18.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2010. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← functions and graphs vector components and resultants → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/matrices.html":"matrices and linear equations — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate matrices and linear equations how can several equations be solved as one organised problem? organising many equations at once a matrix stores coefficients in rows and columns. this is useful whenever several unknowns influence several observations, such as balancing structural forces or combining data measurements. matrix operations preserve patterns that become hard to see in long equations. a matrix is a structured way to keep track of many related numbers. imagine three unknown currents in an electrical circuit or three unknown forces in an equilibrium problem. rather than writing and rearranging each equation separately, we can record their coefficients together and apply systematic operations. matrices are also transformation tools. a rotation takes a coordinate pair and gives another pair; a matrix describes the rule in one compact object. that is the connection to cad, graphics, robotics and machine learning: many quantities can be transformed or compared together without losing their relationships. representing linear systems with matrices two equations x + y = 5 and 2x − y = 1 can be represented by a coefficient matrix a, unknown vector x and outcome vector b. the compact notation ax = b is not a new law; it is another way to write the simultaneous equations. a = [[1,1],[2,−1]]; ax = [5,1]ᵀ for two linear equations, 2x+y=5 and x−y=1, write a=[[2,1],[1,−1]], the unknown vector u=[x,y] and the right-hand side b=[5,1]. the equation au=b encodes both equations at once. adding equations or performing row operations lets us solve for x=2 and y=1. the determinant offers clues about whether a square system has a unique solution, but it is not a universal test for every rectangular system. two equations that describe the same line do not uniquely determine two variables. in practical computation, solving au=b is usually preferable to explicitly calculating an inverse; numerical methods can be more stable and efficient. worked examples question. solve x + y = 5 and 2x − y = 1. solution, step by step. add the equations to eliminate y: 3x = 6, so x = 2. substitute back: y = 5 − 2 = 3. the matrix represents the same steps systematically. worked example 2 question. solve x+y=5 and x−y=1 using elimination. solution. add equations to get 2x=6 so x=3. substitute into x+y=5 gives y=2. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. solve x + y = 7 and x − y = 1. show worked answer adding gives 2x = 8, so x = 4 and y = 3. problem 2 of 5 add matrices [[1,2],[3,4]] and [[2,0],[−1,5]]. show answer and method [[3,2],[2,9]]. problem 3 of 5 multiply [[1,2],[0,1]] by vector [3,4]. show answer and method [11,4]. problem 4 of 5 find det([[2,1],[3,4]]). show answer and method 2×4−1×3=5. problem 5 of 5 when does a square matrix lack an inverse? show answer and method when its determinant is zero. try it yourself arrange measured costs of two parts in a 2×2 table; explore what happens to your equations if the rows become identical. where this model stops if equations duplicate one another, the coefficient matrix is singular and a unique solution may not exist. references linear algebra (18.06sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2011. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← introduction to differential equations coordinate geometry in 2d and 3d → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/molecular-shapes.html":"molecular shapes and polarity — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate molecular shapes and polarity why is water bent while carbon dioxide is linear? why molecules take particular shapes molecules have three-dimensional shapes because electron regions around an atom repel and arrange themselves. shape affects polarity, solubility and interactions. bond polarity and molecular shape together determine whether dipoles cancel. a molecule's arrangement in space matters just as much as its chemical formula. carbon dioxide and water both contain three atoms, yet carbon dioxide is linear while water is bent. their different shapes influence whether individual bond dipoles reinforce or cancel, which in turn affects intermolecular attraction. imagine a tug of war with equal pulls. if the pulls point in exact opposite directions, they cancel; if they meet at an angle, the resultant need not vanish. bond dipoles behave similarly as vectors. this is why deciding that a molecule is polar requires thinking about both bond polarity and geometry. electron pairs and molecular geometry for a simple vsepr model, count bonding regions and lone pairs around the central atom. co₂ has two main electron regions around carbon and is linear. water has two bonds and two lone-pair regions around oxygen, giving a bent molecular shape. molecular polarity depends on both bond dipoles and their spatial arrangement vsepr is a practical model: regions of electron density around a central atom tend to arrange to reduce repulsion. two regions often lead to a linear arrangement; three to trigonal planar; four to a tetrahedral electron-domain arrangement. lone pairs count as regions and can alter the visible arrangement of bonded atoms. for water, oxygen has two o–h bonding regions and two lone-pair regions. its electron-domain geometry is approximately tetrahedral, while the molecular shape is bent. the bond dipoles do not cancel, so water has a net dipole. vsepr is a qualitative method for many main-group molecules, not a substitute for advanced bonding models in every molecule or transition-metal complex. worked examples question. compare co₂ and h₂o. which has a net molecular dipole? solution, step by step. co₂ has two opposite c=o bond dipoles arranged linearly, so they cancel. water is bent, so its o–h bond dipoles do not cancel; water is polar. worked example 2 question. why is water h₂o bent rather than linear in the common vsepr picture? solution. oxygen has two bonding pairs and two lone pairs. four electron regions are tetrahedrally arranged; lone-pair repulsions produce a bent molecular shape (≈104.5° bond angle). practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a simple molecule has three identical bonds symmetrically arranged 120° apart in one plane and no lone pair. would its three equal bond dipoles sum to zero? show worked answer yes; symmetry makes the vector sum of the three equal dipoles zero. problem 2 of 5 what is the approximate molecular shape of co₂? show answer and method linear. problem 3 of 5 is a symmetrical linear co₂ molecule polar overall? show answer and method no; bond dipoles cancel. problem 4 of 5 what is the common vsepr shape of methane ch₄? show answer and method tetrahedral. problem 5 of 5 what is an electronegativity difference often associated with? show answer and method unequal sharing of bonding electrons and bond polarity. try it yourself build a simple ball-and-stick shape using paper or reusable modelling putty. rotate it to see why 2d sketches can mislead. where this model stops vsepr predicts many simple main-group shapes, but transition-metal complexes and unusual bonding may need more advanced models. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← electrons and atomic orbitals states of matter and phase changes → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/moles.html":"moles and chemical calculations — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate moles and chemical calculations how do chemists count enormous numbers of atoms using an ordinary balance? counting particles without counting individually the mole is a counting unit, analogous to a dozen but vastly larger. one mole contains exactly 6.02214076 × 10²³ specified entities. molar mass connects that microscopic count to mass measured in the laboratory. a mole is a convenient counting unit for unimaginably large numbers of particles. just as a dozen means twelve objects, one mole means exactly 6.02214076×10²³ specified entities. we can weigh a sample rather than attempting to count its molecules one by one, because molar mass links mass and amount of substance. this matters whenever reactants are mixed. chemical reactions occur through particle-number ratios, while a laboratory balance gives mass. converting between grams and moles connects the world of measured materials to the balanced chemical equation. moles, mass and proportions for a pure compound, amount in moles equals mass divided by molar mass. use the chemical formula to calculate molar mass from atomic masses, then scale the reaction equation in mole ratios. n = m/m; n = nnₐ the relationship n=m/m uses amount n in moles, mass m and molar mass m in compatible units. water has molar mass about 18.015 g/mol, so 36.03 g of water is approximately 2.00 mol. that represents about 1.20×10²⁴ water molecules. in stoichiometry, convert each available reactant to moles, use coefficients from the balanced equation, identify any limiting reactant, and then calculate the predicted product. a large mass does not necessarily mean a large number of moles: molar mass differs by substance. the actual yield may be lower because reactions can be incomplete or competing processes may occur. worked examples question. how many moles are in 36 g of water? use m(h₂o) ≈ 18 g/mol. solution, step by step. n = m/m = 36/18 = 2 mol. this corresponds to about 2 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 1.2044 × 10²⁴ water molecules. worked example 2 question. how many moles are in 18 g water if m(h₂o)=18 g/mol? roughly how many molecules? solution. n=m/m=18/18=1 mol. number ≈6.022×10²³ molecules. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. how many moles are in 44 g of co₂, taking molar mass as 44 g/mol? show worked answer n = 44/44 = 1 mol. problem 2 of 5 find the mass of 0.25 mol of co₂ (molar mass 44 g/mol). show answer and method m = nm = 0.25 × 44 = 11 g. problem 3 of 5 mass of 0.5 mol nacl at 58.5 g/mol? show answer and method 29.25 g. problem 4 of 5 molecules in 2 mol using avogadro constant 6.022×10²³/mol? show answer and method 1.2044×10²⁴ molecules. problem 5 of 5 is a mole a measure of mass or amount of substance? show answer and method amount of substance. try it yourself weigh a safe household substance and estimate its amount of substance from a known chemical formula, noting impurities. where this model stops moles count specified particles; say whether you mean molecules, atoms, ions or formula units. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← chemical reactions and balancing equations gases and gas laws → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/momentum-collisions.html":"momentum and collisions — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate momentum and collisions why can a short collision produce a very large force? what changes during a collision? momentum combines mass with velocity. in a collision, objects exert forces on each other over a brief interval, exchanging momentum. if external impulse is negligible, total system momentum stays constant, even though individual velocities change. a moving object can be difficult to stop not only because it moves quickly but also because it has mass. momentum combines these effects as mass times velocity. to change momentum, an interaction must exert a force over time: the accumulated effect is impulse. a helmet or airbag does not magically remove the momentum that must change during impact. it increases the time and distance over which the person slows down, which can reduce the peak force. this makes momentum a particularly useful way of thinking about collisions where forces rise and fall very quickly. momentum conservation and impulse impulse is force integrated over time; for roughly constant force, fδt = δp. spreading a momentum change over more time reduces the average force. this principle explains cushioning and crumple zones. p = mv; j = δp = favg δt for a constant-mass particle, momentum p=mv and impulse j=∫f dt=δp. in a closed system with negligible net external impulse, total vector momentum stays constant. consider two skaters initially at rest who push apart: their momenta must be equal in magnitude and opposite in direction even if one skater moves faster. kinetic energy is not always conserved in a collision. in an inelastic collision, some becomes deformation, heat and sound, while momentum of the isolated system still balances. in a perfectly inelastic collision the bodies stick together. conservation statements depend on the chosen system and on how much external impulse acts over the collision interval. worked examples question. a 0.20 kg ball moving at 10 m/s stops over 0.050 s. find average force magnitude. solution, step by step. initial momentum = 0.20 × 10 = 2 kg·m/s. final momentum = 0. change magnitude is 2 n·s. average force magnitude = 2/0.050 = 40 n, opposing the motion. worked example 2 question. a 2 kg cart at 3 m/s sticks to a stationary 1 kg cart. find common speed, ignoring external impulses. solution. momentum before =2×3+1×0=6 kg·m/s. combined mass=3 kg. speed after=6/3=2 m/s. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a 1 kg cart at 3 m/s joins a stationary 2 kg cart on a low-friction track. find their shared speed. show worked answer momentum before = 1 × 3 = 3 kg·m/s. total mass after = 3 kg. final speed = 3/3 = 1 m/s. problem 2 of 5 momentum of 5 kg moving at 4 m/s? show answer and method 20 kg·m/s. problem 3 of 5 impulse from a 10 n constant force for 0.5 s? show answer and method 5 n·s. problem 4 of 5 when is total momentum conserved? show answer and method for a system with zero net external impulse. problem 5 of 5 is kinetic energy always conserved in collisions? show answer and method no; it is conserved only in ideal elastic collisions. try it yourself roll two small toy carts together and compare a springy impact with one where they stick. which quantity is always conserved approximately? where this model stops momentum conservation requires an approximately isolated system; kinetic energy is not always conserved in collisions. references classical mechanics (8.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2016. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← friction and drag circular motion and gravitation → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/motion-kinematics.html":"displacement, velocity and acceleration — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate displacement, velocity and acceleration how do you describe a moving object without yet asking what caused the motion? describing how something moves displacement is the change in position and has a direction. distance is the length of the journey. average velocity is displacement divided by time; speed uses distance. acceleration measures how quickly velocity changes. kinematics describes motion without first asking what forces caused it. position tells us where something is relative to an origin. displacement is the change in position, whereas distance is the total path length travelled. velocity adds direction to speed, and acceleration describes how velocity changes. a car rounding a bend at constant speed still accelerates because its direction changes. conversely, a car travelling east and slowing down has an acceleration pointing west. that is why motion is best described with vectors and graphs rather than with the word “fast” alone. position, velocity and acceleration when acceleration is constant, a few useful equations relate initial velocity u, final velocity v, time t and displacement s. sketch the direction first, then choose positive and negative signs consistently. v = u + at; s = ut + ½at² average velocity equals displacement divided by elapsed time; instantaneous velocity is the derivative of position with respect to time. acceleration is the derivative of velocity. when acceleration is constant in one dimension, equations such as v=u+at and s=ut+½at² follow by integrating these rates. if a vehicle begins from rest and accelerates at 2 m/s² for 5 s, v=10 m/s and displacement is 25 m. these results do not apply unchanged if acceleration varies with time. on a position–time graph, slope is velocity; on a velocity–time graph, slope is acceleration and signed area is displacement. keeping these graphical interpretations connected prevents formula memorisation from replacing understanding. worked examples question. a bicycle starts at 2 m/s and accelerates steadily at 1.5 m/s² for 4 s. find speed and displacement. solution, step by step. v = 2 + 1.5 × 4 = 8 m/s. s = 2 × 4 + ½ × 1.5 × 4² = 8 + 12 = 20 m. worked example 2 question. a cyclist starts from rest and accelerates at 2 m/s² for 6 s. find final speed and distance. solution. v=u+at=0+2×6=12 m/s. s=ut+½at²=0+0.5×2×36=36 m. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a cart starts at rest and accelerates at 2 m/s² for 3 s. how far does it travel? show worked answer s = 0 + ½ × 2 × 3² = 9 m. its final velocity is 6 m/s. problem 2 of 5 travel 100 m in 20 s at steady speed. speed? show answer and method 5 m/s. problem 3 of 5 at constant 3 m/s² from u=4 m/s for 2 s, final velocity? show answer and method 10 m/s. problem 4 of 5 an object moves 6 m east then 2 m west. displacement? show answer and method 4 m east. problem 5 of 5 can speed stay constant while acceleration is nonzero? show answer and method yes, e.g. uniform circular motion changes velocity direction. try it yourself film a ball rolling down a gentle ramp. use video timestamps and ruler marks to estimate positions, then compare successive average speeds. where this model stops these equations require constant acceleration. do not assume constant acceleration if the forcing or resistance changes. references classical mechanics (8.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2016. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← work and conservation of energy forces, free-body diagrams and equilibrium → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/newtons-laws.html":"newton's laws of motion — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate newton's laws of motion understand why objects speed up, slow down, or keep moving—and solve a force problem step by step. three laws, one way of thinking first law: an object maintains its velocity unless an external net force acts on it. being in motion does not require a force; changing motion does. second law: a net force produces acceleration. the same net force gives a smaller acceleration to a larger mass. σf = ma (in an inertial reference frame) third law: when one object pushes another, the second pushes back with an equal and opposite force. these forces act on different objects , so they do not cancel when analysing a single object. newton’s laws describe how motion changes when bodies interact. a force is not required to keep an object moving at constant velocity in a straight line; it is required to change that velocity. this is often counterintuitive because on earth friction and drag continually slow objects unless something pushes them. the first law identifies inertia, the second law quantifies acceleration and the third law describes interaction pairs. think of a passenger in a braking bus: the passenger continues moving forward relative to the bus unless a seat belt or seat supplies a force. nothing has to “push” the passenger forward; their existing motion tends to continue. draw the forces first imagine pushing a cart. gravity acts downward, the floor's normal force acts upward, and your push and friction act horizontally. the cart's horizontal acceleration depends on the difference between those horizontal forces. if a 14 n push acts right and friction is 5 n left, the horizontal net force is 9 n right—not 14 n. newton’s second law for constant mass is the vector relationship σf=ma. it concerns the net external force on a chosen object, not one convenient applied force. if you push a 5 kg trolley with 20 n forward and friction supplies 5 n backward, the net horizontal force is 15 n and the acceleration is 3 m/s² forward. the third law is frequently misunderstood: equal and opposite forces act on different interacting bodies, so they do not cancel in one object's free-body diagram. the earth pulls a falling ball downward while the ball pulls the earth upward with an equal force; the accelerations differ because the masses differ enormously. state your object, coordinate directions and assumptions before applying the equations. explore force and acceleration change the net force and the mass net horizontal force (n) 10 cart mass (kg) 2 net force → m horizontal motion model arrow direction indicates the sign of the net horizontal force. diagram is schematic. acceleration = 5.00 m/s² to the right. worked examples problem: a 2 kg trolley is pushed with 12 n to the right while friction is 4 n to the left. find its acceleration. net horizontal force = 12 − 4 = 8 n to the right . use σf = ma. rearrange: a = σf/m. a = 8/2 = 4 m/s² to the right . the normal force and weight balance vertically because the cart has no vertical acceleration. worked example 2 question. a 4 kg trolley feels 30 n forward and 6 n resistance. find its acceleration. solution. net force = 30−6=24 n. by newton’s second law, a=24/4=6 m/s² forward. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: a 5 kg box experiences 25 n east and 10 n west. find its acceleration. show worked answer σf = 25 − 10 = 15 n east. a = 15/5 = 3 m/s² east . problem 2 of 5 a 3 kg object accelerates at 2 m/s². what is its net force? show answer and method f=ma=6 n. problem 3 of 5 what is acceleration of a 10 kg object with net force zero? show answer and method 0 m/s². problem 4 of 5 if equal opposing forces act on a stationary box, must it accelerate? show answer and method no: the net force is zero. problem 5 of 5 why does a person move forward when a bus brakes suddenly? show answer and method inertia: the body tends to keep its prior velocity until a force changes it. try it yourself roll a toy car on a smooth floor, then on a rough cloth. observe how quickly it slows. friction changes its velocity even after your hand stops pushing. think further: when you push a wall, why do you feel it pushing back? use newton's third law to name the two interacting objects. references mit ocw 8.01sc — classical mechanics, online textbook (chapters 7–8) original explanations, illustrations, examples, and interactive demonstrations. the mit ocw course is credited as a subject reference; no mit text or figures are reproduced. check the mit ocw terms before reusing materials directly. ← back to courses free, independent learning materials. not affiliated with mit.","lessons/numerical-methods.html":"numerical methods and approximation — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate numerical methods and approximation what if a useful equation cannot be solved neatly by algebra? when an exact answer is difficult numerical methods use repeated approximate calculations to approach an answer. they are essential for realistic simulations, but a numerical answer still needs a convergence and error check. many important equations cannot be rearranged neatly to give an exact answer. engineers still need an answer that is accurate enough to guide a decision. numerical methods approach the result with controlled approximations, often through repeated calculations. an approximation is not necessarily inferior to an exact-looking formula. a numerical model that accounts for relevant boundary conditions can predict a physical system better than a simple analytic formula based on unrealistic assumptions. the responsibility is to understand error and verify the result, rather than accepting the computer's digits at face value. approximations and numerical error the bisection method finds a root when a continuous function changes sign over an interval. evaluate the midpoint, keep the half containing a sign change, and repeat. each iteration halves the bracketing interval. if f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs, bisect [a,b] until the root is bracketed tightly. bisection begins with a continuous function that changes sign on an interval. the intermediate value theorem guarantees at least one root somewhere inside. evaluate the midpoint, retain the subinterval with a sign change, and repeat. after n steps, the bracket width is the original width divided by 2^n. this makes the stopping criterion meaningful. if the bracket is only 0.001 wide, the midpoint's absolute error is no more than 0.0005 relative to some root in the bracket, assuming the root remains bracketed. other numerical methods, such as newton's method, may converge faster but require additional conditions and can fail with a poor starting point. every algorithm needs checks for accuracy, units and exceptional cases. worked examples question. estimate √2 by solving f(x) = x² − 2 = 0 between 1 and 2. solution, step by step. at 1.5, f = +0.25, so retain [1,1.5]. at 1.25, f = −0.4375, so retain [1.25,1.5]. at 1.375, f = −0.1094, so retain [1.375,1.5]. the root is near 1.414. worked example 2 question. use one newton step for f(x)=x²−2, starting at x₀=1.5. solution. f=0.25 and f′=3. update x₁=1.5−0.25/3≈1.4167; √2≈1.4142. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. for f(x) = x² − 9, bracket the positive root between 2 and 4. what midpoint is the exact root? show worked answer the first midpoint is (2+4)/2 = 3; f(3) = 0, so the positive root is 3. problem 2 of 5 approximate f′(2) for f(x)=x² with forward difference h=0.1. show answer and method [f(2.1)−f(2)]/0.1=(4.41−4)/0.1=4.1. problem 3 of 5 use the trapezoidal rule for y=x from 0 to 2, one interval. show answer and method (2−0)(0+2)/2 = 2. problem 4 of 5 if a bisection interval is [1,3], what is its midpoint? show answer and method 2. problem 5 of 5 why does reducing the step size not always improve a numerical answer indefinitely? show answer and method roundoff error and instability can eventually dominate discretisation error. try it yourself write a few lines of python to repeat bisection until the interval is less than 0.001 wide; print each interval. where this model stops bisection relies on continuity and an initial sign change; it cannot find every kind of root from arbitrary brackets. references single variable calculus (18.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2010. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← statistical distributions ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/optimisation.html":"optimisation using calculus — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate optimisation using calculus how do engineers choose dimensions that make something as large or as small as possible? finding the best value optimisation starts with a goal, such as maximum volume or minimum material. constraints turn many possible designs into a manageable mathematical family. a derivative identifies a candidate peak or trough, but physical limits still matter. many design questions are really about finding the best possible outcome under constraints: minimum material, maximum storage, shortest travel time or lowest operating cost. the derivative helps because it tells us where a small change in a design variable stops improving the result. but “best” must have a precise meaning. a lightweight beam might deflect too much, and a fast pump might use more power than necessary. in engineering, optimisation is not simply finding a maximum or minimum of an equation; it is deciding on an objective and identifying which values are feasible. critical points and derivatives for a rectangle with fixed perimeter 20 m, if one side is x, the other is 10 − x. its area is a(x) = x(10 − x). differentiate: a′(x) = 10 − 2x. the stationary point occurs at x = 5. a(x) = 10x − x²; a′(x) = 10 − 2x if a smooth one-variable function has a local interior maximum or minimum, its derivative is often zero there. such a stationary point is only a candidate: it could instead be a local minimum, a maximum or a flat inflection point. check the second derivative, the sign of the first derivative, and the endpoints of the allowed interval. for example, suppose a rectangle has a fixed perimeter p. writing one side as x gives the other as p/2−x, so area a=x(p/2−x). then a′=p/2−2x, and the stationary point occurs at x=p/4. because a″=−2, this is a maximum: a square maximises area among rectangles with that perimeter. always return the mathematical answer to the original physical constraints. worked examples question. find the greatest area of a rectangle whose perimeter is 20 m. solution, step by step. solve a′(x) = 0: 10 − 2x = 0, so x = 5 m. then the other side is 5 m and area is 25 m². since a″(x) = −2, this point is a maximum. worked example 2 question. a rectangle has perimeter 20 m. what dimensions maximise its area? solution. if sides are x and 10−x, a=x(10−x). a′=10−2x=0 gives x=5; a″=−2 so it is a maximum. square 5 m × 5 m. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. repeat for a rectangle of perimeter 28 m. what dimensions maximise area? show worked answer each side is 7 m; the maximum area is 49 m². problem 2 of 5 find stationary points of f(x)=x²−4x. show answer and method 2x−4=0 ⇒ x=2. problem 3 of 5 is x=2 a maximum or minimum of f(x)=x²−4x? show answer and method minimum because f″(x)=2 > 0. problem 4 of 5 for positive x, which maximises a(x)=12x−x²? show answer and method a′=12−2x=0 ⇒ x=6. problem 5 of 5 why check endpoints in optimisation with a closed interval? show answer and method the largest or smallest value may occur at a boundary, not a stationary point. try it yourself cut rectangles with equal perimeter from paper strips and compare the areas. does the equal-sided case win? where this model stops stationary points need to be checked against boundaries and design constraints; not all yield maxima. references single variable calculus (18.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2010. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← trigonometry and its applications applications of integration → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/orbit-types.html":"different types of satellite orbits — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate different types of satellite orbits why might one satellite circle earth every 90 minutes while another appears stationary? why satellites use different orbits imagine trying to watch a particular city from space. a satellite flying a few hundred kilometres above earth can take detailed pictures, but it crosses the sky quickly. a satellite much farther away sees a larger portion of earth at once, though fine detail is harder to resolve. neither is the “best orbit” until we decide what the satellite must do. an orbit is not simply a height. three features are especially useful: its size (how far it stays from earth), shape (nearly circular or stretched into an ellipse), and inclination (how tilted its plane is relative to the equator). these determine coverage, travel time and the ground track—the path a satellite appears to trace on a map of earth. low, medium and geostationary orbits low earth orbit (leo) is usually a few hundred to roughly 2,000 km up. it offers close-up imaging, shorter radio paths and frequent trips around earth. but one satellite moves past a location quickly, so continuous coverage generally needs several satellites. medium earth orbit (meo) lies farther out, between leo and geosynchronous altitudes. navigation constellations such as gps use orbits in this broad region because they can cover large areas without being as distant as geostationary satellites. geostationary orbit (geo) is a particular circular, equatorial orbit where the orbital period matches earth's rotation relative to the stars. from the ground, that satellite appears to remain over approximately the same longitude. it is useful for communications and broad weather monitoring, but its great distance introduces radio delay and reduces suitability for very fine surface imagery. inclination and the sun a polar orbit runs nearly north–south, letting earth rotate beneath it so the satellite can pass over many latitudes. a sun-synchronous orbit is usually highly inclined and designed so its orbital plane slowly turns, keeping each pass over a location at roughly the same local solar time. this gives more consistent lighting for comparing images. sun-synchronous does not mean stationary relative to the sun. the important question is therefore not “which orbit is highest?” but “what must we observe, how often, with what delay, and at what cost?” speed, period and orbital radius why does a low satellite go around earth faster? gravity supplies the inward acceleration that continually bends its direction of motion. for an ideal circular orbit , the required inward acceleration is v²/r and the gravitational acceleration is μ/r², where μ is earth's gravitational parameter. setting these equal gives the circular orbital speed. v = √(μ/r) the distance around a circle is 2πr. dividing that distance by speed gives the time for one complete revolution: t = 2π√(r³/μ) here r is distance from earth's centre , not altitude above the ground. for a satellite at height h above a spherical earth, use r = r earth + h. that distinction matters: adding 400 km to an orbital radius of about 6,371 km changes it much less than multiplying the radius by two. two conclusions follow directly: higher circular orbits have lower orbital speed but longer periods . and period changes with r to the three-halves power, not linearly. those statements apply to ideal circular motion around the same central body. why “24 hours” is not quite right earth turns once relative to distant stars in about 23 h 56 min 4 s (one sidereal day). that is the period a geostationary satellite must match. a geosynchronous satellite has that period but may still move north–south or east–west in the sky if its orbit is inclined or elliptical. geostationary is the narrower case: circular and equatorial. how altitude changes an orbit adjust altitude and compare the speed and period of an ideal circular earth orbit. earth 550 km schematic orbit — radius not to scale altitude above surface 550 km orbital speed 7.59 km/s orbital period 95.6 min ideal circular, two-body orbit: μ = 398600 km³/s² and earth radius = 6371 km. excludes drag and other perturbations; visual orbit is schematic. worked examples example 1 — a scaling shortcut. if the circular orbital radius around the same planet increases from r to 4r, by what factor does the period change? step 1. from t ∝ r 3/2 , write t₂/t₁ = (r₂/r₁) 3/2 . step 2. substitute r₂/r₁ = 4: the ratio is 4 3/2 = (√4) 3 = 8. result: the period becomes eight times longer. note that this compares orbital radius , not height above earth's surface. example 2 — finding geostationary altitude. estimate the radius and altitude of geo. take μ = 398,600 km³/s², one sidereal day = 86,164 s and earth's equatorial radius = 6,378 km. step 1. rearrange the period equation: r³ = μ(t/2π)². step 2. substitute the values: r = [398600 × (86164/2π)²] 1/3 ≈ 42,164 km from earth's centre. step 3. subtract earth's equatorial radius: h ≈ 42,164 − 6,378 ≈ 35,786 km . check: the circular speed is √(398600/42164) ≈ 3.07 km/s. that speed is smaller than for a low earth orbiter even though the travel time is much longer. example 3 — a mission choice. a satellite must photograph crop changes in the same region each week, ideally with similar sunlight. would an equatorial geostationary or a sun-synchronous low earth orbit be a more suitable starting choice? reasoning. for local detail, lower altitude usually helps achieve finer ground resolution for a given instrument. repeated passes at similar local solar times also make image comparison easier. conclusion: a sun-synchronous leo is a sensible starting point. but the final orbit requires trade-offs involving revisit frequency, swath width, clouds, cost and payload. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. if orbital radius doubles, by what factor does period change? show worked answer factor = 2^(3/2) = √8 ≈ 2.83. problem 2 of 5 what is leo short for? show answer and method low earth orbit. problem 3 of 5 geostationary satellites appear fixed above what terrestrial latitude? show answer and method the equator. problem 4 of 5 are sun-synchronous orbits typically highly inclined or equatorial? show answer and method typically highly inclined, near-polar. problem 5 of 5 would a circular orbit have nonzero eccentricity? show answer and method no; eccentricity e=0. try deciding a mission orbit choose an orbit for each mission: (1) a weather satellite that continuously views almost an entire hemisphere; (2) a camera that repeatedly images forests under similar lighting; (3) a navigation satellite serving large regions. explain your selection using altitude, coverage, revisit pattern and communications delay , rather than simply naming an orbit. then use the altitude slider above. compare a circular satellite near 400 km with one near 20,000 km. what changes most—speed or period? try estimating the period ratio yourself before reading the displayed values. what this simple calculation leaves out the speed and period equations above assume an isolated central gravity field and a circular two-body orbit. real satellites may follow elliptical paths, experience atmospheric drag in leo, and undergo slow orbital changes caused by earth's non-spherical gravity, the sun and moon, and small manoeuvres. for an elliptical orbit, the period depends on the semi-major axis ; the orbital speed changes along the path. inclination affects where the satellite flies over earth but is not determined by the circular-period formula alone. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← materials for rockets and spacecraft orbital transfers and manoeuvres → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/orbital-transfers.html":"orbital transfers and manoeuvres — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate orbital transfers and manoeuvres why can a spacecraft speed up to enter a higher orbit but arrive moving more slowly? changing orbits takes energy changing orbits is about changing energy and angular momentum, not simply choosing a new altitude. in a hohmann transfer between coplanar circular orbits, two ideal impulses move the craft onto an ellipse and then circularise it. moving a spacecraft to a new orbit is not just changing its altitude. a brief thruster burn changes velocity, which changes the shape and orientation of the subsequent trajectory. a spacecraft may temporarily occupy an elliptical transfer orbit before reaching a new circular orbit. counterintuitively, raising a circular orbit eventually produces a lower orbital speed. to get there, the spacecraft first accelerates at the lower orbit to reach a trajectory with a higher farthest point, then accelerates again at that farthest point to circularise. energy and angular momentum explain why these steps fit together. delta-v and transfer manoeuvres the vis-viva equation gives orbital speed at radius r on an orbit with semi-major axis a. on a higher circular orbit, the steady orbital speed is lower, even though reaching that orbit required positive δv along the transfer. v = √(μ(2/r − 1/a)) a hohmann transfer between two coplanar circular orbits uses two impulsive burns connected by a half-ellipse. the transfer ellipse has semi-major axis a_t=(r₁+r₂)/2. the vis-viva equation v²=μ(2/r−1/a) gives speed at each point of a keplerian orbit; comparing these speeds with circular-orbit speeds gives the ideal δv values. these calculations assume a two-body system and instantaneous burns. plane changes can be expensive, finite burns alter the trajectory, and low-thrust electric propulsion follows a different path. mission planning must also consider timing, eclipse, communications windows and collision avoidance. worked examples question. earth’s μ ≈ 3.986×10¹⁴ m³/s². estimate circular speed at radius r = 7.0×10⁶ m. solution, step by step. for a circle a = r, so v = √(μ/r) = √(3.986×10¹⁴ / 7.0×10⁶) ≈ 7546 m/s = 7.55 km/s. worked example 2 question. a manoeuvre changes spacecraft speed from 7.50 to 7.62 km/s impulsively, with the thrust direction aligned to velocity. find |δv|. solution. δv magnitude =7.62−7.50=0.12 km/s=120 m/s. this is not the total δv of a full transfer. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. at the same central body, if a circular orbital radius quadruples, what happens to circular speed? show worked answer v ∝ 1/√r, so speed is halved. problem 2 of 5 convert 0.35 km/s δv to m/s. show answer and method 350 m/s. problem 3 of 5 what is a hohmann transfer? show answer and method an ideal two-impulse coplanar transfer using a tangent ellipse between circular orbits. problem 4 of 5 at the lower orbit of a hohmann transfer to a higher circle, first burn prograde or retrograde? show answer and method prograde. problem 5 of 5 why do plane changes usually cost δv? show answer and method changing velocity direction requires vector velocity change. try it yourself draw two concentric circular orbits and an ellipse tangent to each. mark where the two ideal transfer burns occur. where this model stops a real transfer may require plane changes, finite-duration thrust and navigation corrections; the two-burn sketch is a starting model. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← different types of satellite orbits anatomy of a satellite → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/orbitals.html":"electrons and atomic orbitals — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate electrons and atomic orbitals what does it really mean when we say an electron is “around” a nucleus? where are electrons likely to be? electrons in atoms are described by quantum states, not miniature planets following fixed paths. an orbital describes a spatial probability distribution associated with a quantum state. it helps predict bonding and chemical properties. electrons do not have simultaneously definite positions and momenta in the way small classical balls do. an orbital describes a quantum state and provides a probability distribution for where an electron might be detected. the familiar s, p and d shapes represent patterns in probability density, not solid containers holding electrons. the idea becomes useful when we ask why atoms form particular bonds or why certain electronic transitions occur. electrons occupy allowed states with specific energies and quantum numbers, constrained by the pauli exclusion principle. these rules give rise to the arrangements represented schematically in the periodic table. orbitals and quantum states orbitals are labelled s, p, d and f with different shapes and capacities. each spatial orbital can hold at most two electrons with opposite spin. electron filling follows energy ordering and the pauli exclusion principle, with some important exceptions. one orbital → maximum 2 electrons; p subshell → 3 orbitals → maximum 6 electrons the principal quantum number n relates to the shell, the angular-momentum quantum number l to subshell type, and the magnetic quantum number m_l to orbital orientation. electron spin provides another quantum number. a single spatial orbital can accommodate at most two electrons with opposite spins. for simple many-electron atoms, aufbau filling rules and hund's rule are useful starting points, but the actual electron configurations of some elements show exceptions. orbital diagrams are not direct experimental pictures. it is more accurate to think of calculated states that help explain measured properties such as spectra, ionisation energies and bonding behaviour. worked examples question. write the ground-state electron configuration of a neutral oxygen atom (z = 8). solution, step by step. place 2 electrons in 1s, 2 in 2s and the remaining 4 in 2p: 1s² 2s² 2p⁴. its outer shell contains six electrons. worked example 2 question. write the electron configuration of oxygen (z=8) and count its valence electrons. solution. fill lowest-energy orbitals: 1s² 2s² 2p⁴. valence shell n=2 has 2+4=6 electrons. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. how many electrons can fill the three p orbitals of a single shell? show worked answer each orbital holds at most two; 3 × 2 = 6 electrons. problem 2 of 5 maximum electrons in an s subshell? show answer and method 2. problem 3 of 5 maximum electrons in a p subshell? show answer and method 6. problem 4 of 5 what is the ground-state configuration of helium? show answer and method 1s². problem 5 of 5 can two electrons in one orbital have identical spin quantum numbers? show answer and method no; paired electrons have opposite spins. try it yourself use boxes and arrows to show how electrons fill the three 2p orbitals of nitrogen before pairing them. where this model stops orbital sketches represent probability distributions, not the path traced by an electron. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← the periodic table molecular shapes and polarity → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/periodic-table.html":"the periodic table — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate the periodic table why do elements in the same column tend to behave similarly? patterns among the elements the periodic table is an organised map of atomic number, not a random list. elements in a group tend to share valence-electron patterns and related chemical behaviour. moving across a period generally changes nuclear attraction and electron arrangement. the periodic table is organised so that elements with related electronic structures appear in patterns. moving across a period generally increases the number of protons and electrons, while moving down a group adds principal electron shells. these changes help explain recurring trends in chemical behaviour. the table is not only a list for memorising. it can help predict whether an unfamiliar element is likely to form particular ions, whether its atoms are relatively large, and how strongly it tends to attract bonding electrons. such predictions are trends, not guarantees for every compound. electronic structure and periodic trends atomic number is the number of protons. periods run horizontally; groups run vertically. metals commonly lose electrons, nonmetals often gain or share electrons, but actual chemistry depends on context and bonding. atomic number z = number of protons atomic radius generally decreases across a period because increasing nuclear charge pulls electrons inward within a similar shell, while it generally increases down a group as additional shells are occupied. first ionisation energy often rises across a period, with important exceptions associated with subshell arrangements and electron pairing. valence electrons play a major role in bonding patterns. group 1 metals often form +1 ions, while many halogens form −1 ions in ionic compounds. transition metals exhibit more varied oxidation states, and chemical environment can change the actual behaviour substantially. use the table to make hypotheses, then check chemistry instead of treating every group trend as an absolute law. worked examples question. an atom has 11 protons. identify the element and state its neutral electron count. solution, step by step. atomic number 11 is sodium (na). a neutral atom has equal positive and negative charges, so it contains 11 electrons. sodium is in group 1. worked example 2 question. compare sodium (z=11) and potassium (z=19): why are their common chemical reactions similar? solution. both are group 1 elements with one outermost electron and often form +1 ions; their different sizes affect reactivity. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. an atom has 17 protons. what element is it, and how many electrons if neutral? show worked answer chlorine (cl) has z = 17. a neutral chlorine atom contains 17 electrons. problem 2 of 5 what does atomic number count? show answer and method protons. problem 3 of 5 which group contains noble gases? show answer and method group 18. problem 4 of 5 across a period, does atomic number increase or decrease? show answer and method increase from left to right. problem 5 of 5 which has more occupied electron shells: li or k? show answer and method potassium. try it yourself choose three neighbours in one table group and record their atomic numbers, outer-electron patterns and a property they share. where this model stops trends have exceptions; the periodic table predicts likely behaviour but does not replace experimental chemistry. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← chemical bonding electrons and atomic orbitals → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/ph-buffers.html":"ph, indicators and buffers — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate ph, indicators and buffers how can a solution resist big changes in ph after a little acid is added? why some solutions resist ph changes ph expresses hydrogen-ion activity on a logarithmic scale. buffers contain a weak acid/base pair that can consume modest added acid or base. their effect has limits: once one partner is depleted, ph can change sharply. a buffer is a solution that resists large changes in ph when relatively small amounts of acid or base are added. it usually contains a weak acid and its conjugate base, or a weak base and its conjugate acid. each component can consume some of the added reagent, reducing the immediate shift in hydrogen-ion activity. buffers matter in biological systems, chemical analysis and industrial processing. but resistance is finite: a sufficiently large addition will overwhelm the available acid or base component. a buffer does not mean the ph can never change. acid–base balance and buffering at introductory level ph ≈ −log₁₀[h⁺] when concentration is expressed relative to the standard reference. henderson–hasselbalch estimates buffer ph from pka and the base-to-acid ratio for suitable dilute systems. ph ≈ pka + log₁₀([a⁻]/[ha]) for a simple weak-acid buffer under suitable activity approximations, the henderson–hasselbalch equation is ph≈pka+log₁₀([a⁻]/[ha]). when the acid and conjugate base concentrations are equal, ph is approximately pka. raising their ratio by a factor of ten increases predicted ph by about one unit, assuming the approximation remains valid. a buffer works best near the acid's pka and when there is enough total buffer concentration to handle additions. strong dilution changes capacity even when the concentration ratio and initial ph change relatively little. complex biological or concentrated solutions require more careful activity and equilibrium calculations. worked examples question. a buffer has pka = 4.76 and equal concentrations of acid and conjugate base. estimate ph. solution, step by step. ratio = 1, so log₁₀(1) = 0. therefore ph ≈ pka = 4.76. worked example 2 question. a buffer contains equal concentrations of weak acid ha and conjugate base a⁻. if pka=4.8, estimate ph. solution. henderson–hasselbalch: ph=pka+log₁₀([a⁻]/[ha]) = 4.8 + log₁₀(1) = 4.8. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. if [a⁻]/[ha] = 10 with pka = 4.76, estimate ph. show worked answer ph ≈ 4.76 + log₁₀(10) = 5.76. problem 2 of 5 when [a⁻]/[ha]=10, what is ph relative to pka? show answer and method ph=pka+1. problem 3 of 5 at ph 7, what is [h⁺] approximately in dilute aqueous solution? show answer and method 10⁻⁷ mol/l, as a concentration approximation. problem 4 of 5 what happens when a little strong acid is added to an effective buffer? show answer and method conjugate base consumes much of the added h⁺, limiting the ph change. problem 5 of 5 can a buffer resist arbitrarily large acid addition? show answer and method no; it has finite capacity. try it yourself compare ph changes in plain water and a safe prepared buffer with indicator strips, using small amounts and lab-safe guidance. where this model stops ph is formally defined using activity; henderson–hasselbalch is an approximation and does not guarantee unlimited buffer capacity. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← chemical equilibrium oxidation, reduction and electrochemistry → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/pipe-pressure-loss.html":"flow through pipes and pressure losses — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate flow through pipes and pressure losses why does a long narrow pipe demand more pumping effort? why a long pipe needs more pressure real fluids lose useful mechanical energy through wall friction and fittings. narrower pipes often increase flow speed for a given flow rate, and pressure losses can rise steeply. pipe sizing therefore trades cost, space, pressure loss and required pump power. water flowing through a long pipe loses mechanical energy to viscous effects. that is why a pressure difference is required to sustain flow, even when the pipe is horizontal and has constant diameter. adding bends, valves, constrictions and fittings can increase the required pressure difference. a useful physical picture is to compare pushing water through a drinking straw and through a wide hose. the narrow straw requires much larger pressure gradients for comparable flow. pipe diameter often dominates pumping effort, which is why seemingly small design choices can have large operating-cost consequences. friction, flow rate and pressure loss for steady flow in a straight pipe, darcy–weisbach relates pressure loss to friction factor f, length l, diameter d and average velocity v. the friction factor depends on reynolds number and surface roughness. δp = f (l/d) (ρv²/2) for fully developed internal flow, darcy–weisbach expresses a major-loss pressure drop as δp=f_d(l/d)(ρv²/2), using the darcy friction factor f_d. the friction factor depends on reynolds number and relative roughness; for fully developed laminar flow in a round pipe, f_d=64/re. minor losses from fittings are often represented by kρv²/2. the system curve usually rises with flow rate because friction losses grow. a pump must supply enough head to overcome elevation changes, pressure requirements and losses. do not confuse absolute pressure at a point with pressure drop over a component. nor should one friction-factor correlation be used blindly in transitional flow or with strongly developing flows. worked examples question. water flows through a 10 m pipe of diameter 0.05 m at 2 m/s. if f = 0.025 and ρ = 1000 kg/m³, find straight-pipe loss. solution, step by step. l/d = 10/0.05 = 200. dynamic pressure = 1000 × 2²/2 = 2000 pa. δp = 0.025 × 200 × 2000 = 10,000 pa = 10 kpa. worked example 2 question. a straight-pipe system has loss proportional to q². if loss is 12 kpa at 2 l/s, what is it at 3 l/s under same conditions? solution. δp₂=12×(3/2)²=27 kpa. this uses the stated quadratic approximation. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. same pipe but length 20 m: what straight-pipe loss results if all else is unchanged? show worked answer δp doubles to 20 kpa because l is twice as large. problem 2 of 5 for the same pipe and fluid, if velocity doubles in turbulent quadratic regime, loss factor? show answer and method approximately 4. problem 3 of 5 what does a rougher pipe often do to turbulent friction loss? show answer and method increase it, with details depending on reynolds number. problem 4 of 5 is a minor loss always numerically small? show answer and method no; “minor” names fitting losses, which can dominate some systems. problem 5 of 5 name two common sources of pipe pressure loss. show answer and method wall friction and valves/bends/fittings. try it yourself draw a simple loop with a pump, straight pipe, elbows and valve. mark where energy losses occur and identify which need extra coefficients. where this model stops never confuse darcy friction factor with fanning friction factor; they differ by a factor of four. references fluid dynamics (2.06) — mit opencourseware, spring 2013. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← bernoulli’s principle pumps and fluid systems → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/pressure-fluids.html":"pressure in fluids — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate pressure in fluids explore how water depth and fluid density affect pressure, then try a simple observation at home. why does the water push harder deeper down? pressure measures how strongly a force acts over an area. in a still fluid, each deeper layer supports the weight of the fluid above it. that is why pressure rises with depth. pressure = force / area p = f/a for a liquid of approximately constant density, the increase in pressure below its surface is: δp = ρgh here ρ is density (kg/m³), g is gravitational acceleration (about 9.81 m/s² on earth), and h is depth (m). the answer is in pascals (pa). this is gauge pressure relative to the surface , not total absolute pressure. pressure is force distributed over area. a drawing pin can pierce material because its sharp tip concentrates the same applied force onto a tiny area. in a stationary liquid, however, pressure at a point acts in all directions and normally increases as you move downward because the water above has weight. you can feel this when diving a short distance in a swimming pool. the increase depends on depth and the liquid's density, not the shape of the pool. an unusually wide lake and a narrow tall column of the same liquid produce the same hydrostatic pressure at equal depth below their free surfaces, assuming the same atmospheric pressure. pressure acts in every direction in a fluid at rest, pressure pushes normal to any surface—even a vertical wall. a deeper point experiences greater pressure, but two points at the same depth in the same connected liquid have equal pressure. water density is roughly 1000 kg/m³; oil is often less dense. at the same depth, lower-density oil creates a smaller pressure increase. for a uniform incompressible fluid at rest, the gauge-pressure increase over a vertical depth h is ρgh. the absolute pressure is atmospheric pressure plus that increase when the surface is exposed to air. at 2 m depth in freshwater, the gauge pressure is approximately 1000×9.81×2=19.6 kpa. hydrostatic pressure gives rise to forces on walls and gates. because pressure changes with depth, the load on a vertical panel is not generally uniform: lower portions carry more pressure. integrating pressure over area determines the total force and its point of action. the simple equation is not a complete description for rapidly moving fluids or liquids whose density changes significantly with depth. explore depth and density pressure below a free surface depth below surface (m) 2 fluid density (kg/m³) 1000 open surface depth increases ↓ h = 2.0 m this drawing shows depth using a schematic tank, not the selected depth to physical scale. gauge pressure: 19.62 kpa. assumes a stationary, constant-density liquid and earth gravity. worked examples problem: what is the gauge pressure 2.0 m below the surface of fresh water? take ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 9.81 m/s², h = 2.0 m. δp = 1000 × 9.81 × 2.0 = 19,620 pa . convert to kilopascals: 19.62 kpa . if the surface is exposed to the atmosphere, absolute pressure is approximately atmospheric pressure plus 19.62 kpa. worked example 2 question. find gauge pressure 5 m under fresh water (ρ=1000 kg/m³; g=9.81 m/s²). solution. p=ρgh=1000×9.81×5=49,050 pa≈49.1 kpa. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: what is the gauge pressure 3 m deep in oil of density 800 kg/m³? show worked answer δp = 800 × 9.81 × 3 = 23,544 pa = 23.54 kpa . problem 2 of 5 gauge pressure at 1 m under water? use g=10 m/s². show answer and method 10,000 pa = 10 kpa. problem 3 of 5 if depth doubles, what happens to hydrostatic gauge pressure for constant density? show answer and method it doubles. problem 4 of 5 find pressure force on 0.2 m² at uniform pressure 20 kpa. show answer and method f=pa=20,000×0.2=4,000 n. problem 5 of 5 why is pressure at one depth the same in every direction in a fluid at rest? show answer and method a static fluid cannot support shear; otherwise it would deform and flow. a simple bottle experiment for a supervised demonstration, have an adult prepare two small holes at different heights in a disposable plastic bottle using appropriate tools before the activity. place the bottle over a sink or tray, add water, and compare the water streams. do not use glass containers or handle sharp tools without supervision. the stream trajectories also depend on changing water level and hole geometry. try explaining: why doesn't a wider tank automatically create more pressure at the same depth? references mit ocw 2.06 — fluid dynamics (pressure and hydrostatics) mit ocw — fluid dynamics syllabus original explanations, illustrations, examples, and interactive demonstrations. the mit ocw course is credited as a subject reference; no mit text or figures are reproduced. check the mit ocw terms before reusing materials directly. ← back to courses free, independent learning materials. not affiliated with mit.","lessons/probability.html":"probability — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate probability how can we reason about uncertain outcomes without predicting any single trial? reasoning about uncertain outcomes probability describes how likely an outcome is in a defined model. for equally likely outcomes, it is the number of favourable cases divided by the total cases. repeated observations may approach a stable fraction even though individual results remain unpredictable. probability gives a disciplined way to reason about uncertainty. when a fair die is rolled, no one knows the next result, but the possible outcomes and their chances can be described. in engineering, uncertainty may instead come from measurement noise, component variation or incomplete information, so identifying the source of randomness is important. a probability is not a guarantee about one trial. an event with probability 0.1 can happen on the first attempt, and an event with probability 0.9 can fail. repeated trials tend to reveal stable proportions under suitable assumptions, but small samples can look very uneven. counting outcomes and conditional probability if a fair six-sided die is rolled, there are six equally likely results. the probability of an even number is 3/6 = 1/2. if two trials are independent, multiply their probabilities to find the chance both happen. p(a) = favourable outcomes / total equally likely outcomes if outcomes are equally likely, probability can be counted as favourable outcomes divided by all possible outcomes. more generally, probabilities must be assigned according to the model or evidence. the addition rule avoids double counting overlapping events: p(a or b)=p(a)+p(b)−p(a and b). conditional probability asks how knowing one event changes our assessment of another: p(a|b)=p(a and b)/p(b), provided p(b)>0. for example, the probability that a test is positive given a fault is not the same as the probability that a fault exists given a positive test. base rates matter, which is why a rare failure may still be unlikely after a positive diagnostic signal. worked examples question. a fair die is rolled twice. what is the probability that both results are six? solution, step by step. each roll gives p(6) = 1/6. independent rolls mean multiply: (1/6) × (1/6) = 1/36 ≈ 2.78%. worked example 2 question. a bag has 3 red and 2 blue beads. draw two without replacement. find probability both are red. solution. p(red first)=3/5; p(red second | red first)=2/4. multiply: 3/5×2/4=3/10. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. what is the chance of getting at least one head in two tosses of a fair coin? show worked answer use the complement: p(no heads) = p(two tails) = 1/4. therefore p(at least one head) = 1 − 1/4 = 3/4. problem 2 of 5 what is p(even) on a fair six-sided die? show answer and method 3/6=1/2. problem 3 of 5 two independent fair coins: p(two heads)? show answer and method 1/2×1/2=1/4. problem 4 of 5 if p(a)=0.3, find p(not a). show answer and method 0.7. problem 5 of 5 can independent events be mutually exclusive if both have positive probability? show answer and method no; independent positive-probability events can occur together. try it yourself flip a coin 30 times and record the observed fraction of heads after every five flips. compare the run with the predicted probability. where this model stops independence and equal likelihood must be justified; do not apply simple counting blindly to a biased system. references introduction to probability and statistics (18.05) — mit opencourseware, spring 2022. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← coordinate geometry in 2d and 3d statistical distributions → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/pumps.html":"pumps and fluid systems — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate pumps and fluid systems how do you choose a pump for a real piping system? how a pump moves fluid a pump adds mechanical energy to a liquid. the piping system consumes that energy through elevation change and hydraulic losses. the operating flow is where the pump’s head–flow curve intersects the system head requirement. a pump transfers mechanical energy from a driver to a fluid. it does not create water, and it does not necessarily impose one fixed flow rate regardless of the piping. the actual operating flow is found where the pump characteristic meets the demand of the connected system. imagine a pump circulating coolant through a heat exchanger. opening a valve reduces system resistance and may increase flow; closing it increases resistance and usually reduces flow. the resulting pump head and efficiency change with the operating point. this is why selecting a pump from only one pressure and flow figure can be misleading. head, flow and pump performance pump head is energy per unit weight, usually expressed in metres of liquid. a simple model for a recirculating system is hsystem = hstatic + kq²; actual losses require components and fluid properties. hydraulic power is ρgqh. p_hydraulic = ρgqh head expresses energy per unit weight of fluid, with metres of fluid as a common unit. a pump curve plots added head against flow rate at a specified speed and impeller. the system curve represents static requirements plus frictional losses, which commonly increase roughly with flow squared in turbulent regimes. power transferred to the fluid is p_h=ρgqh, and required shaft power is p_h/η when overall pump efficiency is η. for water flowing at 0.002 m³/s against 15 m head, hydraulic power is about 294 w; with 60% efficiency, shaft demand is about 490 w. check net positive suction head, operating range and cavitation risk as part of pump selection, not as an afterthought. worked examples question. a water pump delivers 0.010 m³/s against a total head of 15 m. estimate hydraulic output power. solution, step by step. p = 1000 × 9.81 × 0.010 × 15 = 1471.5 w ≈ 1.47 kw. with a 70% overall efficiency, electrical input would be about 1.47/0.70 ≈ 2.10 kw. worked example 2 question. a pump raises water at 0.01 m³/s through 20 m head. estimate hydraulic power for ρ=1000 kg/m³, g=9.81. solution. p=ρgqh=1000×9.81×0.01×20=1962 w≈1.96 kw; shaft power is higher if efficiency is <100%. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. if a pump delivers 0.005 m³/s at 10 m head, find the hydraulic power for water. show worked answer p = 1000 × 9.81 × 0.005 × 10 = 490.5 w. problem 2 of 5 compute hydraulic power at q=0.005 m³/s and h=10 m for water, g=10. show answer and method 500 w. problem 3 of 5 if pump efficiency is 50% and hydraulic power is 1 kw, shaft input? show answer and method 2 kw. problem 4 of 5 what sets the operating point of a pump and piping system? show answer and method intersection of pump head curve and system head curve. problem 5 of 5 why does raising a tank vertically increase static head? show answer and method the required gravitational potential energy per unit weight increases. try it yourself sketch an imagined falling pump head curve and rising system curve. move the valve position in your sketch and reason about the new operating point. where this model stops a pump’s nameplate maximum flow and maximum head usually do not occur at the same operating point. references fluid dynamics (2.06) — mit opencourseware, spring 2013. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← flow through pipes and pressure losses heat transfer → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/reaction-balancing.html":"chemical reactions and balancing equations — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate chemical reactions and balancing equations why must atoms balance even when the substances look completely different? what stays the same in a chemical reaction? chemical reactions rearrange atoms into new combinations. in an ordinary chemical reaction, each element’s atoms are conserved. a balanced equation counts those atoms on both sides; coefficients change amounts, not the identity of substances. a chemical equation describes reactants becoming products, but atoms are rearranged rather than created or destroyed in ordinary chemical reactions. balancing an equation is therefore more than a bookkeeping exercise; it expresses conservation of each element. coefficients tell us relative amounts of substances, not how fast a reaction proceeds. for example, burning hydrogen in oxygen creates water. there are two oxygen atoms in an o₂ molecule, so a balanced equation must account for both. changing h₂o into h₂o₂ merely to fix the count would describe a different substance and is not an acceptable balancing step. atoms, coefficients and balanced equations to balance, list atom counts for each element, adjust whole-number coefficients, and recheck. never change chemical subscripts to make the bookkeeping work: that would change the chemical species. 2h₂ + o₂ → 2h₂o the balanced equation is 2h₂+o₂→2h₂o. it says that two moles of hydrogen molecules react with one mole of oxygen molecules to produce two moles of water, under the ideal stoichiometric interpretation. subscripts stay fixed because they define chemical identity; coefficients can change to balance amounts. some reactions require additional constraints. for ionic equations, both atoms and net electric charge must balance. a balanced equation does not demonstrate that the proposed reaction is chemically feasible, safe or complete. it provides the quantitative ratios used later in stoichiometry once the actual chemistry is established. worked examples question. balance the formation of water from h₂ and o₂. solution, step by step. start with h₂ + o₂ → h₂o. put 2 before h₂o to balance oxygen, giving 4 h atoms on the right. put 2 before h₂ to balance hydrogen: 2h₂ + o₂ → 2h₂o. worked example 2 question. balance hydrogen combustion h₂ + o₂ → h₂o. solution. choose 2 h₂o so o atoms are 2 on the right. then 2 h₂ balances 4 h atoms. balanced: 2h₂ + o₂ → 2h₂o. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. balance fe + o₂ → fe₂o₃ using the smallest whole numbers. show worked answer 4fe + 3o₂ → 2fe₂o₃. both sides have 4 fe atoms and 6 o atoms. problem 2 of 5 balance fe + o₂ → fe₂o₃. show answer and method 4fe + 3o₂ → 2fe₂o₃. problem 3 of 5 balance n₂ + h₂ → nh₃. show answer and method n₂ + 3h₂ → 2nh₃. problem 4 of 5 can coefficients change but subscripts stay fixed when balancing? show answer and method yes: change only coefficients to conserve atom counts. problem 5 of 5 what physical principle motivates balancing equations? show answer and method conservation of atoms of each element in ordinary chemical reactions. try it yourself use coloured paper circles to represent atoms before and after a symbolic reaction; ensure no circles vanish. where this model stops a balanced equation describes conservation, not necessarily how rapidly the reaction happens or its mechanism. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← states of matter and phase changes moles and chemical calculations → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/reaction-energy.html":"energy in chemical reactions — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate energy in chemical reactions why can some reactions warm a container while others cool it? why reactions release or absorb heat chemical reactions involve breaking and forming bonds, changing the energy of the system and surroundings. exothermic processes release heat under a specified condition; endothermic processes absorb it. a reaction’s enthalpy change compares products with reactants. chemical reactions can warm their surroundings or cool them because reactants and products have different energy states. some processes release energy to the surroundings (exothermic), while others absorb energy (endothermic). this says something about heat exchange at specified conditions, not whether the reaction happens immediately or is safe. a burning fuel can release substantial heat but still require ignition because an activation-energy barrier must be overcome. conversely, a process can be energetically favourable yet proceed extremely slowly. separating thermodynamics from reaction kinetics helps resolve these apparent contradictions. enthalpy and energy accounting for a reaction at approximately constant pressure, heat transfer is often described by δh. a negative δh is exothermic by the usual convention. a simple calorimetry estimate uses q = mcδt for a measured surrounding liquid. q = mcδt; δhreaction ≈ −qsurroundings at constant pressure at constant pressure, reaction enthalpy δh describes the heat exchanged for a reaction as written, under appropriate conditions. a negative δh indicates an exothermic change with the usual sign convention. if an exothermic reaction releases 100 kj per mole of reaction and proceeds to 0.2 mol of reaction extent, its ideal heat release is 20 kj. hess's law states that enthalpy change depends on initial and final states, allowing reaction steps to be added algebraically. bond-energy estimates are rough because average bond energies may not represent specific molecular environments. a calorimetry calculation also needs to account for heat capacity and any heat lost to the apparatus or surroundings. worked examples question. a reaction warms 100 g of water from 20°c to 25°c. estimate heat gained by water using c = 4.18 j/(g·k). solution, step by step. qwater = 100 × 4.18 × 5 = 2090 j = 2.09 kj. if other heat transfers are negligible, the reaction released about 2.09 kj. worked example 2 question. a reaction releases 25 kj per mole. what heat is released by 0.4 mol at the stated conditions? solution. 0.4×25=10 kj released; reaction enthalpy is −10 kj for that amount. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. if 50 g of water rises by 3°c, estimate its heat gain with c = 4.18 j/(g·k). show worked answer q = 50 × 4.18 × 3 = 627 j. problem 2 of 5 is a reaction that releases heat exothermic or endothermic? show answer and method exothermic. problem 3 of 5 if δh=+40 kj for a reaction as written, does it absorb or release heat? show answer and method absorb 40 kj at constant pressure. problem 4 of 5 what units are often used for molar enthalpy change? show answer and method kj/mol. problem 5 of 5 does a catalyst change the overall enthalpy change of a reaction? show answer and method no; it changes pathway and activation energy. try it yourself compare temperature change when safe substances dissolve in water, using only materials and procedures approved for home use. where this model stops temperature change alone does not determine reaction enthalpy without masses, heat capacity and an estimate of heat losses. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← solutions and concentrations chemical equilibrium → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/reaction-rates.html":"reaction rates and catalysts — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate reaction rates and catalysts why can a reaction be favourable yet proceed almost imperceptibly slowly? why some reactions happen faster thermodynamics concerns energy and equilibrium, while kinetics concerns how fast change occurs. reaction rate depends on collision opportunities and reaction pathways. higher temperature often increases the fraction of collisions energetic enough to react. two reactions with the same overall energy change can proceed at completely different speeds. iron corrodes slowly in many ordinary environments, while some combustion reactions proceed extremely rapidly after ignition. reaction rate depends on pathways and activation barriers, not simply on whether the reaction releases energy. concentration, temperature, available surface area and catalysts can influence how frequently reactive encounters occur and how likely they are to produce products. crushing a soluble solid may speed its dissolution by increasing exposed surface area, but dissolution itself should not be confused with a chemical reaction. collisions, temperature and catalysts a basic rate law may be rate = k[a]^m, but the exponent m must generally come from measurements, not guesses from the balanced equation. catalysts provide an alternative pathway with lower activation barrier. rate = k[a]^m (empirical rate law) rate laws are measured relationships such as rate=k[a]^m[b]^n. the exponents are reaction orders determined experimentally; they cannot normally be read directly from overall balanced-equation coefficients. temperature affects the rate constant, often approximated by the arrhenius relationship k=aexp(−ea/rt). a catalyst offers a different reaction pathway with a lower effective energy barrier and is regenerated through the catalytic cycle. it does not change the equilibrium constant at the same temperature. real reactions may have several elementary steps and intermediates, so a simple one-step collision picture may explain trends but not full mechanisms. worked examples question. for a first-order reaction with rate = k[a], k = 0.20 s⁻¹ and [a] = 0.50 mol/l, find the instantaneous rate. solution, step by step. rate = 0.20 × 0.50 = 0.10 mol/(l·s). this value applies at the stated concentration. worked example 2 question. a concentration falls from 0.80 to 0.50 mol/l in 10 s. find average disappearance rate. solution. rate of disappearance = −δc/δt = (0.80−0.50)/10 = 0.030 mol/(l·s). practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. for the same law, what is the rate when [a] falls to 0.25 mol/l? show worked answer rate = 0.20 × 0.25 = 0.050 mol/(l·s). halving [a] halves rate in this first-order model. problem 2 of 5 what normally happens to reaction rate when temperature rises? show answer and method usually increases; more particles cross activation-energy barriers. problem 3 of 5 what does a catalyst lower? show answer and method activation energy along an alternative reaction pathway. problem 4 of 5 if concentration changes by 0.2 mol/l over 4 s, average magnitude of rate? show answer and method 0.05 mol/(l·s). problem 5 of 5 why might powder react faster than a solid block of equal mass? show answer and method powder exposes greater surface area for heterogeneous reaction. try it yourself observe how an effervescent tablet behaves in cool versus moderately warm water using a safe, unsealed container. where this model stops a catalyst changes rate, not the position of equilibrium at fixed temperature, and the empirical order need not equal a reaction coefficient. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← oxidation, reduction and electrochemistry ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/redox.html":"oxidation, reduction and electrochemistry — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate oxidation, reduction and electrochemistry why does a battery work, and how is rust related to electron transfer? following electrons through a reaction oxidation involves loss of electrons; reduction involves gain. these processes occur together because electrons cannot simply disappear. separating the half-reactions into different parts of a cell can let electrons travel through a circuit. oxidation and reduction describe transfer of electrons or changes in formal oxidation state. when iron rusts, iron is oxidised while another species is reduced. in a battery, the separation of oxidation and reduction processes allows electron flow through an external circuit and provides electrical energy. the mnemonic “oxidation is loss, reduction is gain” refers to electrons. the two processes must occur together: electrons cannot disappear. a reaction may involve electron-density shifts in covalent species even when discrete free electrons are not actually moving between isolated ions. oxidation, reduction and electrical work oxidation numbers help track electron transfers. in a simple galvanic cell, electrons leave the oxidation electrode (anode) and travel through the external circuit to the reduction electrode (cathode). ions move through an electrolyte to maintain charge balance. zn → zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ (oxidation) assign oxidation numbers according to conventions, then check which elements increase or decrease. in a simple zinc–copper galvanic cell, zinc is oxidised to zn²⁺ at the anode while cu²⁺ is reduced to copper at the cathode. electrons pass through an external conductor, while ions in the electrolyte help maintain charge balance. cell voltage relates electrical driving force to reaction free energy through δg=−nfe under appropriate conditions. standard electrode potentials help predict ideal-cell behaviour, but measured performance depends on concentrations, internal resistance, temperature and reaction kinetics. household battery disassembly is not a safe experiment; paper-based half-reaction balancing is a better starting activity. worked examples question. when zinc metal forms zn²⁺ ions, how many electrons are released for every zinc atom? solution, step by step. zinc loses two electrons: zn → zn²⁺ + 2e⁻. the metal is oxidised; another half-reaction must consume those electrons. worked example 2 question. magnesium metal forms mg²⁺. write its oxidation half-equation and identify electron transfer. solution. mg → mg²⁺ + 2e⁻. magnesium loses two electrons, so it is oxidised. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. in cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → cu, is copper oxidised or reduced? show worked answer reduced: cu²⁺ gains two electrons to form neutral copper. problem 2 of 5 does oxidation involve loss or gain of electrons? show answer and method loss. problem 3 of 5 in zn → zn²⁺ + 2e⁻, is zinc oxidised or reduced? show answer and method oxidised: zinc loses two electrons. problem 4 of 5 what is the oxidation state of elemental oxygen o₂? show answer and method 0. problem 5 of 5 what is the sign of the cathode in a standard galvanic cell? show answer and method positive; reduction occurs there. try it yourself sketch a two-electrode cell with a salt bridge and show separate paths for electrons and ions. use a safe classroom kit for any real assembly. where this model stops cathode means reduction and anode means oxidation in both battery and electrolysis; electrode signs change with cell type. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← ph, indicators and buffers reaction rates and catalysts → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/rocket-engines.html":"rocket propulsion and engines — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate rocket propulsion and engines how does expelling hot gas create thrust even in the vacuum of space? how a rocket engine produces thrust rocket engines accelerate propellant backward; the reaction force accelerates the vehicle forward. the exhaust pushes on the vehicle’s internal surfaces before leaving. no atmosphere is required for rocket thrust. a rocket engine creates a high-speed exhaust jet, but there are many ways to produce and accelerate that jet. chemical rockets release energy through reactions and expand hot gas through a nozzle. electric thrusters use electrical energy to accelerate propellant and can operate efficiently with small thrust over long periods. the appropriate engine depends on the mission. a launch vehicle needs substantial thrust to overcome gravity and atmospheric forces; a deep-space probe may favour high specific impulse even if it accelerates very slowly. comparing two engines using only exhaust temperature or only thrust misses much of the picture. propellants, exhaust and efficiency in a simplified steady rocket, thrust comes from exhaust momentum and any pressure mismatch between nozzle exit and ambient. propulsion systems vary: chemical engines offer high thrust, electric propulsion often offers high exhaust velocity but low thrust. f = ṁvₑ + (pₑ − pₐ)aₑ momentum flow through an engine provides thrust. in a chemical nozzle, hot gas converts some thermal energy into directed exhaust kinetic energy; the pressure difference between exit and surroundings may contribute additional force. the nozzle's performance changes with surrounding pressure, so geometry optimised for sea level need not be optimal in vacuum. specific impulse i_sp is thrust divided by propellant weight-flow rate and is commonly expressed in seconds. it is related to effective exhaust velocity by v_e=i_sp g₀. high i_sp means less propellant for a given ideal δv, but does not guarantee adequate thrust or low total system mass. pumps, combustion stability, cooling, feed systems and control are major parts of practical engine design. worked examples question. a rocket ejects 5 kg/s of propellant at 2500 m/s. ignore pressure thrust. estimate force. solution, step by step. momentum thrust f = ṁvₑ = 5 × 2500 = 12,500 n = 12.5 kn. over one second, the exhaust carries 12,500 kg·m/s of momentum. worked example 2 question. estimate ideal nozzle thrust from mass flow 3 kg/s, exhaust speed 2200 m/s, neglecting pressure thrust. solution. f≈ṁve=3×2200=6600 n. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. an idealised electric thruster expels 0.002 kg/s at 20,000 m/s. ignore pressure correction. find thrust. show worked answer f = 0.002 × 20,000 = 40 n. problem 2 of 5 for a rocket nozzle, what produces momentum thrust? show answer and method accelerating and ejecting propellant mass. problem 3 of 5 what extra term appears if exit pressure differs from ambient? show answer and method (pₑ−pₐ)aₑ. problem 4 of 5 does exhaust speed usually rise or fall through a properly expanded supersonic nozzle? show answer and method rise as pressure/thermal energy converts into directed kinetic energy. problem 5 of 5 why might vacuum and sea-level thrust differ? show answer and method ambient pressure changes the nozzle pressure-thrust contribution. try it yourself use the safe balloon-on-a-string demonstration from the earlier rocket lesson. explain which way air leaves and why the balloon moves oppositely. where this model stops this lesson addresses propulsion physics; do not attempt homemade combustors, pressurised motors or energetic propellant mixtures. references introduction to propulsion systems (16.50) — mit opencourseware, spring 2012. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← rocket stages and the rocket equation rocket stability and aerodynamics → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/rocket-equation.html":"rocket stages and the rocket equation — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate rocket stages and the rocket equation why does carrying propellant to lift other propellant create a design challenge? why rockets need so much propellant as a rocket ejects propellant, it becomes lighter and can accelerate more effectively. the ideal rocket equation connects attainable velocity change with exhaust velocity and the ratio between initial and final masses. staging discards empty hardware. rockets face a special challenge: they must carry the propellant they will later expel. as the rocket burns propellant, the vehicle becomes lighter, so the same thrust can produce greater acceleration. however, carrying additional propellant also increases initial mass, which is why reaching very high speed changes can demand large mass ratios. staging helps by discarding empty tanks and structures rather than accelerating them throughout the entire mission. it reduces dead mass, but introduces separation events, interfaces and control challenges. this is an engineering trade-off, not merely an algebraic trick. mass ratio and the rocket equation for an ideal rocket outside aerodynamic and gravitational losses, δv = vₑ ln(m₀/mf). if exhaust is characterised by specific impulse isp, vₑ = g₀isp. the equation uses natural logarithms and requires consistent mass units. δv = g₀ isp ln(m₀/mf) for an ideal rocket with constant effective exhaust velocity and no external forces, the tsiolkovsky equation gives δv=v_e ln(m₀/m_f). the ratio m₀/m_f includes propellant consumed during the burn and assumes the chosen mass accounting is consistent. a mass ratio of about 2.718 yields δv equal to v_e. real launch vehicles require more propellant than the ideal equation alone suggests because gravity and aerodynamic drag act during flight, and steering may incur further losses. rocket stages and payload constraints make mass budgeting central. the logarithm reveals diminishing returns: doubling propellant mass does not simply double achievable δv when dry mass is significant. worked examples question. a rocket stage has isp = 300 s and mass ratio m₀/mf = 3. estimate ideal δv. solution, step by step. effective exhaust speed = 9.81 × 300 ≈ 2943 m/s. ln(3) ≈ 1.0986. δv ≈ 2943 × 1.0986 ≈ 3233 m/s = 3.23 km/s. worked example 2 question. an ideal rocket has ve=2500 m/s and mass ratio m₀/mf=3. find δv. solution. tsiolkovsky equation: δv=ve ln(m₀/mf)=2500 ln3≈2747 m/s. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. for the same isp, if the mass ratio is e ≈ 2.718, what ideal δv results? show worked answer ln(e) = 1, so δv ≈ 2943 m/s. problem 2 of 5 mass ratio 2, ve=2000 m/s: ideal δv? show answer and method 2000 ln2≈1386 m/s. problem 3 of 5 why does carrying more propellant also create a structural/propellant-mass challenge? show answer and method the rocket must accelerate additional propellant until it is expended. problem 4 of 5 is the ideal rocket equation valid without correction when drag and gravity losses are large? show answer and method no; it excludes those losses. problem 5 of 5 how does staging often help δv capability? show answer and method dropping spent dry mass improves later mass ratios. try it yourself create a spreadsheet varying dry mass and fuel mass. plot how ideal δv changes with mass ratio; note diminishing returns. where this model stops the ideal rocket equation ignores gravitational, drag and steering losses; it does not by itself predict altitude or orbital insertion. references introduction to propulsion systems (16.50) — mit opencourseware, spring 2012. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← gravity and escape velocity rocket propulsion and engines → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/rocket-stability.html":"rocket stability and aerodynamics — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate rocket stability and aerodynamics why does the same gust affect a rocket differently depending on its shape? why a rocket points into the airflow a stable rocket tends to correct a small angular disturbance rather than amplify it. aerodynamic forces act around a centre of pressure, while inertia acts about the centre of mass. their relative locations influence the restoring aerodynamic moment. a slender rocket moving through air may align with its direction of travel or diverge from it depending on where aerodynamic forces act relative to its centre of mass. when disturbed by a gust, a restoring aerodynamic moment can help point the vehicle back into the relative airflow. a destabilising moment does the opposite. this is similar to an arrow with feathers at the rear, but rockets are more complicated because mass distribution, speed, atmosphere and propulsion change during flight. an illustration of fins alone cannot establish that a particular rocket will be stable in every condition. centre of mass and aerodynamic stability for many simple fin-stabilised model rockets, the centre of pressure lies behind the centre of mass during flight. the aerodynamic force then tends to point the rocket back into the airflow. this is a simplified subsonic stability picture. static margin ≈ (x_cp − x_cm)/body diameter, with x measured rearward for simplified subsonic fin-stabilised rockets, a centre of pressure aft of the centre of mass generally contributes static restoring stability for small angles of attack. static margin expresses their separation relative to a reference body diameter. dynamic stability also depends on mass moments of inertia, aerodynamic damping, control and changing flight conditions. a vehicle can be statically stable but still experience problematic oscillations or structural loads. as propellant burns, the centre of mass moves; as speed changes, aerodynamic coefficients change. flight design requires analysing a trajectory and a range of disturbances rather than trusting one static geometric sketch. worked examples question. a small educational model has its centre of mass 0.40 m from the nose and centre of pressure 0.52 m from the nose. body diameter is 0.06 m. find static margin. solution, step by step. separation = 0.52 − 0.40 = 0.12 m. divide by 0.06 m to get 2 body diameters, or 2 calibres of static margin, in the stated coordinate convention. worked example 2 question. in a simple model, a rocket centre of pressure is 0.3 m behind its centre of mass. explain initial stability tendency. solution. a small angle to incoming airflow generally produces a restoring aerodynamic moment when cp lies aft of cm. this is a qualitative static-stability criterion, not a flight guarantee. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. if the centre-of-pressure location is 0.48 m, centre of mass is 0.42 m and diameter 0.04 m, what is the margin? show worked answer margin = (0.48 − 0.42)/0.04 = 1.5 calibres. problem 2 of 5 for typical passive rocket stability, should centre of pressure be ahead of or behind centre of mass? show answer and method behind (aft of) the centre of mass. problem 3 of 5 what is the aerodynamic angle of attack? show answer and method angle between body reference axis and oncoming relative airflow. problem 4 of 5 why are fins used on some rockets? show answer and method they shift aerodynamic pressure effects and can provide restoring moments. problem 5 of 5 can a statically stable rocket still oscillate or tumble? show answer and method yes; dynamic stability and flight conditions also matter. try it yourself draw a paper rocket profile and label estimated mass and aerodynamic centres. move an imaginary mass forward and predict the effect. where this model stops static margin is only one part of stability; speed, aerodynamics, flexibility and flight conditions can change the response. references introduction to propulsion systems (16.50) — mit opencourseware, spring 2012. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← rocket propulsion and engines launch loads and vibrations → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/rockets.html":"how rockets work — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate how rockets work discover thrust, exhaust momentum, gravity and the simplest rocket calculations. how can a rocket push itself in empty space? a rocket does not need to push on air. it carries propellant and accelerates exhaust mass backward. the rocket gains forward momentum as the exhaust carries momentum in the opposite direction. newton's third law gives a useful intuition, while conservation of momentum provides the quantitative picture. a simplified thrust relationship is: thrust ≈ ṁ vₑ here ṁ (kg/s) is the propellant mass flow rate and vₑ (m/s) is effective exhaust velocity. real engines can also have a pressure-thrust contribution. rockets lose mass as propellant is used; this first lesson treats mass as fixed for a short instant. a rocket does not need to push against air to move. it accelerates by expelling mass in one direction and receiving an opposite momentum change. that is why a rocket can work in vacuum, and why its exhaust direction matters even when its surroundings contain no gas. a balloon rocket sliding along a string demonstrates the same principle at a simple level: air leaves the opening and the balloon moves oppositely. a real rocket adds controlled combustion or another propulsion process, a nozzle, tanks, guidance, structures and safety systems. the underlying conservation of momentum is simple; achieving a reliable flight vehicle is not. thrust is not the same as acceleration at earth’s surface a vertical rocket has weight mg downward. if we ignore air resistance for a moment, its upward acceleration at an instant is approximately (t − mg)/m , where t is thrust. if thrust equals weight, acceleration is zero at that instant. if thrust exceeds weight, the rocket accelerates upward in this simple model. a common ideal thrust relationship is f=ṁv_e+(p_e−p_a)a_e, where ṁ is exhaust mass flow, v_e the exit speed relative to the rocket, and the second term accounts for pressure differences at the nozzle exit. thrust changes the rocket's momentum, while aerodynamic drag and gravity also act during atmospheric ascent. a large thrust does not automatically mean an efficient rocket. specific impulse describes how effectively propellant produces impulse, and vehicle mass changes as fuel is consumed. a rocket's mission also depends on stability, mass fraction, guidance and trajectory. when comparing propulsion technologies, distinguish thrust (force), total impulse (force integrated over time) and energy required. explore thrust and weight an idealized rocket at liftoff propellant flow rate (kg/s) 2 effective exhaust speed (m/s) 1000 rocket mass at this instant (kg) 150 thrust ↑ weight ↓ exhaust flows ↓ diagram explains forces; thrust and exhaust motion are not shown to scale. thrust 2000 n; weight 1471.5 n; acceleration 3.52 m/s². ignore aerodynamic drag, pressure-thrust correction and changing mass. no engine design or propellant-making instructions. worked examples problem: a rocket has mass 150 kg at one instant. it ejects 2 kg/s with effective exhaust speed 1000 m/s. estimate upward acceleration near earth’s surface. idealized thrust: ṁ vₑ = 2 × 1000 = 2000 n . weight: mg = 150 × 9.81 = 1471.5 n . net upward force = 2000 − 1471.5 = 528.5 n. acceleration = 528.5/150 = 3.52 m/s² upward . a real trajectory needs air drag, variable mass and changing gravitational acceleration. worked example 2 question. an ideal rocket expels 1.5 kg/s at effective exhaust speed 2000 m/s. estimate thrust, ignoring pressure correction. solution. thrust ≈ ṁve = 1.5×2000=3000 n. it acts opposite to exhaust momentum flow. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: a 100 kg idealized rocket generates 1500 n upward thrust on earth. neglect drag. what is its initial upward acceleration? show worked answer weight = 981 n. net force = 1500 − 981 = 519 n. a = 519/100 = 5.19 m/s² upward . problem 2 of 5 at ṁ=2 kg/s and ve=1500 m/s, ideal thrust? show answer and method 3000 n. problem 3 of 5 why can a rocket accelerate in vacuum? show answer and method it expels reaction mass, conserving momentum; it does not need surrounding air. problem 4 of 5 a 200 kg vehicle experiences net upward force 400 n. acceleration? show answer and method 400/200=2 m/s² upward. problem 5 of 5 if rocket mass drops while thrust stays constant, what happens to thrust/mass acceleration? show answer and method it increases, with other forces treated separately. try it safely with a balloon thread a string through a drinking straw and pull the string taut across a room. tape an inflated, untied balloon to the straw, with the opening pointing backward. release it and observe motion in the opposite direction to the escaping air. extend it: does a larger balloon always travel farther? what else changes—drag, mass, nozzle direction or friction? this is a demonstration of the principle, not a model of an actual rocket engine. references mit ocw 16.50 — introduction to propulsion systems mit ocw 16.50 — lecture notes (rocket equation and propulsion) original explanations, illustrations, examples, and interactive demonstrations. the mit ocw course is credited as a subject reference; no mit text or figures are reproduced. check the mit ocw terms before reusing materials directly. ← back to courses free, independent learning materials. not affiliated with mit.","lessons/satellite-communications.html":"satellite communications and antennas — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate satellite communications and antennas how can a tiny spacecraft send information back to a distant antenna? how spacecraft exchange information communication is an energy-and-information link. a transmitter radiates electromagnetic waves, an antenna directs some of that energy, and a receiver detects a weak signal amid noise. greater distance usually means greater spreading loss. a satellite communication link carries information through electromagnetic waves between transmitters and receivers. the signal becomes weaker with distance because energy spreads through space, and receivers must detect it against noise and interference. antenna direction, available power and data rate therefore become design constraints. imagine sending one image from a low earth orbit satellite during a brief ground-station pass. high data rate is useful, but the satellite may only see that station for minutes. a system must plan both how strongly it can transmit and how long it can maintain a usable connection. signals, antennas and link budgets in free space, received power decreases with distance squared for fixed transmitter and antenna characteristics. data rate, bandwidth, pointing accuracy and atmospheric effects all influence the link budget. free-space spreading: received power ∝ 1/r² for an ideal free-space link, path loss in decibels is l_fs=20log₁₀(4πr/λ), where r is range and λ is wavelength. the full link budget combines transmitter power, antenna gains, propagation losses, pointing losses and receiver sensitivity. greater range increases loss, while antenna directivity can concentrate power toward the intended direction. antenna gain is not extra generated energy; it describes how radiation is distributed spatially. wider bandwidth and higher data rates can demand more received signal quality depending on modulation and coding. ground-station geometry, atmosphere, regulatory spectrum constraints and redundancy all affect actual mission design. worked examples question. two otherwise identical free-space links operate at distances r and 2r. what happens to received power? solution, step by step. increasing range by factor 2 reduces received power to 1/2² = 1/4. in decibels, this is about 6 db more path loss. worked example 2 question. a radio signal travels 36,000 km one way in vacuum. estimate delay using c=300,000 km/s. solution. t=d/c=36000/300000=0.12 s one way; ignore processing and routing delays. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. if range triples, what fraction of received power remains in this simplified model? show worked answer 1/3² = 1/9 of the original received power. problem 2 of 5 time for light to travel 300 km in vacuum at 300000 km/s? show answer and method 0.001 s = 1 ms. problem 3 of 5 if an antenna gain increases, does the radiated energy necessarily increase? show answer and method not necessarily; gain describes directionality for the same total radiated power. problem 4 of 5 why use a directional high-gain antenna? show answer and method to concentrate signal and improve link budget in a chosen direction. problem 5 of 5 what causes free-space signal strength to weaken with increasing range? show answer and method energy spreads across increasing spherical area; received power decreases with range squared under ideal free-space assumptions. try it yourself compare a broad flashlight beam with a narrow one as an analogy for antenna directionality. note how pointing precision becomes important. where this model stops antenna gain, frequency, atmosphere and receiver noise matter; distance-squared behaviour is only one part of a link budget. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← attitude control and reaction wheels thermal management in space → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/satellite-mission.html":"designing a simple satellite mission — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate designing a simple satellite mission where should a satellite design begin: with hardware, orbit or an actual question? what must a satellite accomplish? start with a mission: what information or service is needed, for whom, and how often? requirements follow from that purpose. only then choose orbit, payload, subsystems and budget. this keeps attractive hardware from becoming a solution in search of a problem. a successful satellite begins with a question, not a shopping list of components. do we want to photograph coastal erosion, measure atmospheric temperature or relay emergency messages? the mission objective determines what must be measured, how often, from where and with what accuracy. from that objective follow requirements for payload, orbit, pointing, data handling, power, thermal control and communications. choosing an orbit before defining the mission can leave the instrument with the wrong coverage or resolution. good design starts by making vague ambitions measurable. from mission requirements to system design mission concepts can be assessed with a simple table containing payload resolution or coverage, revisit time, lifetime, mass, average power and communications demand. requirements must be measurable enough to test. average power = σ(load power × duty-cycle fraction) translate the mission statement into testable requirements. for example, “monitor a river basin” becomes a target ground coverage area, required spatial resolution, revisit interval, data latency and operating lifetime. each requirement then maps to subsystem capabilities and a method for verification by analysis, inspection or test. build initial mass, power, volume and data budgets with explicit margins. trade studies compare plausible alternatives rather than assuming one configuration is uniquely best. as the design matures, track interfaces and risks: a communications upgrade may increase power draw and radiator needs, while a higher-resolution instrument may demand better pointing. the mission succeeds as an integrated system, not as separate well-designed parts. worked examples question. a payload uses 20 w for 30% of the time; computer 5 w continuously; radio 10 w for 10%. find average demand. solution, step by step. average payload = 20 × 0.30 = 6 w; computer = 5 w; radio = 10 × 0.10 = 1 w. total average demand = 6 + 5 + 1 = 12 w, before margins. worked example 2 question. a hypothetical satellite needs 80 w payload power for 25% of each day, plus 20 w continuously. find average load. solution. average payload=80×0.25=20 w; add 20 w constant load → average total=40 w. peak when payload active=100 w. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a sensor uses 15 w for 40% of the time and a computer draws 4 w continuously. find average total. show worked answer 15 × 0.40 + 4 = 10 w. problem 2 of 5 a 60 kg satellite has a 10% system margin. budgeted mass? show answer and method 66 kg. problem 3 of 5 list two measurable mission requirements for an earth-observation satellite. show answer and method examples: ground resolution and revisit time, stated quantitatively. problem 4 of 5 why define orbit before finalising a communications link? show answer and method range and contact duration influence antenna, power and data-return requirements. problem 5 of 5 which is a requirement and which is a solution: “image every 12 h” versus “use a sun-synchronous orbit”? show answer and method “image every 12 h” is a requirement; orbit selection is a proposed solution. try it yourself write a one-page concept for a weather or environmental observation satellite: mission question, three requirements and a first subsystem budget. where this model stops average power is not peak power. a mission also needs link availability, energy storage and credible subsystem margins. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← the space environment ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/satellite-orbits.html":"how satellites orbit earth — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate how satellites orbit earth understand orbit as continuous free fall and calculate orbital speed and period. an orbit is a continuous fall if you throw a ball horizontally, gravity curves its path toward earth. imagine throwing it so fast—and from high enough—that earth curves away beneath it just as quickly. the object keeps falling around earth rather than hitting the ground. this is the central idea of orbit . an orbiting satellite is still pulled strongly by gravity. astronauts experience apparent weightlessness because they and their spacecraft are falling together, not because gravity has disappeared. a satellite stays in orbit not because gravity disappears but because gravity continually bends its motion around earth. if you could throw an object sideways fast enough from above the atmosphere, the surface would curve away as it fell. this picture helps explain why an orbit is sustained free fall rather than suspension in space. at low altitudes atmospheric drag eventually slows many satellites and reduces their orbital energy. higher orbits avoid most atmospheric drag but have different periods and visibility properties. the altitude suitable for imaging earth is not necessarily suitable for continuous communications. finding the speed for a circular orbit for a circular orbit we equate gravitational acceleration to the centripetal acceleration needed to turn the satellite continuously: gm/r² = v²/r → v = √(gm/r) earth's standard gravitational parameter μ = gm ≈ 398,600 km³/s² . distance r is measured from earth's centre , so r = earth radius + altitude. use earth radius ≈ 6371 km. orbital period: t = 2π √(r³/gm) higher circular orbits require lower orbital speed but have longer periods. this model ignores air drag, the moon and other complications. for an ideal circular orbit, gravity supplies the required inward acceleration: gmm/r²=mv²/r, giving v=sqrt(gm/r). notice that spacecraft mass cancels: at a given circular orbital radius around the same central body, ideal orbital speed does not depend on spacecraft mass. period follows from circumference divided by orbital speed: t=2πsqrt(r³/gm). the radius r is measured from earth's centre, not from the surface. these relations neglect perturbations such as earth’s nonspherical gravity field, atmospheric drag and other bodies. for elliptical orbits, speed varies around the orbit, and a more general formulation uses the semi-major axis for orbital period. change a satellite orbit ideal circular earth orbit altitude above earth (km) 400 not to scale circular orbit model around earth altitude 400 km animation speed is illustrative, not a real-time simulation. orbit geometry is not drawn to scale. at 400 km: speed ≈ 7.67 km/s; period ≈ 92.4 minutes. for a circular orbit around a spherical earth; earth's mean radius and gravitational parameter are approximations. worked examples problem: estimate the circular orbital speed of a satellite 400 km above earth. distance from earth’s centre: r = 6371 + 400 = 6771 km . v = √(μ/r) = √(398,600 / 6771) km/s. v ≈ 7.67 km/s . a satellite covers a huge distance even in a short time; it continuously changes the direction of its velocity. worked example 2 question. calculate orbital period for an ideal circular orbit of radius 7000 km around earth using μ=398600 km³/s². solution. t=2π√(r³/μ)≈2π√(343×10⁹/398600)≈5828 s≈97.1 min. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: at altitude 800 km, estimate circular orbital speed with μ = 398,600 km³/s² and earth radius 6371 km. show worked answer r = 6371 + 800 = 7171 km; v = √(398,600/7171) ≈ 7.46 km/s . this is slower than a 400 km circular orbit. problem 2 of 5 estimate circular speed at r=8000 km with μ=398600 km³/s². show answer and method √(398600/8000)≈7.06 km/s. problem 3 of 5 what force keeps an unpowered satellite in circular orbit? show answer and method gravity. problem 4 of 5 does a higher circular orbit have a higher or lower speed around the same body? show answer and method lower speed. problem 5 of 5 what is the shape of a two-body circular orbit? show answer and method a circle; it is a special case of an ellipse. sketch and explore draw earth and two circular orbits, one close and one farther away. label the direction of gravitational force at four points in each orbit. every force arrow should point toward earth’s centre. next question: if gravity pulls inward, why doesn't the satellite simply fall straight down? discuss the role of its sideways velocity. references mit ocw 16.851 — satellite engineering (orbital mechanics reference) mit ocw 16.851 — satellite engineering (orbital-mechanics notes) original explanations, illustrations, examples, and interactive demonstrations. the mit ocw course is credited as a subject reference; no mit text or figures are reproduced. check the mit ocw terms before reusing materials directly. ← back to courses free, independent learning materials. not affiliated with mit.","lessons/satellite-power.html":"powering a satellite — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate powering a satellite can sunlight run a spacecraft? build a simple solar-power budget and estimate how much energy a battery must store during eclipse. a spacecraft needs an energy budget a satellite may need electrical power for its radio, computer, instruments, heaters and pointing systems. solar panels generate electricity when they receive sunlight; batteries supply energy when the spacecraft is in earth's shadow. engineers therefore ask two different questions: is enough power available while illuminated? and is there enough stored energy during eclipse? the power budget is a starting point for answering both. a satellite needs energy to operate its computer, communications link, sensors, heaters and actuators. many earth-orbiting spacecraft use solar arrays during sunlight and rechargeable batteries during eclipse, when earth blocks direct sunlight. power planning therefore concerns both the instantaneous loads and the time pattern of generation and storage. imagine a camera that draws a large amount of power only when taking and transmitting images. a simple average-power figure might hide a short peak that the electrical system must support. design must consider peak demand, battery charge state, available sunlight, conversion efficiency and end-of-life degradation. from sunlight to electrical power near earth, sunlight just outside the atmosphere delivers roughly 1361 w/m² onto a surface facing the sun. real solar panels capture only a fraction. in this deliberately simple example, an orientation factor of 0.8 accounts for not facing the sun perfectly; another factor of 0.9 approximates electrical losses. solar output ≈ 1361 × panel area × efficiency × 0.8 × 0.9 (w) for a 30-minute eclipse at steady power demand p, the spacecraft must draw e = p × 0.5 hours from storage. if we plan to use at most 80% of a battery's nominal stored energy, its simplified capacity requirement is e / 0.8 in wh. important: real spacecraft design also considers degradation, temperatures, duty cycles, sunlight duration, battery charging efficiency, safety margins and mission orbit. these numbers are only an educational first-pass estimate. a basic energy budget integrates power over time: e=∫p(t)dt. a 20 w load operated for two hours needs 40 wh, before electrical losses. a battery that nominally stores 100 wh may not deliver all of it within allowable depth-of-discharge, temperature and ageing constraints. solar-array output depends on incident sunlight, effective area, cell efficiency, orientation, temperature and degradation. during an eclipse, stored energy must meet the loads; during sunlit portions, arrays must both serve current demands and replenish storage. a practical power budget uses duty cycles and margins rather than assuming every subsystem draws its maximum continuously. design a miniature power budget adjust panel area, solar-cell efficiency and satellite demand. compare generation with demand and see how much stored energy is needed for a half-hour eclipse. satellite power calculator panel area (m²) 1.0 panel efficiency (%) 25 demand (w) 200 solar panels (schematic) bars represent illuminated power generation and consumption, not battery charge. worked examples problem: a satellite has 1 m² of panels at 25% efficiency, with the orientation/loss factors above. its electrical load is 200 w. does its solar generation exceed its load? output ≈ 1361 × 1 × 0.25 × 0.8 × 0.9 = 245 w . surplus while illuminated ≈ 245 − 200 = 45 w . for a 30-minute eclipse, the load needs 200 × 0.5 = 100 wh of usable energy. at 80% allowable discharge, nominal battery capacity must be at least 125 wh . having a positive power surplus is necessary but not sufficient: the sunlit portion of the orbit must be long enough to recharge the energy used in eclipse. worked example 2 question. a spacecraft draws 150 w over a 40-minute eclipse. find usable energy and 75%-depth-of-discharge nominal capacity. solution. e=150×(40/60)=100 wh. nominal capacity≥100/0.75≈133.3 wh, before efficiency and margin. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: a satellite draws 120 w for a 30-minute eclipse. how many usable wh are needed? what nominal battery capacity does 80% allowable discharge suggest? show worked answer usable energy = 120 × 0.5 = 60 wh . nominal capacity ≥ 60/0.8 = 75 wh , before additional engineering margins and losses. problem 2 of 5 a 60 w load runs for 2 hours: energy? show answer and method 120 wh. problem 3 of 5 a panel of 2 m² at 20% efficiency receives 1000 w/m² normally. ideal electric output? show answer and method 1000×2×0.2=400 w. problem 4 of 5 if panels make 200 w and load is 250 w, instantaneous deficit? show answer and method 50 w. problem 5 of 5 why does a spacecraft need a battery even with solar arrays? show answer and method to operate during eclipse and handle loads that exceed instantaneous solar production. try it yourself on paper, list five things a small satellite might need to power. assign an estimated load and time-on percentage to each; compute its average load. later we can turn that table into a spreadsheet or a python tool. think further: would a spacecraft in deep space have the same solar power available as one near earth? references mit ocw 16.851: satellite engineering — lecture notes (power systems) mit ocw 16.851: satellite engineering course overview explanations, diagrams, examples and interactive demonstrations are original. mit opencourseware is credited as a topic reference; no mit figures or passages have been reproduced. mit ocw licensing and terms . ← back to courses free, independent learning material. not affiliated with mit.","lessons/satellite-subsystems.html":"anatomy of a satellite — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate anatomy of a satellite how can a satellite be understood as a set of interacting functions? a satellite is a connected system a satellite is a system, not just a box of instruments. it needs a mission payload plus platform functions such as power, structure, attitude control, thermal regulation, communications and onboard computing. interfaces link them. a satellite is not simply a camera placed in orbit. it is a coordinated collection of systems that must generate power, control temperature, know and adjust its orientation, communicate, process commands and survive launch. payload instruments provide the mission data; the supporting bus keeps them usable. requirements create dependencies. a more powerful transmitter may require a larger power system and additional heat rejection. a camera requiring long exposures may demand better pointing stability. thinking in terms of connected subsystems prevents a design change in one area from creating unexpected problems elsewhere. how the subsystems work together a useful first design technique is an allocation table: for each subsystem record its purpose, average and peak power, mass, data rate and major interface. budgets are constrained; increasing one subsystem’s demand affects others. total system mass = σ subsystem masses + margins a common spacecraft breakdown includes structures and mechanisms, electrical power, thermal control, command and data handling, communications, attitude determination and control, propulsion where required, and the payload. interfaces connect them through mass, volume, electrical power, data rates, heat flows and mechanical loads. a mass budget, power budget and data budget translate concepts into testable constraints. suppose an imaging instrument produces 2 gb per day. the communications link must transfer that data within available ground-station passes, while storage must accommodate delays. a systems-engineering approach records assumptions, margins and verification plans rather than treating each subsystem in isolation. worked examples question. a concept satellite has 4 kg payload, 3 kg structure, 2 kg power subsystem, 1 kg computer and 2 kg other equipment. find subtotal and 20% margin estimate. solution, step by step. subtotal = 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 + 2 = 12 kg. adding 20% of subtotal gives estimated design mass 12 × 1.20 = 14.4 kg. worked example 2 question. a spacecraft masses 12 kg structure, 5 kg payload, 3 kg battery and 2 kg avionics. find subtotal and margin at 20% of subtotal. solution. subtotal=12+5+3+2=22 kg. margin=0.20×22=4.4 kg. total including margin=26.4 kg. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. subsystem subtotal is 8 kg and concept-stage reserve is 25%. what total allocation do you plan? show worked answer 8 × 1.25 = 10 kg. problem 2 of 5 what subsystem maintains attitude? show answer and method attitude determination and control (adcs). problem 3 of 5 what subsystem sends telemetry to ground? show answer and method communications. problem 4 of 5 if five subsystems each need 2 w continuously, what is total ideal load? show answer and method 10 w. problem 5 of 5 why is a system mass budget maintained? show answer and method to track total mass, allocations and margins against launch constraints. try it yourself sketch a block diagram with payload in the centre and arrows showing power, data and mechanical interfaces to the bus. where this model stops a budget margin is not a substitute for actual design maturity, and one contingency figure rarely fits every subsystem. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← orbital transfers and manoeuvres attitude control and reaction wheels → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/solutions.html":"solutions and concentrations — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate solutions and concentrations why does adding more water make a drink less concentrated? what happens when a substance dissolves? concentration tells us how much solute is present in a chosen amount of solution. diluting with solvent changes the total volume but, if none is lost or reacts, conserves the amount of solute. this makes dilution calculations straightforward. dissolving a substance distributes its particles through a solvent, but not every mixture is a true solution. salt can dissolve in water to form a homogeneous ionic solution; sand remains a suspension or sediment instead. the solvent and solute interact at molecular scale, and the extent of dissolution can depend strongly on temperature. concentration describes how much solute is present relative to a quantity of solution or solvent. clear solutions can still be concentrated, and coloured solutions can be dilute. observing colour alone is not a reliable quantitative measurement. concentration and dilution molarity c is moles of solute per litre of solution. in an idealised dilution, moles before and after are equal: c₁v₁ = c₂v₂. mix thoroughly before assuming uniform concentration. c = n/v; c₁v₁ = c₂v₂ molarity c=n/v uses moles of solute per litre of final solution, not per litre of solvent added before mixing. if 0.10 mol of solute is present in 0.50 l of final solution, molarity is 0.20 mol/l. in a dilution where solute amount remains unchanged, c₁v₁=c₂v₂ can be used with consistent units. solubility imposes a limit on how much solute can remain dissolved under specified conditions. a saturated solution is at equilibrium with excess undissolved material, not necessarily one that contains an intuitively “large” amount. for quantitative work, temperature, units, chemical form and accurate final volumes matter. worked examples question. how much 2.0 mol/l stock solution is needed to prepare 100 ml of 0.50 mol/l solution? solution, step by step. c₁v₁ = c₂v₂. v₁ = (0.50 × 100)/2.0 = 25 ml. add solvent until final solution volume reaches 100 ml. worked example 2 question. dissolve 0.2 mol solute in enough water for 0.5 l final solution. find molarity. solution. c=n/v=0.2/0.5=0.4 mol/l. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a 1.0 mol/l solution is diluted from 50 ml to 200 ml. what is the final concentration? show worked answer c₂ = 1.0 × 50/200 = 0.25 mol/l. problem 2 of 5 concentration of 1 mol in 2 l solution? show answer and method 0.5 mol/l. problem 3 of 5 how many moles in 100 ml of 0.2 mol/l solution? show answer and method 0.1×0.2=0.02 mol. problem 4 of 5 dilute 50 ml of 1 m stock to 250 ml. final molarity? show answer and method c₂=(1×50)/250=0.2 m. problem 5 of 5 does dilution change the moles of solute when none is lost? show answer and method no. try it yourself use coloured water and a measuring cup to see visually how adding solvent changes appearance; do not infer exact concentration from colour alone. where this model stops use the final solution volume, not simply the amount of solvent added. not all volumes add exactly for real liquid mixtures. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← gases and gas laws energy in chemical reactions → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/space-environment.html":"the space environment — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate the space environment what happens to materials and electronics when they leave earth’s atmosphere? why space is a difficult environment spacecraft encounter vacuum, extreme radiation environments, atomic oxygen in low orbit, charging and large thermal swings. these hazards vary strongly with orbital altitude, inclination and mission duration. “space conditions” are not one uniform environment. space is not a perfectly uniform setting. near earth, a spacecraft may encounter residual atmosphere, ultraviolet radiation, energetic charged particles, atomic oxygen, debris and repeated transitions between sunlight and shadow. the severity of these effects depends on altitude, inclination and mission duration. vacuum itself changes practical engineering choices. some ordinary materials release volatile substances that could contaminate optical surfaces, while heat exchange through convection becomes negligible outside the spacecraft. radiation may cause transient electronic upsets or cumulative damage, even when the structure appears intact. vacuum, radiation and thermal cycling vacuum can allow volatile materials to outgas; thermal vacuum testing checks performance with reduced convective heat transfer. radiation causes cumulative dose and occasional particle-induced upsets. engineers use protection, redundancy and qualification tests. radiation dose = energy deposited / mass (gy = j/kg) engineering responses begin by characterising the actual mission environment rather than applying one generic “space rating.” radiation analysis considers total ionising dose and single-event effects; thermal analysis addresses external fluxes and internal power; contamination control examines outgassing and sensitive surfaces. debris and micrometeoroid hazards require risk assessment, while launch and end-of-life disposal introduce further constraints. design mitigations can include shielding, redundancy, watchdog circuits, thermal insulation, coatings and fault-tolerant software. each mitigation adds mass, complexity or testing effort, so reliability depends on a balanced system-level approach. worked examples question. a simplified radiation exposure deposits 0.020 j in 0.50 kg of material. compute absorbed dose. solution, step by step. dose d = e/m = 0.020/0.50 = 0.040 j/kg = 0.040 gy. this is absorbed dose, not a complete assessment of device or biological damage. worked example 2 question. a surface cycles between −40°c and +60°c. find temperature swing in k. solution. difference δt=60−(−40)=100°c = 100 k difference; do not add 273.15 to temperature differences. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. energy deposited is 0.006 j in 0.20 kg. what absorbed dose results? show worked answer d = 0.006/0.20 = 0.030 gy. problem 2 of 5 do vacuum environments eliminate radiative heating? show answer and method no; radiation works through vacuum. problem 3 of 5 give one source of radiation in space. show answer and method solar energetic particles, cosmic rays or trapped radiation belts. problem 4 of 5 why test materials for outgassing? show answer and method released volatiles can contaminate sensitive optics or components. problem 5 of 5 what is atomic oxygen erosion especially relevant to? show answer and method exposed materials in low earth orbit. try it yourself list likely failures for an adhesive, camera detector and circuit board in vacuum and radiation. suggest a test for each. where this model stops absorbed dose alone cannot predict component reliability; particle type, dose rate, shielding and sensitive design features matter. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← deployable mechanisms and origami in space designing a simple satellite mission → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/space-materials.html":"materials for rockets and spacecraft — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate materials for rockets and spacecraft why do spacecraft use aluminium, composites and special coatings in different places? how materials survive launch and orbit material selection in space is a compromise among mass, stiffness, strength, thermal expansion, conductivity, radiation behaviour, vacuum compatibility and cost. a lightweight material may still be unsuitable if it warps with temperature. materials for spacecraft must be strong and sufficiently stiff, but mass is costly to launch. a thin aluminium structure may be adequate mechanically yet expand with temperature, conduct heat into sensitive components or interact unfavourably with coatings and adhesives. choosing a material means balancing many properties at once. space also introduces environmental concerns unusual in an ordinary workshop: vacuum-related outgassing, ultraviolet exposure, energetic particles, atomic oxygen in some low orbits, and thermal cycling. a material that performs well at room temperature in air is not automatically suitable for a particular orbit. strength, mass and environmental compatibility specific stiffness e/ρ is a useful first comparison when low mass matters. expansion strain is approximately αδt for a freely expanding uniform material, while constraints can turn expansion into stress. thermal expansion δl ≈ αlδt strength, elastic modulus, density, thermal expansion and conductivity influence structural and thermal performance. for a long member of length l, uniform temperature change δt gives approximate free thermal expansion δl=αlδt. if movement is restrained, thermal stresses may arise instead of free expansion. material selection must consider manufacturing route, joint design, coatings, fatigue, fracture and contamination sensitivity. composites can offer high specific stiffness but introduce anisotropy and joining challenges; metals often simplify fabrication and conductive paths. material handbooks and mission-specific standards provide screened data, but qualification still depends on processing history and the exact application. worked examples question. an aluminium component 0.50 m long has α = 23 × 10⁻⁶ /k. estimate length change for a 40 k temperature rise. solution, step by step. δl = 23×10⁻⁶ × 0.50 × 40 = 0.00046 m = 0.46 mm. even small movements can affect precision instruments. worked example 2 question. two candidate panels have equal volume 0.01 m³. material a density 2700, b density 1600 kg/m³. find mass saving using b. solution. ma=27 kg; mb=16 kg; mass saving=11 kg, assuming identical volume. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. for the same α, how much does a 1 m member grow with a 20 k temperature rise? show worked answer δl = 23×10⁻⁶ × 1 × 20 = 0.00046 m = 0.46 mm. problem 2 of 5 a 0.002 m³ part of density 3000 kg/m³ has mass? show answer and method 6 kg. problem 3 of 5 why is specific stiffness useful for spacecraft structures? show answer and method it compares stiffness to mass, which is critical for launch loads and system mass. problem 4 of 5 name a space environment concern for material selection. show answer and method radiation, thermal cycling, vacuum outgassing, or atomic oxygen in low orbit. problem 5 of 5 must the lightest material always be chosen? show answer and method no; strength, fatigue, thermal, cost and manufacturability requirements matter. try it yourself make a comparison table for a beam, a thermal radiator and an optical support. why could each favour a different material? where this model stops material properties vary with temperature, processing and direction; composites may behave anisotropically. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← launch loads and vibrations different types of satellite orbits → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/space-thermal.html":"thermal management in space — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / space & aerospace space & aerospace · high school → undergraduate thermal management in space why can a spacecraft overheat in sunlight yet become too cold in shadow? managing temperature without air in vacuum there is almost no surrounding fluid to carry heat away by convection. spacecraft exchange energy mainly by radiation and internally by conduction. surfaces absorb sunlight and emit infrared energy; temperature follows their balance. on earth, a fan can carry heat away through air. a spacecraft in vacuum has essentially no external convective cooling, so it must conduct heat internally and radiate energy from suitable surfaces. solar illumination, planetary infrared radiation, reflected sunlight and electronic equipment all contribute to its thermal balance. the same spacecraft may alternate between intense sunlight and earth's shadow. some components overheat in one condition while others become too cold in another. thermal design therefore includes radiators, insulation, heaters and conductive paths instead of relying on one universal cooling device. radiation and spacecraft heat balance for an idealised surface, thermal radiation scales with absolute temperature to the fourth power. surface emissivity, area and temperature set radiated power. solar absorptivity describes how much incoming sunlight is absorbed. q̇radiated = εσat⁴ (to cold surroundings) a surface radiates approximately q̇=εσa(t⁴−t_surroundings⁴) for an idealised view of an effective radiation sink, with absolute temperatures in kelvin. the fourth-power dependence means temperature strongly affects radiative power. a surface's solar absorptivity and infrared emissivity are distinct properties; a coating can absorb little sunlight while radiating heat effectively. a real spacecraft has multiple surfaces exchanging radiation with each other, earth and deep space, so view factors matter. transient thermal analysis tracks heat capacity through eclipse transitions. a single formula is a useful first estimate but cannot replace detailed geometry, orbit conditions and worst-case hot/cold analyses. worked examples question. a surface of area 1 m², emissivity 0.8 and temperature 300 k radiates to deep space. take σ = 5.67×10⁻⁸ w/(m²·k⁴). estimate power. solution, step by step. 300⁴ = 8.1×10⁹ k⁴. q̇ = 0.8 × 5.67×10⁻⁸ × 1 × 8.1×10⁹ ≈ 367 w, ignoring absorbed radiation. worked example 2 question. a spacecraft absorbs 80 w and dissipates 120 w internally. at steady state, how much heat must it radiate if there are no other energy flows? solution. thermal balance: emitted power=80+120=200 w. real temperatures depend on emissivity, areas, orientation and view factors. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. at the same emissivity and area, by what factor does radiation increase if temperature doubles in kelvin? show worked answer t⁴ scaling gives 2⁴ = 16 times greater emitted power. problem 2 of 5 can a hot spacecraft lose heat by ordinary air convection in vacuum? show answer and method no; thermal radiation and conduction through contact paths dominate. problem 3 of 5 what are units of absolute temperature for radiative calculations? show answer and method kelvin. problem 4 of 5 if an ideal blackbody temperature doubles, emitted power per area scales by what factor? show answer and method 16, from stefan–boltzmann t⁴. problem 5 of 5 what does multilayer insulation mainly suppress? show answer and method radiative heat exchange between surfaces. try it yourself make a paper thermal balance with incoming sunlight, internal electronics heat and outgoing radiation for a small spacecraft. where this model stops a spacecraft does not radiate to an empty zero-temperature sink everywhere; view factors and planetary infrared matter. references satellite engineering (16.851) — mit opencourseware, fall 2003. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← satellite communications and antennas deployable mechanisms and origami in space → ← back to space & aerospace lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/springs-oscillations.html":"springs and oscillations — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate springs and oscillations what connects a guitar string, a suspension spring and a vibrating machine? why a displaced spring moves back an oscillation repeatedly moves around an equilibrium position. springs provide a restoring force that grows with displacement. with little damping, energy transfers between motion and stored elastic energy. a spring pulled away from its equilibrium position tries to return there. yet a mass attached to it usually does not stop exactly at equilibrium: inertia carries it past, and the spring then pulls the other way. that exchange between elastic potential energy and kinetic energy produces oscillation. oscillations appear in suspension systems, precision instruments, building structures and spacecraft mechanisms. whether the motion dies away, keeps a constant amplitude or grows depends on damping and on whether external forces are supplying energy. restoring forces and oscillations hooke’s law models an ideal spring: restoring force is opposite displacement. for a mass on a spring, the natural period depends on mass and stiffness. more mass means slower oscillation; a stiffer spring means faster oscillation. f = −kx; t = 2π√(m/k) for an ideal linear spring, restoring force is f=−kx. with mass m and no damping, newton’s law gives m x″+kx=0, whose angular frequency is ω=sqrt(k/m). the period is t=2πsqrt(m/k). doubling the mass increases the period by sqrt(2), while quadrupling stiffness halves it. real oscillators lose energy through friction and fluid drag, so damping reduces their motion. when a repeated driving force acts near a natural frequency, resonance can produce large responses if damping is low. the simple spring equation assumes small, approximately linear deformation and a single degree of freedom; complex assemblies may have many vibration modes. worked examples question. a 0.50 kg block is attached to a spring of stiffness 200 n/m. estimate the undamped period. solution, step by step. m/k = 0.50/200 = 0.0025 s². its square root is 0.05 s. period t = 2π × 0.05 ≈ 0.314 s. worked example 2 question. a spring with k=200 n/m stretches by 0.05 m. calculate force and elastic energy. solution. force magnitude =kx=200×0.05=10 n. stored energy=½kx²=0.5×200×0.0025=0.25 j. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a spring with k = 100 n/m is stretched 0.03 m. find the magnitude of restoring force. show worked answer |f| = kx = 100 × 0.03 = 3 n. problem 2 of 5 a 50 n/m spring extends 0.1 m. restoring force magnitude? show answer and method 5 n. problem 3 of 5 how does mass affect the period of a spring oscillator t=2π√(m/k)? show answer and method more mass increases the period proportional to √m. problem 4 of 5 what is the phase-space velocity at a turning point of ideal simple harmonic motion? show answer and method zero at the extreme displacement. problem 5 of 5 for ideal shm, where is speed greatest? show answer and method at equilibrium. try it yourself hang a modest mass from a suitable spring, displace slightly and time ten cycles. divide by ten to estimate the period. where this model stops the ideal model assumes small displacement, a linear spring and negligible damping. secure suspended masses safely. references classical mechanics (8.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2016. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← torque and rotational motion properties of fluids → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/states-of-matter.html":"states of matter and phase changes — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / chemistry chemistry · high school → undergraduate states of matter and phase changes why does water boil at a lower temperature on a mountain? why matter changes state a phase change occurs when conditions favour a different arrangement of matter. solid, liquid and gas states differ in molecular freedom and interactions. boiling begins when the liquid’s vapour pressure matches the surrounding pressure. ice, liquid water and water vapour contain the same h₂o molecules. what changes is how those molecules are arranged and how much freedom they have to move relative to one another. in a solid, particles are usually constrained around relatively fixed positions; in a liquid they can rearrange; in a gas they move through much larger available space. temperature and pressure together affect which phase is stable. this is why boiling temperature changes at altitude and why substances can be liquefied through pressure and cooling. a phase change can absorb or release energy even while the temperature remains approximately constant during the transition. energy, particles and phase changes heat supplied during a phase change may change the fraction of each phase rather than temperature. for a simplified constant-pressure change, required latent heat equals mass times specific latent heat. q = ml (latent heat at a specified phase transition) heating a substance raises internal energy, but the energy may go into changing temperature or into changing phase. during melting or boiling of a pure substance at constant pressure, added energy can supply latent heat rather than produce an immediate temperature rise. for mass m, an idealised latent-heat calculation uses q=ml. a phase diagram shows where different phases are stable as pressure and temperature vary. the triple point is where three phases coexist in equilibrium, and the critical point marks the end of the distinct liquid–vapour coexistence boundary. real materials, mixtures and nonequilibrium processes can complicate this simple picture. worked examples question. how much energy is needed to melt 0.20 kg of ice at 0°c? take latent heat of fusion as 334 kj/kg. solution, step by step. q = 0.20 × 334 = 66.8 kj. the ice changes phase at approximately constant temperature, assuming standard pressure. worked example 2 question. estimate energy to melt 0.2 kg ice at 0°c if latent heat of fusion is 334 kj/kg. solution. q=ml=0.2×334=66.8 kj; temperature stays ~0°c during melting at fixed pressure. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. estimate energy to melt 0.05 kg of ice at 0°c using the same value. show worked answer q = 0.05 × 334 = 16.7 kj. problem 2 of 5 energy to melt 0.1 kg ice using 334 kj/kg? show answer and method 33.4 kj. problem 3 of 5 at constant pressure, does a pure substance normally change temperature while melting? show answer and method not while the phase change proceeds in equilibrium. problem 4 of 5 what is sublimation? show answer and method direct conversion from solid to gas. problem 5 of 5 why can evaporation cool skin? show answer and method higher-energy molecules escape, removing energy from remaining liquid and skin. try it yourself watch ice melt at room temperature and record temperature over time using a suitable thermometer. distinguish warming from melting. where this model stops do not use a constant latent heat far outside its stated conditions; actual boiling point changes with external pressure. references principles of chemical science (5.111sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2014. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← molecular shapes and polarity chemical reactions and balancing equations → ← back to chemistry lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/statistical-distributions.html":"statistical distributions — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate statistical distributions why do repeated measurements cluster around some values and not others? why measurements form patterns a distribution describes which values are common, rare or impossible. its centre and spread answer different questions: an average tells us where data cluster, while the standard deviation tells us how widely they scatter. a collection of measurements is rarely described well by a single average. two production lines may produce the same mean dimension but have very different spreads, which changes how many parts meet tolerance. a statistical distribution describes the overall pattern—centre, spread, asymmetry and unusual values. begin with a histogram of real measurements and ask what the shape suggests. is there one central cluster? is the distribution skewed? are there distinct groups that might represent different machines or batches? a beautiful bell curve should not be imposed simply because it is familiar. spread, probability and distributions the mean is the total divided by the number of observations. for sample standard deviation, square each deviation from the mean, sum them, divide by n − 1, then take the square root. a histogram helps reveal skew or multiple clusters. sample mean = σx/n; sample s = √(σ(x−x̄)²/(n−1)) the mean measures a numerical centre, while the standard deviation measures typical spread around the mean. for a normal distribution, about 68% of values lie within one standard deviation of the mean and about 95% within two. those percentages do not automatically apply to skewed, multimodal or otherwise non-normal data. a probability distribution also describes what outcomes might be expected before sampling. distinguish the population's underlying variability from uncertainty in an estimated mean, which usually decreases as independent sample size grows. for process capability, a stable process and a justified distributional model are prerequisites; a capability index alone cannot prove that a process is controlled. worked examples question. a set of measurements is 9, 10 and 11 mm. find its mean and sample standard deviation. solution, step by step. mean = (9 + 10 + 11)/3 = 10 mm. squared deviations are 1, 0 and 1. sample variance = 2/(3−1) = 1 mm²; s = 1 mm. worked example 2 question. for data 2,4,4,6,9, calculate mean and median. solution. sum=25, n=5, so mean=5. ordered middle value is 4, so median=4. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. find the mean of measurements 4, 5, 6, 5. show worked answer sum = 20; divide by 4 to get a mean of 5. problem 2 of 5 find mean of 3,6,9. show answer and method 6. problem 3 of 5 find population variance of 1,3. show answer and method mean 2; variance=((1−2)²+(3−2)²)/2=1. problem 4 of 5 for a binomial(n=10,p=0.2), find expected successes. show answer and method np=2. problem 5 of 5 what is the area under a probability density curve? show answer and method 1 over its full domain. try it yourself measure the length of the same object with a ruler repeatedly and graph your readings. can you distinguish measurement scatter from resolution? where this model stops a familiar bell curve is not a universal assumption. inspect the observed distribution before fitting a normal model. references introduction to probability and statistics (18.05) — mit opencourseware, spring 2022. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← probability numerical methods and approximation → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/thermodynamics.html":"introduction to thermodynamics — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate introduction to thermodynamics how is thermal energy different from temperature and work? why heat and work are linked temperature indicates thermal state, while internal energy is energy stored microscopically within a system. heat and work are transfers across a system boundary, not materials contained inside an object. the first law accounts for these transfers. thermodynamics tracks energy and its transformations at the system level. a kettle receives electrical work, warms water and loses some heat to the surroundings. we can account for these transfers without calculating the microscopic movement of every water molecule. the first law expresses conservation of energy; the second law explains why energy conversions have direction and limits. heat naturally flows from hotter to colder bodies without external work, and a real heat engine cannot turn all incoming heat into useful work. these are constraints on what machines can achieve, not merely rules for solving textbook equations. energy balance and thermodynamic systems for a closed system with negligible motion and elevation effects, internal-energy change equals heat added minus work done by the system. if a gas is heated in a fixed-volume rigid container, boundary work is approximately zero. δu = q − w (w = work done by the system) for a closed system, a common energy balance is δu=q−w, with q defined as heat entering and w as work done by the system. sign conventions vary between texts, so always state yours. enthalpy is particularly useful for flowing systems, where fluid carries internal energy and flow work through an inlet or outlet. imagine a well-insulated rigid container heated electrically. because the boundary does not move, boundary work is zero; with negligible other energy exchanges, the increase in internal energy equals the electrical energy supplied. temperature change depends on the substance and its heat capacity. the ideal gas law and simple constant-heat-capacity relations are useful approximations, but they have operating limits. worked examples question. a closed system receives 500 j of heat and does 120 j of work on its surroundings. find δu. solution, step by step. using the stated sign convention, δu = 500 − 120 = +380 j. the system’s internal energy rises. worked example 2 question. a gas absorbs 500 j of heat and does 180 j of work on its surroundings. find δu using δu=q−w. solution. δu=500−180=320 j; positive means internal energy increases. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a system loses 200 j as heat while 50 j of work is done on it. find δu. show worked answer q = −200 j and w = −50 j (work on system). δu = −200 − (−50) = −150 j. problem 2 of 5 for q=100 j into a system and w=0, δu? show answer and method 100 j. problem 3 of 5 an isolated system has what heat and work interactions? show answer and method no heat transfer and no work transfer across its boundary. problem 4 of 5 why does temperature alone not determine heat transferred? show answer and method heat depends on the process and amount/property of matter, not temperature alone. problem 5 of 5 in an ideal gas at fixed n and t, if volume doubles, pressure becomes…? show answer and method half, by pv=nrt. try it yourself draw a box representing a kettle of water. trace where energy enters and leaves, including the heater, steam and air. where this model stops state the system boundary and sign convention first. open systems also transport energy with flowing mass. references thermodynamics & kinetics (5.60) — mit opencourseware, spring 2008. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← heat transfer waves, sound and light → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/torque-rotation.html":"torque and rotational motion — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate torque and rotational motion why does pushing a door near its hinge barely turn it? why forces cause turning torque measures a force’s turning tendency about an axis. the same force produces more torque when applied farther from the pivot and perpendicular to the lever arm. rotational acceleration also depends on how mass is distributed around the axis. a door opens more easily when you push far from its hinges because the same force has a greater turning effect. torque depends on the applied force and its perpendicular distance from the pivot. this helps explain why tools have handles and why supports on a cantilever beam experience significant moments. translation and rotation can occur together. a freely moving rigid body can accelerate its centre of mass while also spinning. choosing the point about which you take moments can simplify calculations, but it does not change what actually happens. moment arms and rotational motion torque magnitude is rf sin θ. for rotation about a fixed axis, net torque equals moment of inertia times angular acceleration, analogous to f = ma. a larger moment of inertia makes angular acceleration harder to achieve. τ = rf sin θ; στ = iα the torque magnitude about a chosen axis is τ=rfsinθ, where θ is the angle between the position vector and force. in a simple fixed-axis model, net torque is iα, where i is rotational inertia about that axis and α is angular acceleration. mass alone is not enough to predict rotational response; how mass is distributed relative to the axis also matters. two equal and opposite parallel forces applied at different points can produce zero net force but a nonzero couple moment. this is why checking only σf=0 is not sufficient for static equilibrium. for a wrench 0.25 m long with a 40 n perpendicular push, the torque magnitude is 10 n·m; redirecting the force along the handle makes the torque about the bolt nearly zero. worked examples question. a perpendicular 15 n push acts 0.80 m from a hinge. what is the torque? solution, step by step. θ = 90°, so sin θ = 1. torque = 0.80 × 15 = 12 n·m. a force applied at 0.40 m would provide only 6 n·m. worked example 2 question. a 20 n perpendicular force is applied 0.3 m from a hinge. find torque magnitude. solution. τ=rf sin90°=0.3×20=6 n·m. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. what torque does a 10 n perpendicular force produce at 0.25 m from a pivot? show worked answer τ = 0.25 × 10 = 2.5 n·m. problem 2 of 5 find torque for 10 n at 0.5 m perpendicular lever arm. show answer and method 5 n·m. problem 3 of 5 what is torque if the force acts directly through the pivot? show answer and method zero. problem 4 of 5 what is the si unit of angular acceleration? show answer and method rad/s². problem 5 of 5 a rigid body is in rotational equilibrium when…? show answer and method sum of external torques about any fixed point is zero. try it yourself use a ruler pivoted at one end and apply equal gentle forces at two distances. compare how easily it turns. where this model stops torque is calculated about a chosen axis; a different pivot can change individual torque values. references classical mechanics (8.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2016. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← circular motion and gravitation springs and oscillations → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/trigonometry-applications.html":"trigonometry and its applications — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate trigonometry and its applications can you estimate the height of a building without going inside? finding distances without measuring them directly sine, cosine and tangent connect angles with length ratios in right triangles. their value is practical: if one length and an angle are available, you can infer an inaccessible dimension. trigonometry is useful whenever a distance or height is difficult to measure directly. imagine standing a known horizontal distance from a building and measuring the angle to its roof. a right triangle appears even though there is no drawn triangle on the building. geometry turns the angle into a height estimate. sine and cosine also describe how rotating quantities project onto horizontal and vertical axes. that makes them useful for ramps, cranes, robotics and cyclical motion. the familiar soh–cah–toa rules are a starting point, not the whole subject; the deeper idea is that the ratios depend on angle rather than the triangle's overall size. triangles, angles and sine/cosine for an elevation angle θ, tangent is opposite divided by adjacent. if you stand horizontal distance d from a wall and measure the angle to the top, the height above your eye level is d tan θ. add the height of the observer’s eye for total building height. tan θ = opposite/adjacent; height ≈ d tan θ + eye height for a right triangle, tanθ=opposite/adjacent. if you measure a roof at 30° above the horizontal from 20 m away, the estimated height above your eye level is 20tan30° ≈11.55 m. you must still add your eye height to obtain the total building height and check that the ground is approximately level. real measurements introduce uncertainty. if the angle shifts by even a small amount, the estimated height changes—especially for steep sight lines. try calculating the height using θ=29° and θ=31° to see the sensitivity. for non-right triangles, sine and cosine rules extend the idea. for periodic processes, sine and cosine connect angular motion with repeating time signals. explore the triangle change the angle or distance. the diagram and calculated height change together. 30° 10 m 5.77 m elevation angle 30° horizontal distance 10 m height above eye level 5.77 m relationship h = d × tan(θ) assumes level ground and a right triangle. add your eye height to estimate an object's total height. worked examples question. a student stands 20 m from a tower. the angle to its top is 35° and eye level is 1.6 m. estimate the tower height. solution, step by step. height above the eyes = 20 tan 35° ≈ 14.00 m. total ≈ 14.00 + 1.60 = 15.60 m. worked example 2 question. a ladder 5 m long leans at 60° above horizontal. how high does it reach? solution. vertical height = 5 sin60° = (5√3)/2 ≈ 4.33 m. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. with horizontal distance 12 m, elevation angle 45° and eye height 1.5 m, find the height. show worked answer 12 tan 45° + 1.5 = 12 + 1.5 = 13.5 m. problem 2 of 5 find sin30° and cos60°. show answer and method both equal 0.5. problem 3 of 5 a right triangle has opposite=3 and adjacent=4. find tanθ. show answer and method 3/4 = 0.75. problem 4 of 5 a 10 m slope makes 30° with horizontal. what is its horizontal projection? show answer and method 10 cos30° ≈ 8.66 m. problem 5 of 5 convert 180° to radians. show answer and method π radians. try it yourself use a paper protractor, string and weighted washer to make a simple clinometer. try measuring a tree with a known horizontal distance. where this model stops this assumes the ground is level and the target is vertically above its base. record uncertainty in distance and angle. references single variable calculus (18.01sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2010. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← dot products and cross products optimisation using calculus → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/vector-components.html":"vector components and resultants — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · high school → undergraduate vector components and resultants why is it easier to calculate horizontal and vertical forces separately? breaking one vector into two directions a sloping force is one arrow but can be represented as perpendicular components. the components are not extra forces: they are two contributions which together equal the original force. the choice of axes is part of the modelling. a slanted force often affects a machine in more than one direction. rather than calculating separately with awkward angles each time, resolve the force into perpendicular components. the two components together reproduce the original vector; neither component is an additional force being created. suppose you pull a suitcase with a handle tilted upward. part of your pull moves the suitcase forward and part lifts on it, changing the normal force and therefore friction. resolving that one pull into horizontal and vertical directions reveals both effects. the choice of coordinate axes is up to you, so choose axes that simplify the physical situation. projections and resultant vectors if a force f makes angle θ with the positive horizontal axis, its horizontal component is f cos θ, and its vertical component is f sin θ. resultants come from adding corresponding components, then reconstructing the size and direction. fₓ = f cos θ; fᵧ = f sin θ; |r| = √(rₓ² + rᵧ²) for a vector of magnitude f at angle θ measured from the positive horizontal axis, the components are fx=f cosθ and fy=f sinθ. they carry signs: a force pointing left has a negative x-component. conversely, the magnitude of perpendicular components is sqrt(fx²+fy²), and direction is found from the component signs and an appropriate inverse tangent. a resultant is found by adding x-components together and y-components together before calculating the final magnitude. this is much safer than adding vector magnitudes. in three dimensions we add a z-component, and the same bookkeeping works for loads on fasteners, motion in space, and the combined influence of several thrusters. the coordinate system changes the numbers assigned to the components, but not the physical vector. worked examples question. resolve a 10 n pull acting at 30° above horizontal. solution, step by step. horizontal: 10 cos 30° ≈ 8.66 n. vertical: 10 sin 30° = 5.00 n. check: √(8.66² + 5²) ≈ 10 n. worked example 2 question. resolve a 20 n force at 60° above the horizontal. solution. fx = 20 cos60° = 10 n. fy = 20 sin60° ≈ 17.32 n. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a force has components 3 n east and 4 n north. find its magnitude and angle above east. show worked answer magnitude = √(3² + 4²) = 5 n. angle = tan⁻¹(4/3) ≈ 53.1°. problem 2 of 5 resolve 10 n horizontally right into x and y components. show answer and method fx = 10 n, fy = 0. problem 3 of 5 combine forces (4, 3) and (−1, 2) n. show answer and method resultant = (3, 5) n. problem 4 of 5 a force has components −6 n in x and 8 n in y. find magnitude. show answer and method √(36+64) = 10 n. problem 5 of 5 why must the angle reference axis be stated? show answer and method because sine/cosine component assignments depend on how the angle is defined. try it yourself sketch several arrows head to tail on graph paper. add their x- and y-components and compare with the final drawn arrow. where this model stops sine and cosine swap roles if you measure the angle from a different axis. first mark the angle on a sketch. references multivariable calculus (18.02sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2010. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← limits and continuity dot products and cross products → ← back to mathematics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review","lessons/vectors.html":"vectors: magnitude and direction — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / mathematics mathematics · lesson 01 · high school → undergraduate vectors: magnitude and direction how do you describe something that has both a size and a direction? start with a walk, then discover how vectors combine. what is a vector? imagine telling someone to walk five metres . that is a distance, but it does not say where to go. now say: walk five metres east. you have given both a size and a direction . that is the idea of a vector. speed is a scalar: it has a size. velocity is a vector: it has a size and a direction. the force exerted by a rocket engine is also a vector. 5 m east start end the arrow length shows magnitude; the arrowhead shows direction. original diagram. a number such as 20 can tell us a temperature or a mass, but it cannot by itself describe a journey. if two people each walk five metres, one east and one west, their distances match while their displacements differ. that is why a vector has both magnitude and direction. an arrow is a convenient representation, but the vector is the physical or mathematical quantity—not the ink on the page. consider directions while giving instructions to a robot. “move three metres” is incomplete; “move three metres north” is usable. later, when the robot must avoid an obstacle, we can combine several short movements into one resultant displacement. think about the surprising consequence: it is possible to travel a considerable distance and have a zero resultant displacement when you return to your starting point. breaking a vector into components on a flat surface, we can describe motion using two perpendicular directions: horizontal ( x ) and vertical ( y ). a vector of 3 m east and 4 m north has components (3, 4). magnitude = √(x² + y²) its magnitude is √(3² + 4²) = 5 m . this follows from a right triangle; no complicated new rule is required. 3 m (east) 4 m (north) 5 m components of the same displacement, not three separate journeys. vector addition describes the cumulative effect of directed quantities. draw the second arrow starting at the first arrow's head; the arrow from the first tail to the final head represents the sum. moving the arrows without rotating or scaling them does not change the answer, so the same calculation works whether you construct a triangle or a parallelogram. unit vectors help when a picture is no longer enough. in a plane, write a displacement as x units in the horizontal direction plus y units vertically. for a displacement (3, 4), the magnitude is sqrt(3²+4²)=5, while its direction relative to the positive x-axis is arctan(4/3). this is also why two forces of 10 n do not necessarily combine to 20 n: their directions matter. the same methods apply to velocities, accelerations and electric fields. explore: adding vectors two movements can be combined. draw the second arrow from the tip of the first; the arrow from the original start to the final end is the resultant . try different directions a: horizontal 3 a: vertical 1 b: horizontal -1 b: vertical 2 a b a+b brown = a; light brown = b; dashed dark arrow = a + b. a + b = (2, 3); magnitude = √13 ≈ 3.61 units. worked examples problem: a walker travels 3 km east, then 4 km north. how far are they from their starting point? in what direction? take east as x and north as y: (3, 4) km . magnitude: √(3² + 4²) = 5 km . direction: tan θ = 4/3, so θ ≈ 53.1° north of east . the walker has walked 7 km along the path but is only 5 km from the start. distance travelled and displacement are different. worked example 2 question. a robot moves 6 m east and 8 m north. find its displacement magnitude and direction. solution. the components are (6, 8) m. magnitude = √(6²+8²) = 10 m. direction = arctan(8/6) ≈ 53.1° north of east. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn: a drone flies 2 m east and then 2 m south. what is its displacement? show worked answer components (2, −2) m. magnitude = √(2² + (−2)²) = 2√2 ≈ 2.83 m ; direction is 45° south of east . problem 2 of 5 what is the magnitude of the vector (5, 12) m? show answer and method √(25+144) = 13 m. problem 3 of 5 add the vectors (2, −1) and (−3, 4). show answer and method add components: (−1, 3). problem 4 of 5 what displacement results from 7 m east and 7 m west? show answer and method zero: the two displacements cancel. problem 5 of 5 a vector has magnitude 10 n and points due west. what are its east-positive x and north-positive y components? show answer and method (−10, 0) n. try it in real life draw a map on graph paper. let one square represent one metre. trace two journeys using arrows. now draw a single arrow connecting your first and last position. you have just constructed vector addition. next idea: what happens when forces point in opposite directions? that leads directly to newton's laws. references mit opencourseware: 18.02sc, session 1 — vectors . original explanations, diagrams, interactive demonstration and practice problems prepared for this website. mit ocw is credited as a topic reference; no mit diagrams or text reproduced. mit ocw licence information . ← back to courses independent, free learning material. no affiliation with mit.","lessons/waves-sound-light.html":"waves, sound and light — the learning workshop skip to main content courses / physics physics · high school → undergraduate waves, sound and light what travels when a ripple crosses a pond? a disturbance that moves through space a wave carries a disturbance and energy through space or a medium, often without transporting the medium itself over long distances. frequency counts cycles per second; wavelength measures the distance between repeating points. their product gives propagation speed. a wave transports a disturbance and energy from place to place without requiring the medium itself to travel the whole distance. a pulse on a rope makes individual points move locally, while the pulse travels along the rope. sound needs a material medium; light can propagate through vacuum. the same broad ideas—amplitude, wavelength, frequency and superposition—appear in acoustics, optics, vibration measurement and radio communications. but different waves have different physical mechanisms, so a sound wave in air should not be imagined as exactly the same kind of oscillation as an electromagnetic wave. wavelength, frequency and wave speed for a periodic travelling wave, speed = frequency × wavelength. sound in air is a mechanical pressure wave; light is an electromagnetic wave and can travel through vacuum. waves reflect, interfere and sometimes form standing patterns. v = fλ wave speed is v=fλ, where f is frequency and λ wavelength in the medium. if a sound wave travels at roughly 343 m/s and has frequency 686 hz, its wavelength is about 0.5 m. changes in medium can change wave speed and wavelength while frequency often stays set by the source at a boundary. when waves overlap, their displacements combine; this produces interference. reflection can create standing waves, which explain resonances in pipes and strings. for light, refraction occurs when propagation speed changes between media; the ray direction often changes too. a numerical formula is useful only after identifying which wave and medium are involved. worked examples question. a sound wave travels at 340 m/s and has frequency 170 hz. find wavelength. solution, step by step. λ = v/f = 340/170 = 2 m. doubling frequency at the same wave speed would halve wavelength. worked example 2 question. a wave travels at 340 m/s with frequency 170 hz. find wavelength. solution. v=fλ, so λ=340/170=2 m. practice set five questions, from quick checks to applied problems. reveal the answer after trying each one. problem 1 of 5 your turn. a ripple has wavelength 0.25 m and frequency 4 hz. find propagation speed. show worked answer v = 4 × 0.25 = 1 m/s. problem 2 of 5 frequency 5 hz means how many cycles per second? show answer and method 5. problem 3 of 5 a wave has λ=0.5 m and f=20 hz. speed? show answer and method 10 m/s. problem 4 of 5 what changes during reflection from an ideal stationary mirror: light frequency or direction? show answer and method direction changes; frequency remains unchanged. problem 5 of 5 can a sound wave propagate through vacuum? show answer and method no; sound requires a material medium. try it yourself move one end of a rope gently to send a pulse. observe whether a marker on the rope travels with the pulse. where this model stops wave speed depends on the medium and conditions; do not use the speed of light for sound waves. references physics iii: vibrations and waves (8.03sc) — mit opencourseware, fall 2016. topic reference and route for further learning. this lesson’s text and examples were written for the learning workshop, not copied from mit course materials. if material is adapted in future, it must follow the licence and any third-party restrictions. see mit ocw terms and cc by-nc-sa 4.0 . ← introduction to thermodynamics electricity and magnetism → ← back to physics lessons free independent educational notes · not affiliated with mit · draft for review"};
(function(){
  var root=document.documentElement;
  function updateButton(){document.querySelectorAll('[data-theme-toggle]').forEach(function(btn){var dark=root.dataset.theme==='dark';btn.textContent=dark?'Light mode':'Dark mode';btn.setAttribute('aria-label',dark?'Switch to light mode':'Switch to dark mode');btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(dark));});}
  function init(){
    updateButton();
    document.querySelectorAll('[data-theme-toggle]').forEach(function(btn){btn.addEventListener('click',function(){var next=root.dataset.theme==='dark'?'light':'dark';root.dataset.theme=next;try{localStorage.setItem('learning-workshop-theme',next);}catch(e){}updateButton();});});
    var search=document.getElementById('course-search');
    var dirs=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('details.course-subject'));
    function filter(){
      if(!search)return;
      var q=search.value.trim().toLocaleLowerCase().split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean);
      var seen=0;
      dirs.forEach(function(d){
        var hits=0;
        d.querySelectorAll('.directory-lesson-list li').forEach(function(li){
          var link=li.querySelector('a[href]');
          var key=link&&link.getAttribute('href');
          var hay=[li.textContent,d.dataset.subject,window.LW_SEARCH_INDEX[key]||''].join(' ').toLocaleLowerCase();
          var visible=!q.length||q.every(function(token){return hay.includes(token);});
          li.hidden=!visible;
          li.classList.toggle('search-excluded',!visible);
          if(visible)hits++;
        });
        d.hidden=q.length>0 && hits===0;
        d.classList.toggle('search-excluded',q.length>0&&hits===0);
        seen+=hits;
        if(q.length && hits)d.open=true;
      });
      var msg=document.getElementById('course-no-results');
      if(msg){msg.style.display=q.length&&!seen?'block':'none';msg.textContent=seen?'':('No lessons match “'+search.value.trim()+'”. Try a different topic.');}
      var count=document.getElementById('course-search-count');
      if(count)count.textContent=q.length?(seen+' matching '+(seen===1?'lesson':'lessons')):'';
    }
    if(search)search.addEventListener('input',filter);
    document.querySelectorAll('[data-expand-all]').forEach(function(b){b.addEventListener('click',function(){dirs.filter(function(d){return !d.hidden;}).forEach(function(d){d.open=true;});});});
    document.querySelectorAll('[data-collapse-all]').forEach(function(b){b.addEventListener('click',function(){dirs.forEach(function(d){d.open=false;});});});
  }
  if(document.readyState==='loading')document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded',init);else init();
})();
